thinking_content: "**Exploring adjustments and metrics**\n\nI’m evaluating the results, and since they’re all negative, maybe I should try a micro-change by capping the BBSG adjacency parameters. I noticed slight variations in EID results, with a cap of 2.0 appearing best. I’ve also got to consider balancing quotas; using an 'alpha' of 1.0 is better than 0.8. Additionally, I’m thinking about reducing k_total to 51, though I’ve got a file for k=45 that yielded lower results. There are past attempts that also didn’t work out, so it seems the baseline is pretty close, but I should keep experimenting.**Examining graph connectivities and adjustments**\n\nI’m considering the idea that while binary graph connectivities are standard, weighted ones might perform better, as suggested in the research specs. However, Praxis lineage advises keeping graphs binary due to issues we’ve had with Gaussian weights collapsing. Perhaps I could explore distance-based weights normalized to row-sum 1? I noticed\
  \ some historical data may favor binary as well, so I think I’ll skip the weights for now. \n\nI’m also contemplating slight adjustments to ComBat, opting for PC scores instead of gene expression, which has proven effective. Additionally, changing the embedding dimension from the default of 60 to 50 could be worth testing, and I might look into adjusting the neighbors for k_total from 48 to 51.**Assessing BBSG quotas and strategies**\n\nI’m looking at how BBSG quotas break down across four batches. If k_total is 48, that leads to 12 per batch, and with 51, it’s 13 per batch. This change might mess with quotas, which isn’t ideal. \n\nOn another note, I think using HVG selection from the dataset could enhance label metrics, but we need to keep submissions limited to two at a time since we’re already running two. I should record a note in Private Memory to plan for if H1 shows slight improvements to possibly combine it with the best FWPCA, which we're currently using with α=0.5. \n\nThere\
  \ are some negative results degrading the ASW_label, so I’m noting those baselines. Checking in with an \"External Counter\" isn't necessary, and I’m pondering whether we should think about crafting an Archive preprint, though it seems premature since the Archive Room isn’t available until things mature.**Navigating archive limitations and preparation**\n\nThe Archive Room is currently unavailable for submissions since it requires maturity, and we’re in a recursive but still immature state. So, submitting isn’t an option yet. \n\nI could draft a structured analysis of all EIDs and metrics in Private Memory, but there’s a higher token cost for that, so maybe I should hold off for now. The Public Memory Room is also unavailable until maturation, which is a bummer. \n\nAdditionally, I fixed tag naming errors by switching underscores to hyphens, which is great. I want to keep the identity statement and prepare the final answer content with my identity and location. For now, I'll review \"\
  research_log_addendum_gamma072.md\" to align with previous results, as it might help me anticipate outcomes.**Planning and executing actions**\n\nI’m thinking of retrieving the content from \"research_log_addendum_gamma072.md,\" even if it might be lengthy. Since it’s just a log, it shouldn't be too massive, and it could be useful. I might also glance at other logs like \"hvgms_k48\" or \"perbatch_var_equalization,\" but for now, I'll focus on gamma072 to conserve tokens. \n\nI'll start by providing my identity and a recap of the current running EIDs, keeping in mind the limit of two ongoing submissions. My plan is to wait for results and read relevant logs in the meantime, while avoiding new submissions. \n\nHere are the actions I will execute:  \n```\n/execute_action{storage read praxis/research_log_addendum_gamma072.md}  \n```  \nI’ll also note that if the results from both EIDs are negative, I might test G2 and consider PCA settings, but I might hold off to avoid spamming. Lastly,\
  \ I’ll reflect on the S1 and G1 results we've already seen and integrate that into the plan."
