tick: 318
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Private Memory Room.\nRecap: Praxis default remains strongest; micro-ablations have been neutral/negative. I’ll try a single, minimal change directly in graph space: downweight PCs whose variance is highly batch-explained when computing neighbor distances, while keeping BRBG residualization and binary BBSG intact.\n\nSubmitting GW1 (graph PC weights by (1−R2)^0.5).\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation GW1: BRBG + BBSG with graph PC weights w_j=(1−R2_j)^0.5\"\ntags: \"ablation, graph-weighting, pc-weights, residualization, bbsg, embed-then-correct\"\nabstract: \"Keep Praxis defaults but, in the graph path, weight residualized PCs by w_j=(1−R2_j)^0.5 so PCs strongly explained by batch contribute less to distances. Hypothesis: improves ASW_label/NMI without harming mixing thanks to BBSG quotas. Binary edges and cosine metric preserved.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\
    \n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from praxis.graph_bbsg import _build_bbsg\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float =\
    \ 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W  # Zhat\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Praxis defaults\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      alpha_weight = 0.5\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # Embedding: FWPCA α=0.5 → PCA60 → ComBat\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh\n      Xd = Xd.astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n \
    \     var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      w_feat = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_weight).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * w_feat\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Zemb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Zemb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=min(15, k_total), metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization → per-PC graph weights → BBSG (binary)\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total,\
    \ 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      # New: weight graph PCs by (1 - R2)^0.5 to attenuate batch-dominated axes\n      w_pc = np.sqrt(np.maximum(1.0 - R2, 1e-6)).astype(np.float32)  # shape (n_pcs_graph,)\n      Zgraph = Zcorr * w_pc  # broadcast across rows\n\n      Cg, Dg = _build_bbsg(Zgraph.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric, alpha=1.0, cap=2.0)\n      if Cg is not None and Dg is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 12}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Submitted GW1: Graph PC weighting by (1−R2)^0.5 applied after BRBG residualization, before BBSG. Rationale: reduce influence of batch-dominated axes in neighbor distances while keeping quotas and binary edges. If neutral/negative, I will fully pause tweaks until maturity and focus on finalizing\
    \ the analysis for Archive.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering next steps**\n\nI need to figure out what to do next. The agent has done a lot of ablations and found that the default is robust. We’re currently at tick 318, which is past the age limit of 300 ticks, but maturity isn’t reached until 50 ticks. There’s time to innovate. Since public memory is restricted until maturity, they can prepare documents in private memory while exploring other research avenues. The station status is healthy, but the top score is stuck at around 0.7363. Maybe we could try \"Dual pipeline\" or improve \"graph-only\" methods?**Exploring various strategies**\n\nI’m noticing many lines mention negative results for local scaling, DMN union, and heavy stitching. One idea is using an \"EtC variant with robust PCA and a whitening parameter\" to prevent over-equalization, but prior attempts showed that PC whitening in embedding can be detrimental. Another option could be \"graph building with a union of batch quotas,\" though DMN union previously\
  \ yielded negative results. There's also \"BM-PCA per-batch mean centering,\" but it wasn’t promising last time. I might consider \"adaptive residualization\" with cross-validated parameters, keeping in mind the rules about cell types and allowing batch labels.**Addressing overfitting concerns**\n\nI’m contemplating that cross-validation within the dataset might lead to overfitting during evaluation, though it’s acceptable without labels. There's a caution around heavy hyperparameterization affecting generalizability. A label-free heuristic for lambda could be beneficial, and looking back at \"BRBG Addendum: Label-free Auto-parameterization (γ and k)\" in capsule #6 might help. They haven't reviewed that yet. Implementing those auto parameters could yield stable results, even if it doesn’t necessarily improve scores, but it's still a worthwhile contribution.**Exploring embedding methods**\n\nI’m considering \"EtC embedding with feature-weighted PCA,\" where they used α=0.5, but earlier\
  \ attempts with 1.0 and 0.3 were negative. I might explore \"soft-threshold variable weighting\" to avoid overemphasizing low-variance genes, using a formula like weight = (var + τ)^(-α/2). However, previous negative results with PCA whitening might not change much. I could also try \"angular-cosine pre-normalization\" or look into crafting a \"two-stage neighbor graph\" with computed quotas, but the DMN technique previously yielded negative outcomes. There are lots of angles to explore!**Exploring graph improvements**\n\nI'm thinking about trying \"connectivity weighting\" by per-batch inbound counts while maintaining binary edges. A new baseline could involve a \"Graph-based Correction Only\" pipeline using a balanced graph without residualization, though that might degrade performance. Another option is a \"PCR-based shrinkage schedule,\" calculating gamma_j through cross-prediction with K-folds. I also want to consider \"Contrastive embedding training,\" but GPU limitations could cause\
  \ timeouts. I plan to summarize negative results in a draft, but with Archive restricted, I might have to wait. \n\nNext steps include refining a new approach by investigating low R2 PCs to boost structure while preserving quotas. I'll explore graph weighting strategies by adjusting PCA distance metrics. Finally, I need to craft a minimal ablation report while implementing as a function to ensure clarity and reproducibility, all while maintaining effective communication about my actions.**Structuring content for ablation**\n\nI’m planning to structure the content similarly to the baseline. I need to ensure the tags don’t include underscores. The title will be \"Ablation GW1: BRBG+BBSG with graph PC weights w_j=(1−R2_j)^0.5\" and the tags will be \"ablation, graph-weighting, pc-weights, residualization, bbsg, embed-then-correct.\"\n\nThe abstract will focus on weighing PCs in graph space by the square root of (1 - R2) to reduce batch-dominated effects. I’ll lay out the steps for the graph\
  \ pathway, including computing Zg and adjusting weights accordingly. Finally, I'll address an optional retuning of the metric to 'cosine' and update private memory with the results."
