tick: 348
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Private Memory Room.\nRecap: ANOVA-F down-weighting harmed labels; up-weighting preserved labels and modestly improved kBET but didn’t beat the default (0.7237). Hypothesis: pure ANOVA-F up-weighting (without variance term) might better concentrate batch signal pre-ComBat. I’ll test two pure ANOVA variants (linear and log up-weight), clamped to avoid extremes. Graph path remains Praxis default.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"EtC(ANOVA-F up-weight β=0.5, pure, clamp[0.5,2.0]) + BRBG + BBSG (binary)\"\ntags: \"embed-then-correct, anova-weighting, batch-aware, brbg, bbsg, ablation\"\nabstract: \"Pure ANOVA-F up-weighting in the EtC embedding (no variance term): w = 1 + β·F_norm, β=0.5, clamped to [0.5,2.0]. Goal: concentrate batch signal pre-ComBat without over-amplifying extreme genes. Graph path unchanged (BRBG on PCA50, γ_max=0.74, λ_r=0.9; BBSG k=48, binary).\"\ncontent: |\n\
    \  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from praxis.graph_bbsg import _build_bbsg\n  from praxis.embed_weighting import anova_F_per_gene, minmax_norm\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches\
    \ == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      beta_anova = 0.5\n      emb_n_neighbors = 15\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches_cat = adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').cat.codes.to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\
    \n      # Embedding: pure ANOVA-F up-weight → PCA60 → ComBat\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      F_g = anova_F_per_gene(Xd, batches_cat)\n      F_norm = minmax_norm(F_g)\n      w = 1.0 + beta_anova * F_norm\n      w = np.clip(w, 0.5, 2.0).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * w  # broadcast column-wise\n\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Z = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization → BBSG (binary)\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n\
    \      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      Cg, Dg = _build_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric, alpha=1.0, cap=2.0)\n      if Cg is not None and Dg is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"EtC(ANOVA-F log-up-weight β=0.5, c=5, pure, clamp[0.5,2.0]) + BRBG + BBSG (binary)\"\ntags: \"embed-then-correct, anova-weighting, logarithmic, batch-aware, brbg, bbsg, ablation\"\nabstract: \"Pure ANOVA-F log-up-weighting in the EtC embedding: w = 1 + β·log1p(c·F_norm), β=0.5, c=5, clamped to [0.5,2.0]. Goal: temper extreme F while still concentrating batch signal for\
    \ ComBat. Graph path unchanged (BRBG + BBSG).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from praxis.graph_bbsg import _build_bbsg\n  from praxis.embed_weighting import anova_F_per_gene, minmax_norm\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n  \
    \    for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      beta_anova = 0.5\n      c_scale = 5.0\n      emb_n_neighbors = 15\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches_cat = adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').cat.codes.to_numpy()\n\
    \      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # Embedding: pure ANOVA-F log-up-weight → PCA60 → ComBat\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      F_g = anova_F_per_gene(Xd, batches_cat)\n      F_norm = minmax_norm(F_g)\n      w = 1.0 + beta_anova * np.log1p(c_scale * F_norm).astype(np.float32)\n      w = np.clip(w, 0.5, 2.0).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * w\n\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Z = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization → BBSG (binary)\n  \
    \    Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      Cg, Dg = _build_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric, alpha=1.0, cap=2.0)\n      if Cg is not None and Dg is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```\n\nIf both are neutral/negative, I’ll close this line with a concise negative-results note and pivot to label-free auto-parameterization of γ and k (capsule #6) or cross-organ validation."
thinking_content: "**Exploring weighting transforms**\n\nI’m considering testing other weighting transforms like \"pure ANOVA up-weight without variance,\" \"log scaling,\" and \"clamp weights.\" We can run one of these tests, but I need to remember the two concurrent submissions limit—we currently have none running and can submit two more.\n\nThe main difference this time is the multiplied variance weighting with ANOVA-F. Maybe this interplay reduces effectiveness. I could also explore other variations like clamping weights or applying them in principal component space. Additionally, summarizing results in a Public Memory note might be helpful.**Planning next steps**\n\nI need to respond to the last output related to statuses and actions. Since the user hasn't posed a question, I should propose next actions. The normal approach would be to produce commands for submitting new experiments and recording them. \n\nI plan to implement pure ANOVA upweight with \\( β = 0.5 \\) and \\( β = 0.8\
  \ \\), while clamping to [0.5, 2.0] to avoid extremes. \n\nAnother option is to upweight with the log1p transform. However, to isolate one factor per Codex, I should compare \"pure ANOVA upweight\" against \"pure variance weight\" while keeping everything else identical. That should help clarify the effects.**Implementing weighting criteria**\n\nI need to ensure we mark the acceptance criteria while keeping the analysis style intact, but the primary goal is to improve the Research Score. Previous tests and baseline improvements are nearing a plateau, but implementing pure ANOVA upweight might yield slight improvements.\n\nI'll create a storage module to import the weighting. We’ve already written embed_weighting.py, and I'll use compute_weights with anova_dir set to 'up' and alpha_var at 0.0 to drop variance. \n\nFor submissions, U3 will be pure ANOVA up-weight with \\( β = 0.5 \\) clamped to [0.5, 2.0], and U4 will involve an ANOVA up-weight with a log transform. I’ll import the necessary\
  \ module for these computations and implement the code accordingly.**Preparing submissions and updates**\n\nI need to keep track of the two concurrent limit, and since we currently have none running, we can submit two now. I also want to create a Private Memory update.\n\nLooking at the metrics for experiment 582, I see ASW_batch at 0.657 compared to the default 0.659, and kBET improved to 0.792 from 0.788, which is a positive change! However, the composite is slightly lower, so maybe I should adjust to increase kBET while maintaining ASW_batch.\n\nWe’ll need to craft our commands now: the current location is the Private Memory Room, and we’ll need to go to research to submit. Importing embed_weighting, we’ll compute Xd, F, and normalized Fn, then compute w, clamp, and proceed to PCA and ComBat similarly, keeping everything straightforward and avoiding unnecessary formatting.**Executing submissions**\n\nI’m ready to do two submissions:\n\n1. \"EtC(ANOVA-F up-weight \\( β=0.5 \\), pure)\
  \ + BRBG + BBSG (binary)\"\n2. \"EtC(ANOVA-F log-up-weight \\( β=0.5 \\), \\( c=5 \\)) + BRBG + BBSG (binary)\"\n\nTo keep things efficient, I’ll import the embed_weighting functions and work on producing the YAML files. I’ll clamp normalized weights to [0.5, 2.0] to avoid drastic fluctuations per gene.\n\nFor the coding structure, I’ll maintain the standard pipeline and ensure the calculations are consistent while adjusting weights as needed. I’ll check the YAML formatting for any special characters as well.\n\nFinally, I’ll log these actions in Private Memory and note future actions based on results."
