tick: 350
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: ANOVA-F weighting variants (down-, up-, pure/log) did not surpass the Praxis default (0.7237). Up-weighting preserved labels but remained neutral (≤0.7221). I will (1) read Praxis I’s auto-parameter capsule, (2) test a label-free auto-parameterization of γ and k_total in BRBG+BBSG with a single, principled experiment, and (3) add an archive addendum summarizing the ANOVA-F results.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{read 6}`\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BRBG+BBSG with label-free auto-parameters: γ curvature from R² and dataset-level k_total from cross-batch mixing\"\ntags: \"brbg, bbsg, auto-parameterization, residualization, balanced-graph, embed-then-correct\"\nabstract: \"Test a label-free auto-parameterization for BRBG+BBSG. We set per-PC shrinkage γ_j = min(γ_max, λ · R²_j^p) with p derived from the R² distribution (maps\
    \ median R² to 0.5, clipped to [0.7,1.3]) and choose k_total by estimating cross-batch mixing at k_small=12 in Zg (increase to 54 if mixing <0.45; decrease to 44 if >0.55; else 48). Hypothesis: adaptive γ curvature and data-driven k improve balance without hand-tuning.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from praxis.graph_bbsg import _build_bbsg\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n\
    \      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1]), B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def _auto_k_total(Zg: np.ndarray, batch_codes: np.ndarray, k_small: int = 12, metric: str = 'cosine',\n                    low: float = 0.45, high: float = 0.55):\n      # Estimate dataset-level cross-batch mixing at small neighborhood\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_small + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n      nn.fit(Zg)\n      dists, idx = nn.kneighbors(Zg, return_distance=True)\n      idx = idx[:, 1:]  # drop self\n      neigh_batches = batch_codes[idx]\n\
    \      same = (neigh_batches == batch_codes[:, None])\n      cross_frac = 1.0 - same.mean(axis=1)\n      m = float(np.mean(cross_frac))\n      if m < low:\n          return 54\n      elif m > high:\n          return 44\n      return 48\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config (Praxis defaults; auto-params for γ and k_total)\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.9\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      emb_n_neighbors = 15\n      random_state = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches_cat = adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').cat.codes.to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\
    \n      # Embedding: FWPCA α=0.5 (variance weights only) → PCA60 → ComBat on PC scores\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh\n      Xd = Xd.astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * 0.5).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization (auto-curvature) → BBSG (auto k_total)\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=random_state)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n   \
    \   Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n      # Auto curvature: map median R2 to 0.5 via exponent p; clip p to [0.7, 1.3]\n      med = float(np.median(R2))\n      med_clip = min(max(med, 0.05), 0.95)\n      p = np.log(0.5) / np.log(med_clip + 1e-8)\n      p = float(np.clip(p, 0.7, 1.3))\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * np.power(R2, p, dtype=np.float32))\n\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      # Auto k_total from small-scale cross-batch mixing\n      k_total = _auto_k_total(Zg, batches_cat, k_small=12, metric=metric)\n\n      Cg, Dg = _build_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric, alpha=1.0, cap=2.0)\n      if Cg is not None and Dg is not None:\n          adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n          adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto\
    \ archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum (Ticks 344–350): Batch-aware ANOVA-F weighting in EtC (negative/neutral results under current evaluator)\n\n  We tested ANOVA-F-based feature weighting in the embedding, both as down-weighting and up-weighting, and as pure/log-scaled variants. Graph path remained BRBG (PCA50, λ=0.9, γ_max=0.74) + BBSG (k=48, binary). Results vs our default (EID 567 = 0.7237):\n  - Down-weighting: (w ∝ var^{-α/2}(1+F)^{-β})\n    • β=0.3 → 0.7158 (EID 577): kBET ↑0.835, but ASW_label 0.204; Cell_cycle ↓0.161.\n    • β=0.5 → 0.7218 (EID 578): kBET 0.944, but ASW_label 0.138 (over-correction).\n  - Up-weighting (with variance): (w ∝ var^{-α/2}(1+β·F_norm))\n    • β=0.3 → 0.7198 (EID 581); β=0.5 → 0.7221 (EID 582). Labels preserved (ASW_label 0.248) but no gain over default.\n  - Pure ANOVA up-weight (no variance):\n    • Linear, β=0.5, clamp[0.5,2.0] → 0.7186 (EID 585).\n    • Log, β=0.5, c=5, clamp[0.5,2.0] → 0.7177 (EID 586).\n\
    \n  Interpretation: ComBat performs best when batch signal is neither suppressed nor excessively emphasized beyond the default variance weighting; our attempts did not improve the composite metric, despite some kBET increases. Under this evaluator, the EtC–BRBG–BBSG default remains a robust ridge. We conclude that batch-aware ANOVA-F weighting, as instantiated here, is neutral/negative and not recommended.\n\n  Next: We are testing a label-free auto-parameterization for BRBG+BBSG (adaptive γ curvature from the R² distribution and data-driven k_total from small-scale cross-batch mixing). We will report results in a subsequent update.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating experimental results**


  I need to consider the next steps based on the latest results for experiments 585 and 586, which are around 0.718-0.717, compared to the best baseline of 0.7237. Unfortunately, attempts at up-weighting through ANOVA haven''t exceeded these baselines. The guidelines suggest avoiding micro-tuning, so I should pivot to new strategies like label-free auto-parameterization and cross-organ validation for better generalization. It seems documenting our negative results will be essential too.**Planning ANOVA-F addendum**


  I’m considering publishing a short addendum in the Archive to summarize the ANOVA-F attempts, detailing each variant along with their EIDs and deltas. The conclusion would highlight that, under the current evaluator, F weighting seems to have plateaued and doesn’t exceed the default. I could either publish this as a message update to archive capsule #21 or post a reply. It’s allowed to do both, but I want to keep it standard. The addendum should include key suggestions like pivoting to label-free auto-parameterization.**Implementing heuristics for parameters**


  Capsule #6 offers some useful heuristics for gamma and k. I think we can implement a heuristic where we auto-select gamma_max and lambda based on the R2 distribution, and adaptively choose k_total based on neighbor distances and batch mixing. However, I also need to be cautious with the number of experiments. We should aim to conduct minimal tests to validate a principled idea with a clear hypothesis, especially for label-free auto-parameterization. For self-tuned k_total, we could derive k per cell from local density, but I need to consider the evaluation code''s requirements for graph connectivities.**Modifying BBSG and reading guidelines**


  I think the BBSG builder might expect a fixed k_total, so to implement variable degrees, I''ll need to modify the interface of _build_bbsg. To minimize changes, I can use dataset-level heuristics to choose k_total, ensuring the median same-batch neighbor probability aligns with the target mixing, possibly computing local across-batch variance. Additionally, I can determine a gamma schedule with lam set at 0.9 times the global R2 median. The first step is to read Capsule #6, “Label-free Auto-parameterization (γ and k),” and guide my algorithm from there. I''ll proceed to access it now.**Implementing autoparam algorithm and next actions**


  I’m planning to implement a minimal autoparam algorithm with two experiments. First, for gamma, I’ll set lam at 0.9 by default, but adapt gamma_max from R2 metrics. I can compute gamma based on local density, although past tests suggest adaptive modifications may not yield improvements. For k_total, I’ll select it based on sample size. After that, I need to summarize negative ANOVA-F experiments and potentially update the Archive with a brief addendum, explaining outcomes and linking to relevant EIDs. I’ll read Private capsule #6 as the next step!**Updating the archive and implementing autoparam**


  I’m planning to add a concise reply to Archive capsule 21, summarizing the ANOVA-F experiments with results indicating they don''t surpass the default. I''ll execute this as an addendum rather than an update for less disruption. Next, I''ll read Private capsule #6 and submit the autoparam experiment. The autoparam will compute gamma based on adaptive metrics and k_total according to neighbor mixing. I’ll ensure proper imports for the code and keep an eye on token usage during implementation. Let''s wrap this up!'
