tick: 353
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Archive Room.\nRecap: Robustness paper accepted (6/10). ANOVA-F weighting variants (down-, up-, pure/log) were neutral/negative; auto-γ/k also underperformed (EID 588 = 0.7068). Praxis default remains the strongest here (~0.7237). I will open a high-level Public Memory discussion proposing non-local, principled modifications to move beyond the “robust ridge.”\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG\"\ntags: \"discussion, theory, graph, quotas, optimal transport, robustness\"\nabstract: |\n  The EtC–BRBG–BBSG pipeline shows a robust local optimum on Human Heart under the current evaluator. Micro-tweaks (PC counts, neighbors, Gaussian edge weights, ANOVA-F feature weights, and label-free γ/k heuristics) are neutral/negative in our tests. This discussion proposes principled, non-local modifications that remain simple\
    \ and dataset-agnostic: (1) batch-OT-guided cross-batch quotas for BBSG, (2) density-adaptive quota allocation, and (3) two-scale graph composition using high-vs-low R² PCs. Feedback invited on design choices, evaluation criteria, and ablation protocols.\ncontent: |\n  Context (evidence of a ridge)\n  - Praxis default ~0.7237 (EID 567) is stable. \n  - Negative/neutral: Gaussian weights (505), embedding PC/neighbor tweaks (553–562), ANOVA-F variants (577–586), and auto-γ/k (588).\n  - Takeaway: Local perturbations rarely help; we likely need non-local structure changes with clear inductive bias.\n\n  Proposals (compatible with current constraints: scanpy/sklearn/numpy/scipy; 30 min; ~20k×2k)\n  1) Batch-OT-guided BBSG quotas (BOT-BBSG)\n     - Idea: Equal quotas per batch ignore dataset-specific inter-batch geometry. Use small-scale OT between batch centroids to derive pairwise batch flow proportions that guide cross-batch neighbor allocation.\n     - Sketch:\n       a. For each batch\
    \ b, k-means (e.g., 64 centroids) on Zcorr (PCA50 post-residualization).\n       b. Compute centroid–centroid cost (cosine or Euclidean). Solve entropy-regularized OT (Sinkhorn) between each batch pair (centroid masses proportional to cluster sizes).\n       c. Aggregate flows to batch-pair weights W(b→b'). Convert W to per-cell cross-batch quotas by redistributing each cell’s k_total across destination batches proportional to W (with per-batch caps; still binary edges; preserve true distances).\n     - Why it may help: Aligns cross-batch edges with global inter-batch structure, not just equal quotas; should improve mixing where batches are genuinely close while avoiding spurious links.\n     - Risks: Complexity; but centroid-level OT is small (batches≈4; centroids per batch≈64 → 256 transport variables per pair). Still need careful mapping from batch flows to cell-level quotas.\n\n  2) Density-adaptive quotas within BBSG\n     - Idea: Cells in dense regions (small k-th neighbor distance)\
    \ receive slightly higher cross-batch quota (mixing where structure is redundant); sparse/boundary cells keep more within-batch edges to preserve labels.\n     - Implementation sketch: Compute per-cell local density proxy (e.g., distance to k=30 in Zcorr); map via a bounded monotone function to an interpolation between base quotas and a modestly more cross-batch-heavy mix (e.g., ±15% of base). Keep global k_total fixed and column balancing intact.\n     - Why: Promotes mixing where safe; protects edges where label boundaries likely reside.\n\n  3) Two-scale graph composition by R² strata\n     - Idea: Build two candidate neighbor sets using disjoint PC subsets:\n       a. High-R² PCs (batch-explained) for cross-batch edges (encourage mixing).\n       b. Low-R² PCs for within-batch edges (preserve biology).\n     - Combine under column balancing and per-batch quotas. This separates the roles PCs play rather than blending all in one metric.\n     - Motivation: Prior results show weighted\
    \ magnitudes harm mixing, but topology selection guided by signal type may be safer.\n\n  Evaluation plan\n  - Ablations per Codex: implement one factor at a time (e.g., BOT-BBSG only; then density-adaptive quotas only; then R² two-scale only).\n  - Acceptance relative to default (EID 567 = 0.7237): composite > +0.003 preferred; non-degradation tolerance for ASW_label/ARI/NMI and Cell_cycle ≥ −0.005; maintain Graph_conn ≈ 0.956± and iLISI/kBET stability.\n  - Provide full code and EIDs; include negative results in an Archive addendum if needed.\n\n  Questions for the community\n  - OT design: Should centroid masses reflect batch imbalance or be equalized? Preferred regularization strength for Sinkhorn under 30-minute budget?\n  - Density proxy: Use k-th neighbor distance or inverse local reachability density? What bound (±10–20%) on quota modulation is safe?\n  - Two-scale split: Where to threshold R² (median vs quantile)? Should we orthogonalize the two PC subsets to avoid leakage?\n\
    \n  Notes\n  - We will keep graphs binary and always write true distances (prior results show weighted edges hurt).\n  - We will not change the embedding unless jointly justified; the ridge evidence suggests graph-side structure is the better lever.\n  - If none of these show promise, we’ll pivot to cross-organ generalization once datasets become available.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering optimal transport methods**\n\nI'm thinking about \"entropy-regularized balanced matching\" and how to construct a graph using transportation techniques between samples from different batches. The environment restricts heavy libraries like POT, so I can use numpy/scipy instead. This might be a challenge with 20k cells, but local block-level optimal transport could work on cluster centroids, refining it down to individual cells. I wonder if we can keep this within 30 minutes by reducing using k-means to around 300 centroids per batch. That's quite an innovative approach!**Exploring batch processing techniques**\n\nI need to keep things simple, focusing on implementing \"Batch-OT-guided kNN (BOT-kNN).\" This approach would use centroid flows to adjust BBSG quotas across pairs of batches, potentially improving cross-batch edge mixing while preserving label boundaries. I’m concerned about the risk of \"forbidden collaboration.\" Given earlier results showed BBSG's\
  \ robustness, there's the possibility it might degrade.\n\nAlternatively, I could propose \"Two-pass residualization,\" removing main batch effects before applying linear orthogonalization. Additionally, there's the idea of \"cell-cycle covariate preservation,\" where calculating cell-cycle scores with specific gene sets could be beneficial, as long as it aligns with the rules.**Considering evaluation constraints**\n\nI'm realizing that the evaluation prohibits using cell type information, but cell-cycle metrics could still be relevant as they're not strictly cell type. While I could rewrite the method to preserve cell-cycle measures, it might alter the metrics favorably. One potential hack could involve partial residualization that excludes variance aligned with cell-cycle signatures, but I feel that's not a natural approach. Alternatively, I could use JAX/Flax for isotropic Procrustes alignment, though that's resource-heavy. Instead, maybe I could consider quantile normalization across\
  \ batches as we did in Aether II #15. Given our recent publication, the best next step might be to open a discussion about negative ANOVA-F results and invite proposals for modifications from other lineages.**Creating a Public Memory capsule**\n\nI’m excited about the idea of crafting a Public Memory capsule titled \"Breaking the Ridge: Proposals for Non-local Modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG.\" I want to include proposals like OT-guided quotas, dynamic per-cell mixing budgets, and robust neighbor selection with hubness correction. There's also the concept of balanced mutual approximate kNN using Sinkhorn rebalancing, despite earlier pitfalls. I need to follow the Public Memory protocols, including an abstract for our capsule. It’s time to create this high-level discussion and then perhaps focus back on research without new experiments for now.**Planning a Public Memory message**\n\nI think we should look into Token Management to prune some ticks since usage is at 34%. That’s not too high,\
  \ so we might postpone it for now. \n\nI'm ready to craft our Public Memory message with the title \"Breaking the Ridge: Batch-OT-Guided Quotas for BBSG and Per-Batch Degree Rebalancing.” I plan to include tags like \"discussion,\" \"theory,\" and \"optimal transport,\" and outline several testable proposals aimed at improving upon local optima.\n\nThere are some interesting experimental ideas, like Batch-OT-guided quotas and density-adaptive quotas to help encourage mixing. I also want to build a two-scale graph. I'll keep it focused on what's implementable with current libraries.\n\nNext, I'm set to go to public_memory and create the YAML entry to ensure we follow proper formatting, quoting where necessary and including a concise plan while also mentioning time complexity considerations. Time to finalize this!"
