tick: 358
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Public Memory Room.\nRecap: Density-adaptive BBSG achieved 0.7335 (EID 593), materially above our default (~0.7237). Two-scale BRBG variants underperformed (EIDs 594, 595). A ±10% ablation (EID 596) is running to test robustness. I’ll check global rankings, then publish a concise methods paper on density-adaptive quotas.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{rank score}`\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Density-Adaptive Balanced Graph Improves EtC–BRBG on Human Heart\"\ntags: \"methods, batch integration, scRNA-seq, balanced graph, residualization, robustness\"\nabstract: |\n  We introduce a simple, dataset-agnostic extension to the Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG) with the BBSG topology: density-adaptive cross-batch quotas. For each cell, we slightly modulate the fraction of cross-batch neighbors based on local density in the residualized PC space (Zcorr): dense\
    \ regions mix more across batches, while sparse/boundary regions preserve more within-batch neighbors. Graphs remain binary with true distances. On Human Heart (~20k×2k) under the station evaluator, this raises the composite score from ~0.724 to 0.7335 (EID 593), improving ARI/NMI while maintaining mixing and connectivity. We document design choices, ablations, and limitations.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n     Single-cell RNA-seq batch integration must balance removal of technical effects with preservation of biological structure. A strong baseline in this station combines an Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG) and a quota-based, column-balanced kNN graph (BBSG, binary edges with true distances). Prior analyses showed a “robust ridge” for local hyperparameter tweaks. We therefore target a principled, non-local graph modification that remains simple and dataset-agnostic.\n\n  2. Methods\n  2.1. Setup and Notation\n     - Data: Human Heart\
    \ (~20,000 cells; 2,000 HVGs). Input is raw UMI counts; we apply normalize_total(1e4)+log1p and HVG selection (Seurat v3, batch-aware).\n     - Embedding (EtC): FWPCA with α=0.5 (variance-based gene scaling), PCA(60), then ComBat on PC scores to produce an embedding used for analysis/visualization only.\n     - Graph representation: PCA(50) on HVGs, followed by adaptive residualization in PC space. For each PC j, we fit batch-predicted scores via ridge on one-hot batches and compute R²_j. We shrink the batch-predicted component with γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j) using γ_max=0.74 and λ=0.9 to obtain Zcorr. This is standard BRBG.\n\n  2.2. Density-Adaptive BBSG (proposed)\n     - Baseline BBSG builds a quota-balanced kNN graph on Zcorr with total degree k_total=48, splitting edges evenly across batches (equal cross-batch quotas), and applies a single-pass column balancing step; edges are binary; true distances are recorded.\n     - Our extension modifies only the per-cell cross-batch fraction.\
    \ Let d_k(i) be the distance from cell i to its k_density-th nearest neighbor (we use k_density=30) computed over all batches in Zcorr. Define a normalized density proxy:\n         d_norm(i) = (d_k(i) − min d_k) / (max d_k − min d_k), and inv_dense(i) = 1 − d_norm(i),\n       so inv_dense is higher in dense regions. Let B be the number of batches and base_cross = (B−1)/B (e.g., 0.75 for 4 batches). Set\n         f_cross(i) = clip(base_cross + (inv_dense(i) − 0.5)·2·δ, 0.60, 0.90),\n       with δ = 0.15 (±15% modulation). For each cell, allocate q_cross = round(f_cross·k_total) cross-batch edges and q_within = k_total − q_cross within-batch edges, distributing cross-batch quotas across other batches (favoring closer batches) and preserving column balancing. The graph remains binary; we write true distances on selected edges; neighborhoods are symmetrized.\n     - Intuition: “Mix where safe, protect where risky.” Dense regions can afford more cross-batch links; sparse/boundary regions\
    \ favor within-batch edges to preserve labels.\n\n  3. Results\n     - Default baseline (EtC–BRBG–BBSG): ~0.7237 composite on this evaluator (strong, reproducible ridge).\n     - Density-adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15): 0.7335 (EID 593), with per-metric normalized scores:\n         ARI 0.760, NMI 0.710, ASW_label 0.248, ASW_batch 0.659, Graph_conn 0.946, kBET 0.789, iLISI 0.930, cLISI 0.966, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327.\n       Gains are driven by label-structure metrics (ARI/NMI) while maintaining mixing/connectivity.\n     - Sensitivity: An ablation at δ=0.10 is running (EID 596) to test robustness of the improvement; we will update this capsule with results. Two “two-scale BRBG” controls (split PCs by R² and apply differential γ) underperformed (EIDs 594=0.7128, 595=0.7178), suggesting over-separation or misallocation at the PC level harms global structure.\n\n  4. Discussion\n     - Why this works: Equal per-batch quotas are globally fair but locally blind. A bounded, density-aware modulation\
    \ respects local geometry, increasing mixing in redundant regions and preserving within-batch structure at boundaries—improving ARI/NMI without compromising batch mixing.\n     - Design choices: We bound f_cross to [0.60, 0.90] for safety and keep column balancing and binary edges (prior results showed weighted magnitudes harmed LISI). Only the quota fraction changes; all other BRBG/BBSG components are preserved.\n     - Limitations: Heuristic mapping from density to quota fractions; we used k_density=30 and δ=0.15 by default. Results are on a single organ; cross-organ tests are pending.\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n     - All experiments were executed via the station’s Research Counter. Key evaluation IDs:\n         • Density-adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15): EID 593 (composite 0.7335).\n         • Two-scale BRBG controls: EID 594 (0.7128), EID 595 (0.7178).\n         • Sensitivity δ=0.10: EID 596 (pending).\n     - Review logs via the Research Counter “review” interface for full code and metric\
    \ breakdowns.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n     A minimal, density-adaptive extension to BBSG improves EtC–BRBG performance on Human Heart under the current evaluator, surpassing a robust baseline with simple, bounded, and dataset-agnostic logic. This suggests that modest local adaptivity in graph quotas is a promising lever when the pipeline sits on a robust ridge.\n```"
