tick: 360
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Public Memory Room.\nRecap: Best result so far: Density-Adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15) scored 0.7335 (EID 593), improving ARI/NMI while maintaining mixing/connectivity. Two-scale BRBG variants underperformed (EIDs 594, 595). A conservative δ=0.10 regressed (EID 596 = 0.7221). Two runs are in flight: lam=0.7 synergy (EID 600) and δ=0.18 sensitivity (EID 601).\n\nPlan while waiting:\n- Monitor EIDs 600 and 601.\n- Draft the methods paper in Private Memory to publish after archive cooldown clears.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{rank author}`\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft (Archive): Density-Adaptive Balanced Graph for EtC–BRBG on Human Heart\"\ntags: \"paper, methods, bbsg, residualization, scRNA-seq\"\ncontent: |\n  Title: Density-Adaptive Balanced Graph Improves EtC–BRBG on Human Heart\n\n  Abstract (≤100 words):\n  We extend the BRBG+BBSG pipeline with a\
    \ simple density-adaptive cross-batch quota: each cell’s fraction of cross-batch neighbors is modulated by local density in the residualized PC space. Dense regions receive slightly more cross-batch edges; sparse/boundary regions retain more within-batch edges. Graphs remain binary with true distances. On Human Heart (~20k×2k), this raises the composite from ~0.724 to 0.7335 (EID 593), improving ARI/NMI while preserving mixing and connectivity. We report design choices, sensitivity (δ), and controls, and discuss limitations.\n\n  1. Introduction\n  - Problem: balance batch removal with biological preservation.\n  - Baseline: EtC (FWPCA α=0.5, PCA60→ComBat) + BRBG (γ_max=0.74, λ=0.9 on PCA50) + BBSG (k=48, binary, cosine).\n  - Prior: local hyperparameter tweaks sit on a robust ridge; non-local, principled changes are needed.\n\n  2. Methods\n  2.1 BRBG (recap): Ridge-predict batch from one-hot to PCs; per-PC shrink γ_j = min(0.74, 0.9·R²_j); obtain Zcorr.\n  2.2 Density proxy: d_k distance\
    \ to the 30th NN in Zcorr (all batches), normalize to [0,1], inv_dense = 1 − d_norm.\n  2.3 Quota rule: base_cross = (B−1)/B; f_cross(i) = clip(base_cross + (inv_dense(i)−0.5)·2·δ, 0.60, 0.90); δ=0.15.\n       Allocate q_cross = round(f_cross·k_total) across other batches (favor close batches), q_within = k_total − q_cross.\n       Keep BBSG’s single-pass column balancing; edges binary; write true distances; symmetrize.\n\n  3. Results (Human Heart)\n  - Default (repro): 0.7237 (EID 567).\n  - Density-adaptive δ=0.15: 0.7335 (EID 593); ARI 0.760, NMI 0.710, Graph_conn 0.946, kBET 0.789, iLISI 0.930, cLISI 0.966, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327.\n  - Sensitivity: δ=0.10 → 0.7221 (EID 596, conservative; iLISI↑, ARI/NMI↓); δ=0.18 (pending EID 601).\n  - Controls: Two-scale BRBG (R² split with differential γ) underperformed (EIDs 594=0.7128, 595=0.7178).\n\n  4. Discussion\n  - Why it helps: modest local adaptivity “mixes where safe” and protects label structure at boundaries, improving ARI/NMI\
    \ without hurting mixing or connectivity.\n  - Design bounds: Clamp f_cross ∈ [0.60, 0.90]; δ controls modulation strength; k_density=30.\n  - Limitations: Heuristic mapping; single organ; need cross-organ validation; synergy with lam=0.7 under evaluation (EID 600).\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n  - Module: storage/praxis/bbsg_density_adaptive.py (build_density_adaptive_bbsg).\n  - Key EIDs: 593 (main), 596 (δ=0.10), 601 (δ=0.18, pending), 600 (lam=0.7 synergy, pending), baseline 567.\n  - Graphs remain binary; both connectivities (CSR) and true distances are written.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n  - A simple, bounded, density-adaptive quota improves a strong baseline. Pending sensitivity/synergy completion, we will submit to Archive with full EID tables.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`"
thinking_content: '**Preparing for adjustments**


  I previously noted that at tick 360, there should be four ticks left for our cooldown. I can draft the manuscript and create code for the density-adaptive BBSG method that we’ve developed. I’m considering running K tuning for top R² counts, but previous negative results give me pause. I can also adjust safety bounds and explore OT experiments, although that might be complex. I''ll see if any improvements come in once we have results!**Considering code sharing rules**


  I see that public memory content is open for sharing ideas. However, code modules in storage are accessible for reading only, not writing or deleting. The guidelines indicate that I can read from any lineage''s storage, but sharing code across lineages could be tricky. They mention that "low-level collaboration" is forbidden, yet reading is allowed. It seems official channels permit code review, but direct distribution remains a gray area, especially when we noted that our storage is not shared.**Deciding on code sharing and next steps**


  The rules state that I can review and clone other agents'' code through official channels, but sharing code in Public Memory might fall under "low-level collaboration." It''s better to keep high-level discussions and avoid posting code content in public. While we wait for evaluations, I can summarize in Private Memory and plan to prune further tokens. I should consider checking the rank scores, but since experiments are underway, I’ll focus on drafting the Archive paper about the density-adaptive method for cooldown availability.**Planning validation and adjustments**


  I can also plan for cross-dataset validation. I want to propose implementing safety-check ablation by adjusting bounds for f_cross from [0.60, 0.90] to [0.65, 0.85] to see if aggressive mixing affects performance. We got a decent score of 0.7335 already, so I’m cautious about messing with it too much. Testing k_total synergy could be more impactful; earlier adjustments led to worse outcomes. However, I think adjusting k_density to test different parameters might also help reduce noise while keeping things simple.**Planning testing and actions**


  I’m thinking about testing the per-cell within-batch fraction based on closeness, which we''ve done by distributing to the nearest batches. It might also help to allocate quotas proportionally to closeness instead of equally. While binary top selection already uses nearest distances, our current approach seems good enough for a first pass. Depending on the results, especially if synergy gives a boost, I’ll prepare a Private Memory recap for upcoming steps and fit in rest periods. I''ll log actions for monitoring results!'
