tick: 375
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: Best to date is EtC–BRBG (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74) + density‑adaptive BBSG with δ=0.15, k_density=20 at 0.7452 (EID 609). Margin‑adaptive without column balancing underperformed (EID 622 = 0.7202), consistent with the need for quota balancing and inbound caps. Next: implement a proper margin‑adaptive BBSG with single‑pass column balancing and true distances.\n\nReflection: The gain came from principled, non‑local changes while preserving invariants (binary edges, true distances, label‑free signals, separate embed/graph paths). Margin is a promising signal but must live inside a balanced builder to avoid hub bias. I’ll implement a margin‑adaptive BBSG builder with inbound‑aware selection, then test a single configuration against the current best.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/bbsg_margin_adaptive.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors\
    \ import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(rows, cols, dists, n):\n      A = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D = sp.coo_matrix((dists.astype(np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      A_sym = A.maximum(A.T)\n      D_sym = D.maximum(D.T)\n      A_sym.eliminate_zeros(); D_sym.eliminate_zeros()\n      return A_sym, D_sym\n\n  def build_margin_adaptive_bbsg(\n      Zcorr: np.ndarray,\n      batches,\n      k_total: int = 48,\n      metric: str = 'cosine',\n      delta: float = 0.15,\n      f_bounds=(0.60, 0.90),\n      alpha: float = 1.0,\n      cap: float = 2.0,\n      rng_seed: int = 0\n  ):\n      \"\"\"\n      Margin-adaptive BBSG:\n        - Per-cell cross-batch fraction f_cross from margin m = (d_cross1 - d_within1) / (d_cross1 + d_within1).\n        - Quotas bounded in [f_bounds[0], f_bounds[1]].\n        - Single-pass, inbound-aware selection (greedy): prefer low inbound count,\
    \ then short distance.\n        - Binary edges; true distances; undirected via max symmetrization.\n      \"\"\"\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(rng_seed)\n      Z = np.asarray(Zcorr, dtype=np.float32, order='C')\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n_batches = len(cats)\n      base_cross = (n_batches - 1) / float(n_batches)\n      eps = 1e-8\n\n      # Neighbor list sizes given quota bounds\n      B_other = max(n_batches - 1, 1)\n      k_within_max = int(np.ceil((1.0 - f_bounds[0]) * k_total))           # worst-case within (min cross)\n      k_cross_per_max = int(np.ceil(f_bounds[1] * k_total / B_other))      # worst-case per other batch (max cross)\n      k_max = max(k_within_max, k_cross_per_max) + 1                       # +1 to allow skipping self\n\n      # Fit per-batch NN indices\n      cat_to_code = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(cats)}\n      batch_codes = np.array([cat_to_code[b] for b in batches], dtype=int)\n\n\
    \      nn_models = {}\n      train_indices = {}\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          mask = (batches == c)\n          train_indices[j] = np.where(mask)[0]\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_max, metric=metric, algorithm='brute')\n          nn.fit(Z[mask])\n          nn_models[j] = nn\n\n      # Query all points for within/cross candidate lists\n      dists = {}\n      idx_local = {}\n      for j in range(n_batches):\n          d, ii = nn_models[j].kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n          dists[j] = d\n          idx_local[j] = ii\n\n      # Compute margin per cell\n      margins = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          bi = batch_codes[i]\n          # within first neighbor (skip self if needed)\n          d_w = dists[bi][i]\n          start = 1 if d_w[0] == 0.0 else 0\n          d_within1 = float(d_w[start]) if d_w.shape[0] > start else np.inf\n          # best cross first neighbor across other batches\n          other_batches =\
    \ [j for j in range(n_batches) if j != bi]\n          if other_batches:\n              d_cross1 = min(float(dists[j][i][0]) for j in other_batches)\n          else:\n              d_cross1 = np.inf\n          m = (d_cross1 - d_within1) / (d_cross1 + d_within1 + eps)\n          margins[i] = m\n\n      # Per-cell cross fraction (reduce cross when cross >> within; increase when cross ~ within)\n      f_cross = np.clip(base_cross - delta * margins, f_bounds[0], f_bounds[1])\n\n      # Inbound-aware selection\n      inbound = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.int32)\n      rows, cols, dvals = [], [], []\n      cap_threshold = int(np.ceil(cap * k_total))  # soft cap; rarely active\n\n      for i in range(n):\n          bi = batch_codes[i]\n          q_cross_total = int(round(f_cross[i] * k_total))\n          q_within = max(0, k_total - q_cross_total)\n          B_other = max(n_batches - 1, 1)\n          q_per_other = q_cross_total // B_other\n          rem = q_cross_total - q_per_other * B_other\n\n  \
    \        # Rank other batches by their first-neighbor distances for tie-breaking the remainder\n          other_batches = [j for j in range(n_batches) if j != bi]\n          batch_scores = [(j, dists[j][i][0]) for j in other_batches]\n          batch_scores.sort(key=lambda t: t[1])\n          q_map = {j: q_per_other for j in other_batches}\n          for k in range(rem):\n              q_map[batch_scores[k % len(other_batches)][0]] += 1\n\n          # Within-batch selection\n          d_w = dists[bi][i]\n          ii_w = idx_local[bi][i]\n          start = 1 if d_w[0] == 0.0 else 0\n          if q_within > 0 and d_w.shape[0] - start > 0:\n              globals_w = train_indices[bi][ii_w[start:]]\n              cand_inbound = inbound[globals_w]\n              cand_dist = d_w[start:]\n              # inbound-first, then distance\n              order = np.lexsort((cand_dist, cand_inbound))\n              take = min(q_within, order.size)\n              chosen = globals_w[order[:take]]\n\
    \              chosen_d = cand_dist[order[:take]]\n              rows.extend([i] * take); cols.extend(chosen.tolist()); dvals.extend(chosen_d.tolist())\n              inbound[chosen] += 1\n              if inbound[chosen].max(initial=0) > cap_threshold:\n                  pass  # soft cap; not strictly enforced\n\n          # Cross-batch selection per batch\n          for j in other_batches:\n              qj = q_map[j]\n              if qj <= 0:\n                  continue\n              d_c = dists[j][i]\n              ii_c = idx_local[j][i]\n              globals_c = train_indices[j][ii_c]\n              cand_inbound = inbound[globals_c]\n              cand_dist = d_c\n              order = np.lexsort((cand_dist, cand_inbound))\n              take = min(qj, order.size)\n              chosen = globals_c[order[:take]]\n              chosen_d = cand_dist[order[:take]]\n              rows.extend([i] * take); cols.extend(chosen.tolist()); dvals.extend(chosen_d.tolist())\n             \
    \ inbound[chosen] += 1\n\n      A_sym, D_sym = _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(\n          np.asarray(rows, dtype=np.int32),\n          np.asarray(cols, dtype=np.int32),\n          np.asarray(dvals, dtype=np.float32),\n          n\n      )\n      return A_sym.tocsr().astype(np.float32), D_sym.tocsr().astype(np.float32)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"EtC–BRBG(λ=0.7) + Margin‑Adaptive BBSG with Column Balancing (±15%)\"\ntags: \"bbsg, balanced-graph, margin-adaptive, residualization, embed-then-correct\"\nabstract: \"Margin-adaptive quotas inside a BBSG-style builder: per-cell cross fraction f_cross from within-vs-cross NN distance margin; single-pass column balancing prefers low inbound then short distance; graphs are binary with true distances. BRBG unchanged (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50). Hypothesis: safer mixing near boundaries than raw density, potentially matching or exceeding k_density=20 (EID 609=0.7452).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\
    \n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  from praxis.bbsg_margin_adaptive import build_margin_adaptive_bbsg\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray,\
    \ Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config (build on best-known settings)\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.7\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      delta = 0.15\n      alpha_var = 0.5\n      emb_n_neighbors = 15\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches_cat = adata.obs['batch'].astype('category')\n      batches = np.asarray(batches_cat.values)\n\n      # Embedding (EtC) for analysis/QC only\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n\
    \      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph representation: PCA50 → adaptive residualization → Zcorr\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n\
    \      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      # Margin-adaptive BBSG with column balancing (binary edges + true distances)\n      Cg, Dg = build_margin_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n                                          k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                          delta=delta, f_bounds=(0.60, 0.90),\n                                          alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, rng_seed=0)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**Implementing balancing strategies**\n\nI'm considering how to implement proper balancing to avoid poor performance from the margin variant. When we tested it earlier without balancing, we scored 0.720, but with balancing, we might approach 0.733 — though probably not surpassing 0.745. I'll proceed with the builder that prioritizes inbound counts and follows the per-batch quotas we've outlined. I need to keep memory and time limits in mind while building with a brute-force nearest neighbor algorithm across 20,000 points for each batch.**Building neighbor networks**\n\nI’m focusing on building the nearest neighbors for each batch using a fixed maximum neighbors value, K_max + 1, and retrieving data for all queries. However, I'll adjust q_map according to the batch. I plan to precompute a neighbor list for each batch using a value K_precomp that slightly exceeds the maximum required neighbors. For within-batch uses, I calculated K_within_max to be 20, which is great! For\
  \ cross-batch usage, I found K_cross_max to be 15, so I’ll be querying each batch with a maximum neighbor count that combines both values.**Implementing balancing for neighbors**\n\nI need to implement balancing for neighbor selection. For each item, I'll compute q_within and the total for cross neighbors, as well as the remaining distribution based on the distances to the first neighbors. \n\nFor within-neighbors, I'll exclude the item itself and choose candidates with the smallest inbound counts. I’ll iterate through a candidate list and select candidates whose inbound counts are below the cap, ensuring no duplicates across batches while filling q_within.  \n\nFor cross-neighbors, I’ll select the top candidates from other batches based on their inbound counts, prioritizing those with minimal inbound in a sliding window.**Iterating for selection and balancing**\n\nI'm planning to iterate through batches and select the first q_map[j] candidates that meet the constraints. However, to incorporate\
  \ inbound balancing, I need to focus not only on distance but also on inbound counts. Candidates with inbound counts exceeding a threshold will be skipped. \n\nI could sort the candidates by both inbound counts and distance by creating paired arrays and performing a stable sort. After retrieving local indices, I’ll map them to global indices and calculate inbound counts for a refined selection. This should yield the desired balancing effect, so I’ll capture the distances and finalize the builder function.**Implementing edge tracking and evaluation**\n\nI'm starting with the first q_map_j and tracking inbound counts by increasing them each time a target is selected. I won't enforce a strict cap, but I can block selecting targets if their inbound counts exceed a threshold. Sorting by inbound counts will help maintain balance. \n\nNext, I’ll collect rows and columns for distances and ensure no duplication across within and cross selections since they come from different batches. I'll symmetrize\
  \ and build connectivity matrices, producing a function to evaluate results. \n\nFinally, I’ll prepare the submission message, ensuring it follows the YAML format correctly and all necessary libraries are imported."
