parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Public Memory Room.\nRecap: Best config remains EtC–BRBG (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74) + density-adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15, k_density=20) with binary edges and true distances (EID 609 = 0.7452). Margin-adaptive quotas underperformed with and without column balancing (EIDs 622 = 0.7202, 624 = 0.7171). Archive and public updates posted; code module published.\n\nI’ll pause coding to reflect deeply on why density works and margin fails, then outline a principled, parameter-lean path forward.\n\n`/execute_action{goto reflect}`\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Reflection @tick378 — Why density-adaptive works, why margin underperformed, and what next\n\n  Observations\n  - Density proxy success: Using distance to the k-th NN in Zcorr (k_density=20) modestly increases cross-batch quotas in compact regions and decreases them in sparse/border regions. This improves iLISI without eroding label structure, especially when quotas are bounded\
    \ [0.60, 0.90] and selection is column-balanced (low inbound, then distance).\n  - Margin proxy shortfall: Margin uses an extreme statistic (first within vs first cross distance). It is high-variance and overly conservative at boundaries, suppressing otherwise “safe” cross edges. Even with column balancing, iLISI drops, while Graph_conn can inflate (hub bias partially reintroduced or cross edges misallocated).\n  - Invariants that matter: (1) Separate embed vs graph paths; (2) binary connectivities with true distances; (3) label-free signals; (4) per-batch quotas with single-pass column balancing and inbound fairness; (5) bounded f_cross.\n\n  Hypotheses\n  - H1 (Robustness of the signal): Density works because it aggregates over many neighbors; margin failed because it relies on the most variable pair of distances. Robust proxies (trimmed-mean distance, quantile bands) should outperform extreme-ratio signals.\n  - H2 (Budget consistency > local greed): Global quota consistency with\
    \ column balancing is essential. Any proxy that locally reduces quotas must reallocate cross edges globally to maintain class-wise flow; otherwise, safe regions under-mix and iLISI suffers.\n  - H3 (Scale matters): k_density=20 aligns well with k_total=48 and 4 batches: it probes a “meso-scale” neighborhood that’s larger than the per-batch quota slice but smaller than the global degree, stabilizing the proxy.\n\n  Principles for next changes\n  - Keep invariants: binary edges + true distances; Zcorr graph; bounded quotas; inbound-aware balancing.\n  - Prefer robust, aggregated signals over extremes.\n  - Parameter-lean: avoid new free knobs; if added, enforce global budgets or normalization to keep zero-mean shifts.\n\n  Next research directions (ranked)\n  1) Parameter-lean density: Replace k_density with a scale-free proxy (e.g., per-cell density index from the median of distances to the [15, 25] band) and normalize across cells to zero mean so that global cross-edge budget remains\
    \ unchanged (affects only allocation, not total).\n  2) Two-signal robust gating (ablation): f_cross ∝ + (robust density) − (robust margin), with each signal z-scored across cells and coefficients fixed to 1, then renormalize f_cross over the dataset to match base_cross (no fitting). This removes tuning and balances signals.\n  3) Stability checks: Unit tests for invariants (binary CSR connectivities, symmetric distances, per-batch quota sums, inbound distribution stats).\n  4) Cross-organ validation (when available): confirm that the parameter-lean variant generalizes; publish concise addendum with EIDs.\n\n  Stop-doing\n  - Do not pursue single-distance margins or entropy near boundaries—they either collapse iLISI or are neutral under balancing.\n  - Avoid heavier graph surgery (mutual graphs, deep candidate pools); prior negatives indicate regressions.\n\n  Publication plan\n  - If the parameter-lean density proxy matches 0.7452 within noise or improves it, publish an addendum “Parameter-Lean\
    \ Density-Adaptive Quotas” with full EIDs and zero-tuning argument.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering parameter adjustments**\n\nI’m thinking we can propose new heuristics to auto-set k_density using the 95th percentile neighbor distance, but I have to ensure not to rely on cell labels. It might be a good idea to compile the code into a storage module and suggest some mild bracketing of delta or lam, especially since lam at 0.7 seems to perform well. Our score of 0.7452 stands out among other lineages, and I might want to reflect on general principles before considering another paper on density adaptation and parameter-free optimization.**Exploring new approaches for f_cross**\n\nI’m considering using the distribution across all cells to propose an unsupervised scheme for f_cross. Rather than relying on the K-density parameter, I could compute a continuous smoothing scale based on the distance to the k-th neighbor, with k as ceil(0.015*n). However, that's still somewhat parameter-dependent. Alternatively, I could calibrate based on distance distribution and\
  \ compute d_k for k=round(sqrt(n)). But before diving into new experiments, maybe taking a moment to reflect in the Reflection Chamber would be helpful.**Reflecting on BBSG improvements**\n\nI’m thinking we can go to the Reflection Chamber and propose some YAML content for our reflections. It should cover fundamentals to improve BBSG, like a parameter-free variant and adaptive cross-batch quotas using the overlap coefficient. I might also tie this to the percolation threshold to ensure graph connectivity. Another idea is to maintain equal cross-edge flow with a global budget for cross edges. However, I need to be cautious about heavy stitching in mutual graphs. We may also consider adjusting k_total to 50, but previous attempts suggest it hasn't been very effective.**Exploring two-pass quotas and iLISI improvements**\n\nI have an idea about \"two-pass quotas\" where the first pass uses base quotas and the second fills deficits with the nearest cross-batch. But I'm not sure if this could\
  \ degrade mixing since a single-pass column balancing was already used. I really want to improve iLISI while keeping other metrics intact. There's potential to push iLISI further by increasing the f_bounds upper limit from 0.90 to 0.92, but that might risk our label structure, which is crucial to maintain. Alternatively, I could calculate density per cell using the average distance to neighbors across batches.**Reflecting on density parameter improvements**\n\nI’m considering modifying residualization, perhaps with a lambda value of 0.7 leading to positive synergy. I could explore adjusting gamma_max, which was neutral before. Another thought is about using HVG gating effect, computing R2 per PC vector, and possibly per-cell residualization scale, as long as it only uses batch labels. I might propose \"hemi-balanced quotas\" as well but need to be careful about unintended label use. \n\nIt feels crucial to reflect on the density parameter and explore parameter-free variants. After reflection,\
  \ I’ll log my next steps in Private Memory while pausing new experiments for now.**Planning reflection and future actions**\n\nI'm ready to reflect on the generalizable formulation of \"density-adaptive quotas,\" explaining why k=20 is the best choice. This value approximates the neighbor count that reflects the subcluster scale, based on the interplay of a 48-degree graph and four batches. \n\nI’ll propose a theoretical 'safe mixing star' criterion to require a d_cross1/d_within1 threshold while noting that previous attempts show boundaries can be critical. I’m considering robust stats like the median for top-L distances and might design a zero-free parameter approach for calibrating weights without coding it just yet. I'll draft the final message summarizing these actions, including my identity and location."
