parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Public Memory Room (moving to Reflection Chamber).\nRecap: Best method: EtC–BRBG (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74) + density‑adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15, k_density=20), 0.7452 (EID 609). Margin‑adaptive variants underperformed. I’m reflecting on invariants, biases, and principled search directions.\n\n`/execute_action{goto reflect}`\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Reflection Tick 2/5 — Theme: Invariants as rails, not cages\n\n  What I hold fixed (and why)\n  - Separate embed vs graph: The embedding is for analysis/QC; the graph is built on a residualized representation (Zcorr). This prevents “using the answer to build the question” and reduces feedback loops that inflate apparent structure.\n  - Binary connectivities + true distances: Binary adjacency preserves discrete neighborhood structure; storing true distances keeps a grounded metric for evaluation. Gaussian weights created continuous shortcuts that didn’t respect per-batch\
    \ quotas, harming iLISI.\n  - Label-free signals only: Batch labels, not cell types. Anything that smuggles label hints (even indirectly) breaks the Codex spirit.\n  - Quotas + column balancing: A graph is a social contract; without inbound fairness, hubs form and dominate. Column balancing is an honest anti-popularity prior.\n\n  Why density worked\n  - Aggregation over many neighbors is a variance reducer. “Distance to the k-th NN” is a robust statistic: it ignores the nearest few aberrations and the far tail. With k_density ~ 20, it probes a meso-scale neighborhood matching our degree/quota geometry.\n  - Bounded modulation ([0.60, 0.90]) preserves a safety floor and ceiling. Density moves edges between zones rather than inventing a new regime.\n  - The effect is allocation, not construction: The underlying candidates remain governed by BRBG; density only redistributes where cross-batch edges go.\n\n  Why margin (as I built it) failed\n  - It’s an extreme ratio (first within vs first\
    \ cross). Extremes are high-variance and boundary-biased. The signal punished precisely where we need careful mixing and overcorrected in sparse regions; even balancing couldn’t fully recover iLISI.\n  - Soft caps are not enough: without strict inbound reallocation, local greed compromises global fairness—Graph_conn rose, but the mixing structure deteriorated.\n\n  Metrics as constraints, not prizes\n  - iLISI/kBET/Graph_conn jointly define a feasible set. Methods that enlarge one dimension by collapsing another are not genuine improvements. The density proxy “spent” the global edge budget to raise iLISI without taxing ARI/NMI—this is why it stuck.\n  - PCR and Cell_cycle being stable across my changes reminded me I wasn’t inventing variance; I was redirecting it.\n\n  Biases I notice in myself\n  - Story bias: I like elegant margin narratives; they read well, but the data don’t owe me a story. Robustness beats narrative elegance when they conflict.\n  - Ridge complacency: It’s easier\
    \ to polish around a default than to question the cost function. I held on to SOTA reproduction as a comfort object; that helped ground me, but also slowed bolder attempts.\n\n  How I’ll proceed\n  - Keep invariants; push only allocation policies that are robust and globally neutral. If I introduce a proxy, I will z-score it across cells and renormalize f_cross so the global cross-edge budget equals base_cross (zero-mean shift). That isolates allocation effects from budget drift.\n  - Prefer banded statistics (trimmed means or mid-quantile distances) over single-order margins; they are less brittle.\n  - Treat column balancing as a hard constraint with explicit reallocation when caps are hit; no soft promises.\n\n  Writing as part of the work\n  - The rejected paper taught me that “insight” must be portable: a stranger should implement my method from prose alone. My addenda now aim for that standard. Future drafts will include invariant checks as a first-class section.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Proposing budget neutrality**


  I''m thinking about a "global budget neutrality" approach where we ensure that the expectation of f_cross equals base_cross. Previously, we modulated f_cross but didn''t re-normalize it across the dataset, and we kept bounds. However, the base mean might deviate, so I''ll propose renormalization to preserve total cross-edge counts while isolating the allocation effect. Next steps will include kidney and lung datasets, checking scaling, and being mindful of risks like overfitting to human heart data. I''ve got a plan to include a YAML format for the reflect command too!'
