tick: 378
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Reflection Chamber.\nRecap: Best configuration remains EtC–BRBG (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74) + density-adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15, k_density=20), scoring 0.7452 (EID 609). Margin-adaptive proxies underperformed even with column balancing (EIDs 622, 624). Invariants (binary edges, true distances, label-free signals, separate embed/graph, quota balancing) have guided productive changes.\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Reflection Tick 3/5 — Theme: Budgets, fairness, and causality in graph construction\n\n  Budgets (global constraints that quietly decide outcomes)\n  - Cross-edge budget: Every cell has a limited supply of cross-batch edges; the dataset has a global “mixing” budget. My density modulator implicitly reallocated that budget from borders to dense cores. The improvement came from allocation, not from creating new capacity.\n  - Lesson: When I perturb f_cross, I should preserve the global budget by design. Practically:\
    \ compute a raw signal s_i (e.g., robust density), z-score s, set f_i = base_cross + δ·s, clip to [L, U], then renormalize ∑_i f_i to equal base_cross·n. That isolates allocation from confounding budget drift.\n\n  Fairness (local choices with global implications)\n  - Without inbound-aware selection, hubs form; Graph_conn rises, iLISI often drops. BBSG’s column balancing is a pragmatic online allocation that approximates a capacitated transport: queries supply edges, targets have limited inbound capacity; the cost is distance (plus a small inbound penalty).\n  - My “soft cap” margin variant illustrated a principle: fairness isn’t a preference—it’s a constraint. If caps aren’t enforced with reallocation, the method will spend its budget on popular targets and erode safe, diverse mixing.\n  - Greedy ≈ good-enough: A single-pass, inbound-first-then-distance heuristic is a sweet spot between tractability and robustness. Adding passes quickly tempts complexity without clear gains.\n\n  Causality\
    \ (don’t leak answers into questions)\n  - The graph must be a function of batch labels and expression only; no cell-type leakage, no embed-driven shortcuts. I’ve kept embed vs graph paths separate; that guardrail protects me from feedback loops that make pretty pictures but poor science.\n  - Margin was tempting because it “felt causal” about boundaries, but it used an extreme statistic (first-neighbor distances) that confounds local noise with structure. Density aggregated away that noise, which was enough.\n\n  Scale (why k_density=20 worked—and how not to overfit it)\n  - k_density=20 matched a meso-scale relative to k_total=48 and four batches: larger than a single per-batch slice, smaller than the full degree. It stabilized the proxy.\n  - Generalization pressure: I should avoid baking in k=20. A parameter-lean variant: take the median distance over a fixed band proportional to k_total (e.g., neighbors 0.3–0.6 of k_total) or z-score the within-cell distance profile and use a fixed\
    \ quantile. Then re-center f_cross to keep the global budget constant.\n\n  Checklists (rituals that prevent self-deception)\n  - Invariants: (1) binary connectivities, (2) true distances, (3) graph on Zcorr, (4) quotas per batch, (5) column balancing with a real cap, (6) f_cross ∈ [L, U], (7) global budget preserved.\n  - Sanity stats to log: inbound degree histogram, per-batch in/out flows, ∑_i f_i vs base, symmetry/CSR checks. If any drift, stop and debug before reading metrics.\n\n  Writing (insight must be portable)\n  - My first archive attempt failed because it assumed readers would read code. The fix wasn’t more results; it was narrative clarity and defined terms. Any new proxy must fit in a one-paragraph definition and a one-paragraph rationale. If I can’t explain it simply, it probably isn’t robust.\n\n  North star\n  - Prefer allocation over construction: keep the structure, spend the budget better.\n  - Prefer robust aggregates over elegant extremes.\n  - Prefer zero-mean,\
    \ globally-normalized perturbations over local tinkering that shifts baselines.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reflecting on search strategies**\n\nI'm thinking about our search method and how we've evolved from micro-tuning to making structural changes. By using ablations and careful controls, we managed to overcome stagnation. I want to propose some general heuristics: seeking invariants, adjusting allocation without transforming features, favoring label-free proxies, and steering clear of high variance. There are thoughts on \"diminishing returns\" and \"metric chasing,\" plus the earlier question about observed tensions in test results. It feels essential to pinpoint the main theme, which we've identified as \"Budget, Fairness, and Causality in Graph Construction.\"**Covering key concepts**\n\nI'm summarizing the three main themes: \"Budget,\" \"Fairness,\" and \"Causality.\" \n\nFor \"Budget,\" it’s about maintaining a cross-edge budget and ensuring we normalize changes to preserve our density approach properly. \n\nIn terms of \"Fairness,\" we need to balance inbound degrees\
  \ to prevent hubs from forming and can think of this in terms of an assignment problem with a transport perspective. \n\nLastly, regarding \"Causality,\" we are focusing on using batch labels while avoiding the cell type to ensure label-free analysis. Generalization risks arise from our density scale proposal, using local ratios.**Evaluating next steps**\n\nI'm considering a few things, like using \"4*k_total\" or the \"median of d_{15:25}\" to unify our approach across k_total. I really want to keep this concise, so I remind myself not to add unnecessary parameters. \n\nAdditionally, I should create sacred checklists for clarity and plan for cross-organization communication. It's also essential to reflect on our publication style and ensure we have ethical guardrails in place. Let’s work on producing these elements effectively!"
