parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Reflection Chamber.\nRecap: Best method: EtC–BRBG (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74) + density‑adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15, k_density=20), 0.7452 (EID 609). Margin‑adaptive proxies underperformed even with column balancing. I’m reflecting on the underlying principles and how to generalize them without overfitting the evaluator.\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Reflection Tick 4/5 — Theme: From heuristic tweaks to principled allocation\n\n  I: I gravitate to small, testable changes. The density win revealed something deeper: the problem I’m solving is not “find better neighbors,” but “allocate a fixed cross-batch budget fairly under geometric constraints.” The success came when I treated graph construction as allocation, not architecture.\n\n  A principled view\n  - Think of BRBG + BBSG as a capacitated transport: each query (cell) supplies k_total edges; targets have a limited inbound “capacity” (enforced by column balancing); costs\
    \ are distances in Zcorr; fairness is encoded by penalizing popular targets (inbound-first selection).\n  - Density modulation acts like a per-query regularizer on cross-batch flow: in dense regions, allow more cross flow; at borders, restrict. Crucially, it doesn’t change the global budget—it reallocates it.\n  - Margin, as I implemented it, perturbed quotas with a brittle signal and sometimes violated “budget consistency in practice” by starving cross edges where they were still safe. That violated the spirit of an allocation problem with soft fairness constraints.\n\n  Budgets and duals\n  - The “inbound counts” are proxies for dual variables (Lagrange multipliers) in a min-cost flow: popular targets become expensive. My soft-cap margin variant ignored this dual update; the column-balanced one respected it but still overreacted to a noisy signal.\n  - Design principle: if I modify f_cross, I must (a) preserve the global sum exactly (post-normalize), and (b) keep the inbound dual pressure\
    \ active so capacity is respected while the allocation shifts.\n\n  Robustness over elegance\n  - Density works because it aggregates across many neighbors—variance suppression. Margin relied on an extreme statistic (the closest within vs cross). In high-dimensional, semi-sparse geometry, extremes are fragile and encode noise. Aggregates over bands (trimmed means, mid-quantiles) are resilient.\n  - This suggests a scale-free alternative: compute a robust density index from a band (e.g., neighbors 30–60% of k_total), z-score across cells, apply a fixed δ, clip to [L, U], and then renormalize the global cross budget. No new knobs beyond δ and bounds. If δ remains fixed, the entire scheme is parameter-lean.\n\n  Metrics as multi-objective constraints\n  - iLISI (mixing), Graph_conn (cohesion), ARI/NMI (biology preservation) together define a Pareto frontier. Gaussian weights improved neither frontier nor stability; they smudged topology. Density-adaptive quotas improved mixing without collapsing\
    \ biology—hence a genuine frontier move.\n  - Beware metric anchoring: scores are normalized to baselines. I remind myself the aim isn’t to chase iLISI alone but to keep a balanced profile. The best win (EID 609) improved iLISI while holding ARI/NMI steady and PCR/Cell_cycle neutral—consistent with “reallocation” rather than “fabrication.”\n\n  Generalization and humility\n  - The sweet spot k_density=20 matched the meso-scale of our graph. I won’t canonize that number; I will formulate a banded, scale-free proxy and enforce zero-mean shifts to avoid dataset-specific drift.\n  - Cross-organ validation is a duty, not optional. If the idea is truly principled, it should transfer with the same invariants and minimal knobs.\n\n  Writing and stewardship\n  - A reproducible method is a contract: clear prose, minimal parameters, and invariant checks (binary CSR, true distances, per-batch quota sums, inbound histogram, global f_cross sum). These checks should be listed in the paper as part of\
    \ the method, not relegated to code.\n  - My lineage memory is for successors; I owe them clarity, not just EIDs. The guiding sentence I keep: “Prefer allocation over construction; prefer robust aggregates over elegant extremes; preserve global budgets; enforce fairness as a constraint, not a preference.”\n```"
