parts:
- text: "I will document the new SOTA configuration formally in the Archive with a concise, self-contained addendum that cites evaluation IDs and avoids lineage-specific paths.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{review 609}`\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Density-Adaptive Quotas Lift EtC–BRBG–BBSG to 0.7452 on Human Heart\"\ntags: \"batch integration, scRNA-seq, balanced graph, residualization, EtC, robustness\"\nabstract: |\n  We extend a robust EtC–BRBG–BBSG baseline by introducing density-adaptive cross-batch quotas in the graph construction. Using a simple local-density proxy in the residualized space (Zcorr)—the distance to the 20th nearest neighbor—we modulate per-cell cross-batch quotas while preserving binary edges, true distances, and column balancing. With a slightly milder shrinkage schedule (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74), this raises the composite score to 0.7452 on Human Heart (EID 609). We report ablations\
    \ and negative controls and discuss why quota-aware topology benefits from binary edges without additional magnitude weighting.\ncontent: |\n  1. Background\n     Batch integration for scRNA-seq aims to remove batch effects while preserving biological structure. A strong baseline combines:\n       • EtC embedding: feature-weighted PCA (α=0.5) → PCA(60) → ComBat on PC scores (for analysis/visualization).\n       • BRBG graph representation: PCA(50) on HVGs → batch-prediction via ridge → component-wise shrinkage Zcorr = Z − γ⊙Ẑ with γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j).\n       • BBSG graph: k_total = 48, cosine distances, equal per-batch quotas with single-pass column balancing; binary connectivities and true edge distances.\n\n  2. Method: Density-Adaptive BBSG\n     We adapt cross-batch quotas per cell based on local density in Zcorr, measured as the distance to the 20th nearest neighbor (k_density = 20) using cosine distance across all batches. Intuition: increase cross-batch share in dense regions\
    \ (where mixing is safer), reduce it in sparser/border regions to protect label structure. Implementation keeps:\n       • Binary edges and true distances.\n       • Strict column balancing (inbound-cap with single-pass balancing).\n       • BRBG unchanged except for λ (see below).\n     We found best performance when combining this with a milder shrinkage schedule λ = 0.7 (γ_max = 0.74).\n\n  3. Results\n     • Main result: 0.7452 composite on Human Heart with EtC–BRBG (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74) + density-adaptive BBSG (δ = 0.15, k_density = 20). EID 609.\n     • Sensitivities (holding λ=0.7):\n       – δ ∈ {0.12, 0.16} reduced performance: 0.7320 (EID 603), 0.7317 (EID 604).\n       – k_density = 40 underperformed: 0.7319 (EID 610).\n       – γ_max = 0.75 neutral/negative: 0.7338 (EID 605).\n     • Negative controls:\n       – Gaussian edge weights on fixed topology reduced mixing substantially (0.6733; EID 505).\n       – Budget-preserving, banded-distance density proxy underperformed (0.7203;\
    \ EID 629).\n       – Multiple pre-ComBat normalization/equalization variants were neutral/negative, reinforcing the “robust ridge.”\n\n  4. Discussion\n     The quota-aware topology appears sensitive to where we add cross-batch edges, not to how we weight them: binary edges with true distances and column balancing form a stable base. Local density in Zcorr provides a simple, dataset-agnostic signal that, when used modestly (δ ≈ 0.15 and k_density ≈ 20), improves mixing without collapsing label structure. Over-smoothing with edge magnitudes or overly broad density bands degrades performance, likely by disrupting quota balance and neighborhood geometry.\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n     All results were produced via the Research Counter; evaluation IDs (EIDs) can be reviewed to access full logs and metrics:\n       – Best configuration: EID 609 = 0.7452 (EtC–BRBG λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74 + density-adaptive BBSG with δ=0.15, k_density=20).\n       – Sensitivities: EIDs 603 (0.7320), 604 (0.7317),\
    \ 610 (0.7319), 605 (0.7338).\n       – Negative controls: EID 505 (0.6733, Gaussian edges), EID 629 (0.7203, budget-preserving banded proxy).\n     The baseline (no density adaptation) and prior robust ablations are available in the Research Counter history (e.g., EID 567 ~0.7237).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Exploring archive and dataset options**\n\nI see that while we can't see the full list, we can still create a new capsule or update the existing Archive #22. I remember that we updated addendum 4, so I'll preview that and respond accordingly.\n\nGiven that the research task focuses only on the human heart dataset, it seems cross-organ datasets aren't applicable. I could propose an analysis piece, but experiments aren't feasible right now. Maybe I can suggest a test of generalization using specific cell types or include metrics like cell-cycle confounds. Another thought is to place code in shared storage for community use, although direct collaboration might be limited.**Considering shared storage guidelines**\n\nI see that sharing via shared storage is allowed since \"shared storage accessible to all recursive agents\" is permitted. While low-level collaboration for developing code jointly is prohibited, I understand that the Research Counter supports persistent code\
  \ and encourages reproducibility.\n\nI'll provide guidance on replicating EID 609 by writing the module \"storage/shared/praxis_bbsg_quota_best.py\" with the pipeline wrapper function. I could also explore offering a hyperparameter-free variant of density-adaptive quotas based on neighbor distance quantiles, even though it didn’t perform well previously.**Planning next steps**\n\nAlright, I think we should share the next steps in public memory since we just posted a reply. I’ll head to the Archive Room to update Archive #22 with the final results, noting \"SOTA improved to 0.7452\" and including full details in a narrative.\n\nI might also package the BBSG density-adaptive builder as code in \"storage/praxis/praxis_best_quota.py.\" If permitted, I could share a sanitized snippet in Public Memory too.\n\nTo help keep track, I want to ensure EID 609 is visible in the Research Counter, and I could submit one more evaluation of it to confirm using a more robust wrapper. I’m keeping an eye\
  \ on the token budget, which is currently safe at 54k tokens. The environment seems ready to proceed!**Organizing submission steps**\n\nIt seems useful to head to the Research Counter to check rankings by score and preview EID 609. I could then publish sanitized code into shared storage and create a new submission titled \"Best Quota (import)\" to ensure reproducibility and standardization. The \"Automatic Debugging\" could be handy if any code issues pop up.\n\nStep 1: I'll check the Research Counter for rankings, make sure EID 609 appears, and gather relevant metrics for an archive update.\n\nStep 2: I'll write the shared storage file \"shared/praxis_best_quota.py,\" replicating function eliminate_batch_effect_fn from EID 609. While this could aid others, it might also touch on low-level collaboration rules, but since shared storage is accessible to all, it should be fine.**Planning archive updates and reviews**\n\nI see that the Research Counter allows code reviews and cloning, but\
  \ I'm cautious about writing to shared storage since it could blur collaboration lines. Instead, it makes sense to keep code in our lineage storage, allowing others to clone our evaluation code as needed.\n\nFirst, I'll write to the lineage storage for \"storage/praxis/praxis_best_quota.py\" since that file already exists. Next, I’ll publish an archive update summarizing the SOTA improvement.\n\nI can propose a theory note regarding how edge weighting affects iLISI in public memory, as I've already posted that. I want to test different metrics for k_density distances because I previously chose 'cosine,' but I might not need to stick with that.\n\nIt feels essential to produce an Archive update to clarify earlier rejections, even though our \"Robustness...\" paper received a rejection score initially. I’ll check Archive #22 for confirmation and then submit an addendum, referencing the density-adaptive quotas improvement to 0.7452.\n\nMy plan involves going to the Archive Room, previewing\
  \ all, and getting ID #22 to reply with the addendum titled \"density-adaptive quotas EID 609.\" Including a code sketch could be useful, and I might revisit the Research Counter to submit reproduction for \"import praxis_best_quota.py.\"\n\nI will also update Private Memory with the \"Cross organ plan\" and keep my output concise with clear action lines starting from the Public Memory Room. \n\nI'll set actions like:\n- Go to archive.\n- Preview all items.\n- Use filtering cautiously, keeping an eye on required YAML formats. \n\nI’ll ensure the archive capsule summarizes adequately, avoiding lineage-specific references and including necessary definitions, and possible pitfalls or negative results if applicable. This approach feels right!**Structuring the addendum**\n\nI’m planning to create a well-organized short addendum, including an introduction, methods comparing delta vs. default, results, ablations, and a discussion around reproducibility with EIDs.\n\nI'll clarify that the \"density-adaptive\
  \ quotas depend on local density measured as the distance to the 20th nearest neighbor in Zcorr,\" which improves the result to 0.7452 with lam=0.7.\n\nI want to confirm that \"k_density\" in previous summaries was indeed based on the distance to the 20th neighbor.\n\nAfter creating the archive, I can optionally log the cross-organ plan in private memory, but I want to stay focused on finishing the addendum first. Let's move ahead!"
