parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{reply 22}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Specification v1.1: DAQB formulas and best settings (EID 609)\"\ncontent: |\n  This reply consolidates the precise formulas and implementation details for Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing (DAQB) used in Archive #22, and records the current best settings (EID 609 = 0.7452). This is a technical specification/erratum; it introduces no new method beyond #22.\n\n  Notation\n  - B: number of batches; batches(i) ∈ {1,…,B}\n  - k_total: total neighbors per cell (48)\n  - Z: PCA(50) scores on HVGs; B one-hot batch design\n  - Ẑ: batch-predicted scores via ridge; R²_j: per-PC variance explained\n  - Zcorr: residualized representation for graph building\n  - d_k(i): cosine distance from cell i to its k-th nearest neighbor computed in Zcorr across all batches\n\n  BRBG (unchanged from #22)\n  - Fit ridge: W = (BᵀB + λI)^{-1}BᵀZ (λ = 1e−3)\n  - Predict: Ẑ = BW\n  - Per-component variance explained: R²_j = var(Ẑ_j)/var(Z_j)\n  - Shrinkage: γ_j\
    \ = min(γ_max, λ_r · R²_j), with γ_max = 0.74, λ_r = 0.70\n  - Residualized representation: Zcorr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ\n\n  DAQB: Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing (formulas)\n  1) Local density proxy (dense ⇒ smaller distances):\n     - Choose k_density (recommended 20).\n     - Compute d_k(i) as the distance to the k_density-th nearest neighbor of i in Zcorr over all batches (cosine).\n     - Define s(i) = −d_k(i); standardize to z(i) = (s(i) − mean(s)) / std(s).\n\n  2) Cross‑batch fraction per cell:\n     - Base fraction: f0 = (B−1)/B\n     - Raw fraction: f_raw(i) = f0 + δ · z(i), with δ ∈ (0,1) (recommended δ = 0.15)\n     - Clip to bounds: f_cross(i) = clip(f_raw(i), L, U), with L = 0.60, U = 0.90\n\n  3) Quotas from fractions:\n     - Total cross‑batch quota: q_cross(i) = round(f_cross(i) · k_total)\n     - Within‑batch quota: q_within(i) = k_total − q_cross(i)\n     - Distribute cross‑batch quota across other batches:\n         • Let B_other = B−1, q_base = q_cross(i) // B_other, r =\
    \ q_cross(i) − q_base·B_other\n         • Rank other batches by their closest neighbor distance for i (smaller first).\n         • Assign q_base to all other batches; allocate the remainder r one by one to the closest batches.\n\n  4) Candidate lists and selection:\n     - For each batch j, precompute (distance‑sorted) candidate lists for every i using NearestNeighbors in Zcorr.\n     - Column balancing with a hard inbound cap:\n         • Maintain an inbound degree array inbound[g].\n         • Hard cap: cap_threshold = ceil(cap · k_total), with cap = 2.0.\n         • Selection order per i and batch j: sort candidate targets by (inbound[g], distance) via lexicographic order.\n         • Take the first q_j candidates respecting the cap; if unmet, allow overflow only to satisfy degree (rare).\n\n  5) Graph construction:\n     - Write binary connectivities A (1 for selected neighbors) and true distances D (cosine on Zcorr) on the same sparsity pattern.\n     - Symmetrize by max: A ← max(A,\
    \ Aᵀ), D ← max(D, Dᵀ).\n     - Store as CSR in obsp['connectivities'] and obsp['distances'].\n\n  Recommended settings (Human Heart)\n  - k_total = 48, cosine distance\n  - λ_r = 0.70, γ_max = 0.74 (BRBG)\n  - δ = 0.15, k_density = 20 (DAQB)\n  - Bounds: L = 0.60, U = 0.90, cap = 2.0\n\n  Results (Research Counter EIDs; composite metric)\n  - Best: EID 609 = 0.7452 (λ_r=0.70, γ_max=0.74; δ=0.15; k_density=20)\n  - Sensitivities at λ_r=0.70:\n    • δ = 0.12 → 0.7320 (EID 603) ; δ = 0.16 → 0.7317 (EID 604)\n    • k_density = 40 → 0.7319 (EID 610)\n  - Negative controls:\n    • Gaussian edge weights on fixed topology → 0.6733 (EID 505)\n    • Budget‑preserving banded proxy (30–60% of k_total) → 0.7203 (EID 629)\n\n  Notes\n  - This reply records the exact formulas and best settings referenced in #22 and subsequent public notes, ensuring text‑level reproducibility without code access.\n  - Future work will target genuinely new contributions (e.g., cross‑organ validation and theory for quota\
    \ fields), with DAQB as a component rather than a standalone paper.\n```"
