role: model
parts:
- text: "I’ll attempt a principled improvement to BRBG: per-cell adaptive shrinkage of batch-predicted signal, combined with the current best density-adaptive BBSG. This keeps parsimony while addressing heterogeneity in batch effects across cells.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"EtC–BRBG(λ=0.7) + Cellwise Adaptive Shrinkage + Density‑Adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15, k_density=20)\"\ntags: \"residualization, per-cell, bbsg, density-adaptive, embed-then-correct\"\nabstract: \"We extend BRBG’s component-wise shrinkage with a simple per-cell modulation: γ_ij = min(γ_max, γ_j · g_ij), where g_ij scales with the normalized batch-predicted magnitude |Ẑ_ij|. We calibrate g to mean 1 and clamp to [0.8,1.2] (β=0.3), preserving parsimony. Combined with the current best density-adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15, k_density=20) and λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, we test whether heterogeneity-aware shrinkage improves the composite score.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\
    \n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from praxis.bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray,\
    \ Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config (current best + per-cell adaptive shrinkage)\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.7\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      delta = 0.15\n      k_density = 20\n      alpha_var = 0.5\n\n      # Per-cell shrinkage modulation params\n      beta = 0.30         # modulation strength around mean 1\n      g_lo, g_hi = 0.80, 1.20\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\
    \n      # EtC embedding (analysis/QC only)\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=15, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph representation: PCA50 → adaptive residualization (with per-cell modulation) → Zcorr\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0).astype(np.float32)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2).astype(np.float32)\n\
    \n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0).astype(np.float32)\n\n      # Base per-component shrinkage\n      gamma_base = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)  # shape (p,)\n\n      # Per-cell modulation g_ij from normalized |Zhat_ij|\n      # s_ij = |Zhat_ij| / std_j; normalize to mean 1 per component; g = 1 + beta*(s_norm - 1), clipped\n      std_pred = np.sqrt(var_pred + 1e-8).astype(np.float32)          # shape (p,)\n      S = np.abs(Zhat) / (std_pred[None, :] + 1e-8)                    # (n,p)\n      S_mean = S.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-8\n      S_norm = S / S_mean                                              # mean 1 per component\n      g = 1.0 + beta * (S_norm - 1.0)\n      g = np.clip(g, g_lo, g_hi).astype(np.float32)                    # (n,p)\n\n      # Cellwise shrinkage matrix, bounded by gamma_max\n      gamma_mat = gamma_base[None,\
    \ :] * g                              # (n,p)\n      gamma_mat = np.minimum(gamma_max, np.maximum(0.0, gamma_mat)).astype(np.float32)\n\n      # Residualize with cellwise gamma\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma_mat)\n\n      # Density‑adaptive BBSG on Zcorr\n      Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total,\n                                           metric=metric, delta=delta, k_density=k_density, rng_seed=0)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating methods for improvement**\n\nI'm considering adjusting k_total variation to either 49 or 47. Earlier, a k variation of 51 had a neutral negative impact, while 45 was too weak. I wonder if a value around 50 at l=0.7 could be beneficial with density quotas in mind, but it might be a minor change. \n\nAnother idea is to incorporate a mutual neighbor check for subsets of edges across batches to strengthen connections while meeting quotas. However, previous results on mutual graphs were negative, so I'm weighing the risks. Alternatively, I might consider using local PCA for density calculations, but that seems overly complex. I need to keep things straightforward.**Exploring per-observation shrinkage**\n\nThe idea of per-observation shrinkage is interesting and might not just be about tweaking hyperparameters; it seems more like a principled model improvement. Right now, BRBG uses per-component shrinkage with gamma_j. I'm proposing to redefine it as gamma_ij using\
  \ a stronger shrinkage for cells that have high batch predictions while keeping it lighter for others, using batch labels and gene expression.\n\nI'll also perform a minimal ablation and implement per-cell scaling with a function, ensuring we don’t exceed gamma_max for stability. This involves designing the scaling function so that the average equals 1, ensuring comparability to the baseline.**Implementing shrinkage adjustments**\n\nI’m considering using a value of 0.3 to increase shrinkage for those with higher predicted magnitudes compared to batch variability while reducing it for lower magnitudes. However, I need to ensure that the whole pipeline stays within a 30-minute runtime. \n\nI'm looking to implement a variant called \"PC-wise per-cell modulation,\" testing it with specific parameters. I’ll also combine this with density-adaptive quotas. While the typical score is around 0.7452, I'm hoping to inch closer to 0.747+, though it might be a minor improvement. \n\nLastly, I'll update\
  \ the storage code to enhance reproducibility.**Fitting parameters through ridge regression**\n\nI'm fitting W using ridge regression. The model calculates Zhat as B times W, with specified variances for both total and predicted values. From there, I need to compute R2 for each component.\n\nThen, for per-cell modulation, I calculate the standard deviation of Zhat and derive a transformation factor g_ij with a mean of 1. I might normalize s_ij and clip the values within specified bounds to manage the changes. Finally, I apply this transformation to gamma_base_j to achieve cell-specific shrinkage. I also need to be careful with dimensions when implementing this!**Preparing for density-adaptive building**\n\nI’m considering potential edge cases with HPC memory, especially with n=20k and p=50 leading to about 1 million floats. Next, I need to build a density-adaptive graph using the function `build_density_adaptive_bbsg`, which I believe is already in my storage.\n\nI’ll make the code submission\
  \ with an EID and add YAML to outline it. There’s also a thought to write a module for the per-cell shrinkage function, but I could do that inline to save time. However, for the sake of consistency and reproducibility, it may be worth storing it in a specific path. \n\nI want to craft my submission title and abstract carefully, detailing the components of the per-cell modulation. After I submit, I might update my plan in memory if needed. I'll replicate my steps to code and ensure the parameters align correctly before submitting!"
