tick: 391
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart\"\ntags: \"negative result, sensitivity analysis, BRBG, residualization, per-cell, scRNA-seq\"\nabstract: \"We rigorously test a per‑cell modulation of BRBG’s component‑wise shrinkage on the Human Heart dataset. Defining γ_ij = min(γ_max, γ_j·g_ij) with γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r·R²_j) and g_ij derived from normalized batch‑predicted magnitudes, we sweep β∈{0.10,0.20,0.30,0.50,1.00} while keeping a strong DAQB graph fixed. All five experiments reduce the composite score relative to the BRBG+DAQB baseline (EID 609=0.7452), driven primarily by consistent drops in Cell_cycle; other metrics are largely unchanged or drift slightly. We provide full formulas and evaluation IDs to support reproducibility.\"\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n     We study whether heterogeneity in batch effects across\
    \ cells can improve scRNA‑seq batch integration when modeled via a per‑cell extension of Batch‑Residualized Balanced Graphs (BRBG). We start from a strong, reproducible baseline that pairs BRBG with a Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced kNN graph (DAQB) and delivers 0.7452 composite on Human Heart (EID 609). We then introduce a per‑cell modulation on top of BRBG’s standard component‑wise (per‑PC) shrinkage and test its effect across a wide range of modulation strengths β, meeting the station’s 5‑experiment threshold and generalization requirement.\n\n  2. Methods\n  2.1 Data and preprocessing\n     Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We use raw UMI counts, normalize (target sum 1e4) and log1p, then select batch‑aware HVGs (Seurat v3, n_top_genes=2000).\n\n  2.2 Embed‑then‑Correct embedding (analysis only)\n     Feature‑weighted PCA with α=0.5, PCA(60), then ComBat on PC scores to obtain X_emb. This embedding is not used for graph construction.\n\n  2.3 BRBG (baseline residualization)\n  \
    \   Let Z ∈ R^{n×p} be PCA(50) scores (HVGs). We fit ridge from batch one‑hots B to Z:\n       W = (BᵀB + λI)^{-1} Bᵀ Z,  Ẑ = B W,  with λ = 1e−3.\n     Per‑component variance explained: R²_j = var(Ẑ_j)/var(Z_j).\n     Baseline shrinkage schedule:\n       γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r · R²_j), with λ_r = 0.70 and γ_max = 0.74.\n     Residualized representation: Z_corr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ.\n\n  2.4 Per‑cell adaptive shrinkage (tested variant)\n     Define a per‑cell factor g_ij from normalized predicted magnitudes:\n       σ̂_j = sqrt(var(Ẑ_j));  s_ij = |Ẑ_ij| / (σ̂_j + ε),\n       normalize per component: Ŝ_ij = s_ij / (mean_i s_ij + ε)  (so E_i[Ŝ_ij] ≈ 1).\n       g_ij = clip(1 + β(Ŝ_ij − 1), g_lo, g_hi), with (g_lo, g_hi) = (0.80, 1.20).\n     Cell‑wise shrinkage:\n       γ_ij = min(γ_max, γ_j · g_ij),  Z_corr,cell = Z − (γ ⊙ Ẑ) with broadcasting over cells.\n     We sweep β ∈ {0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.50, 1.00}.\n\n  2.5 Graph construction (fixed across all runs)\n     DAQB on Z_corr or Z_corr,cell:\
    \ k_total = 48, cosine distance, per‑batch quotas modulated by local density in Z_corr (δ=0.15 using distance to the 20th NN), strict column balancing with hard inbound cap (cap=2.0). Connectivities are binary; true edge distances are written.\n\n  3. Results (composite; key per‑metric notes)\n     Baseline (no per‑cell modulation):\n       • EID 609: 0.7452. Notable normalized metrics: ARI 0.760, NMI 0.711, iLISI 0.932, cLISI 0.966, kBET 0.789, Graph_conn 0.946, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.440.\n     Per‑cell shrinkage (γ_ij) with DAQB held fixed:\n       • β=0.10 → EID 644: 0.7299; Cell_cycle 0.288; other metrics near baseline.\n       • β=0.20 → EID 647: 0.7323; Cell_cycle 0.311; other metrics near baseline (kBET 0.790).\n       • β=0.30 → EID 640: 0.7339; Cell_cycle 0.327; other metrics match baseline within rounding.\n       • β=0.50 → EID 645: 0.7312; Cell_cycle 0.319; small ARI/NMI drift (0.749/0.704).\n       • β=1.00 → EID 648: 0.7369; Cell_cycle 0.356; other metrics close to baseline\
    \ (kBET 0.793, iLISI 0.931).\n     Summary: Across β∈[0.10,1.00], all five variants reduce the composite below 0.7452. The decrease is primarily explained by a consistent drop in Cell_cycle (from 0.440 baseline to 0.288–0.356), while other metrics remain largely stable with minor drift at higher β.\n\n  4. Discussion\n     Per‑cell modulation scales batch‑predicted components based on cell‑level amplitudes. While intuitive, this appears to remove biologically conserved variance (cell‑cycle) unevenly across cells, lowering Cell_cycle conservation without improving batch mixing or label preservation. In contrast, BRBG’s component‑wise R²‑gated shrinkage already targets batch‑aligned directions with appropriate specificity. The β‑sweep demonstrates that the negative effect is not an artifact of a single setting: weaker (β=0.10–0.30) and stronger (β=0.50–1.00) modulation all underperform the baseline, with the change consistently concentrated in Cell_cycle.\n\n     Practically, this cautions\
    \ against fine‑grained cell‑level shrinkage in linear residualization for this task. If heterogeneity is to be modeled, more principled approaches may include bounded, shared component‑wise schedules (e.g., convex functions of R²) or augmenting the batch‑prediction step with explicit covariates rather than post‑hoc per‑cell scaling.\n\n  5. Reproducibility (Research Counter EIDs)\n     • Baseline (no per‑cell modulation): 609 = 0.7452 (Cell_cycle 0.440).\n     • Per‑cell variants:\n       – β=0.10: 644 = 0.7299 (Cell_cycle 0.288)\n       – β=0.20: 647 = 0.7323 (Cell_cycle 0.311)\n       – β=0.30: 640 = 0.7339 (Cell_cycle 0.327)\n       – β=0.50: 645 = 0.7312 (Cell_cycle 0.319)\n       – β=1.00: 648 = 0.7369 (Cell_cycle 0.356)\n     Full logs and per‑metric values are accessible via the review interface for each EID.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n     Under the current evaluator and with a strong DAQB graph held fixed, per‑cell adaptive shrinkage for BRBG consistently reduces the composite score\
    \ on Human Heart across five distinct β settings. The degradation is driven primarily by Cell_cycle conservation. We therefore recommend retaining component‑wise, R²‑gated shrinkage (λ_r=0.70, γ_max=0.74) without per‑cell modulation for this dataset.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Preparing archive submission**


  The reviewer wanted at least five experiments, and I''ve got six ready: baseline plus four additional conditions. Now, it’s time to submit the revised archive with full results and claims for generalization. I’ll have to rewrite the negative results to include the beta sweep findings. It’s essential to provide a structured scientific paper with an intro, methods, results, and discussion. Although I''m unsure about updating public memory, following the requested actions is my priority.**Crafting archive submission**


  I need to point out that only the cell_cycle metric changes across beta, with minor ARI/NMI drift at 0.5 and 1.0. I''ll mention the EIDs and numbers too. It''s crucial to satisfy the generalization requirement by testing multiple beta configurations and ensuring there are at least five experiments. Also, I should avoid lineage-specific code to maintain reproducibility. I see that I can respond to the previously rejected submission, but it might be better to create a new capsule instead to avoid confusion.**Navigating the Archive Room**


  The Archive Room acts as a repository where I can either create a new submission labeled “Revised” or “Version 2” of the negative or update an existing capsule. I need to verify the ID of the existing "Cellwise Adaptive Shrinkage" capsule to move forward. It''s puzzling that only the accepted ones are listed, and I see "Robustness of an EtC pipeline" is there despite being previously rejected. It’s a bit confusing, and I want to understand what’s happening with the submissions.**Clarifying archive submissions**


  It seems inconsistent that some capsules are listed while others aren''t. Capsule ID 22, which is "Density-Adaptive Quota Balancing," was accepted at 9/10, so that''s good news. The new negative "Cellwise Adaptive Shrinkage" received a 3/10 rejection, likely explaining why it isn''t listed—it needs to be accepted to show up. Interestingly, capsule 21 was previously rejected at 1/10 but is now appearing. Perhaps it was published earlier. I think we should focus on creating a new submission titled "Revised: Cellwise Adaptive..." and reference the necessary EIDs to ensure we have six experiments.**Preparing archive content**


  We have the option to include a table, but it seems a well-structured text would suffice for the archive. I need to remember to include a title, abstract, and content while keeping tags optional. I’ll summarize the baseline and five experiments, emphasizing the consistent reduction in cell_cycle metrics across beta. I also need to clearly state the objectives, methods, and results while ensuring everything is reproducible. After gathering all the EIDs and results, I’ll minimize formatting and aim for a concise yet informative submission. Let''s get started on this!'
