role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write praxis/brbg_augmented.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from praxis.bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n\n  def _to_dense_row_sum(X):\n      if sp.issparse(X):\n          return np.array(X.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      return X.sum(axis=1).astype(np.float64, copy=False)\n\n  def _fraction_zeros(X):\n      if sp.issparse(X):\n          n = X.shape[1]\n          nnz = np.array(X.getnnz(axis=1)).ravel()\n          return 1.0 - (nnz / np.maximum(n, 1))\n      else:\n          return (X == 0).mean(axis=1).astype(np.float64, copy=False)\n\n  def _zscore(v, eps=1e-8):\n      v = np.asarray(v, dtype=np.float32)\n      return (v - v.mean()) / (v.std() + eps)\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n\
    \      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B, cats\n\n  def _ridge_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def _build_design(batches, cov1, cov2, mode: str = 'interactions'):\n      \"\"\"\n      modes:\n        - 'interactions': [B, B*cov1, B*cov2] (batch-specific covariate effects)\n \
    \       - 'main': [B, cov1, cov2] (global covariates only, no interactions)\n      \"\"\"\n      B_onehot, _ = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      n = B_onehot.shape[0]\n      cov1 = cov1.reshape(n, 1).astype(np.float32)\n      cov2 = cov2.reshape(n, 1).astype(np.float32)\n      if mode == 'interactions':\n          B_cov1 = B_onehot * cov1\n          B_cov2 = B_onehot * cov2\n          return np.concatenate([B_onehot, B_cov1, B_cov2], axis=1).astype(np.float32)\n      elif mode == 'main':\n          return np.concatenate([B_onehot, cov1, cov2], axis=1).astype(np.float32)\n      else:\n          raise ValueError(f\"Unknown mode: {mode}\")\n\n  def eliminate_augmented(adata: ad.AnnData,\n                          mode: str = 'interactions',\n                          l2: float = 1e-3,\n                          lam: float = 0.7,\n                          gamma_max: float = 0.74,\n                          delta: float = 0.15,\n                          k_density: int = 20,\n           \
    \               k_total: int = 48,\n                          metric: str = 'cosine',\n                          n_pcs_emb: int = 60,\n                          n_pcs_graph: int = 50,\n                          alpha_var: float = 0.5) -> ad.AnnData:\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Covariates from RAW COUNTS\n      C = adata.layers['counts'] if 'counts' in adata.layers else adata.X\n      total_counts = _to_dense_row_sum(C)\n      frac_zeros = _fraction_zeros(C)\n      cov1 = _zscore(np.log1p(total_counts))\n      cov2 = _zscore(frac_zeros)\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # EtC embedding (analysis only)\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd =\
    \ (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=15, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph representation\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0).astype(np.float32)\n\n      # Augmented design and prediction\n      B_aug = _build_design(batches, cov1, cov2, mode=mode)\n      Zhat = _ridge_fit_predict(B_aug, Zg, l2=l2).astype(np.float32)\n\n      # Per-component shrinkage (component-wise only)\n      var_total\
    \ = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0).astype(np.float32)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2).astype(np.float32)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma[None, :])\n\n      # DAQB graph\n      Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n                                           k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                           delta=delta, k_density=k_density, rng_seed=0)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n\n  # Default alias keeps backward compatibility with prior submission\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      return eliminate_augmented(adata, mode='interactions', l2=1e-3)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BRBG-AR(main-only): Global covariates without interactions + DAQB\"\ntags: \"augmented-design,\
    \ covariates, main-effects, residualization, bbsg\"\nabstract: \"Isolate augmented design effect without batch interactions: design = [B, log_total_counts, frac_zeros]; ridge l2=1e-3; residualization schedule and DAQB unchanged (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74; δ=0.15, k_density=20; k=48; binary edges + true distances). Hypothesis: main covariates avoid Cell_cycle loss seen with interactions.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  import anndata as ad\n  from praxis.brbg_augmented import eliminate_augmented\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      return eliminate_augmented(adata, mode='main', l2=1e-3)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BRBG-AR(interactions, strong ridge): l2=1e-2 + DAQB\"\ntags: \"augmented-design, batch-interactions, strong-regularization, residualization, bbsg\"\nabstract: \"Test stronger regularization for batch×covariate interactions: design = [B, B×log_total_counts, B×frac_zeros], ridge l2=1e-2. Residualization\
    \ schedule and DAQB unchanged. Hypothesis: heavier ridge curbs biology removal (Cell_cycle) while retaining mixing.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  import anndata as ad\n  from praxis.brbg_augmented import eliminate_augmented\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      return eliminate_augmented(adata, mode='interactions', l2=1e-2)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating experimental design**\n\nThe reviewer suggested an augmented design, and we tried one variant, but the result was negative. I realize we would need to conduct five experiments to publish any findings. It's important to record the negative result along with suggestions for further research. Doing that will help clarify our path moving forward, plus it could provide valuable insights for others. It feels like a lot of effort, but it's necessary for thoroughness!**Planning experiments**\n\nFor now, I can log the negative result in Private Memory with EID 650 and record the metrics. I need to plan a minimal ablation suite to test different covariate sets and penalty strength l2, including its effect on the gamma schedule. I’ll create a small grid for ridge in the augmented design, adjusting l2 values and possibly dropping interactions. I aim to maintain parsimony by testing three variants plus a baseline. It’s essential to be mindful of token usage given current\
  \ limits.**Proposing new experiments**\n\nI can propose two more experiments right now. \n\nFirst, there's AR-MainOnly, which adds covariates as columns without any interactions, like [B, cov1, cov2]. \n\nThen, there's AR-InteractionsStrongRidge that includes interactions with a stronger ridge, specifically setting l2 to 1e-2. \n\nAlso, AR-InteractionsGammaGentle would adjust the lambda residualization factor to protect biology while keeping l2 constant. However, I want to ensure we maintain parsimony by changing one aspect at a time. I’ll submit these plans accordingly!**Updating module functions**\n\nTo make things easier, I think I'll create wrappers that call a function with parameters. The module didn't expose the parameterization we need, so I can replicate the code with a selected design. I’ll also update the storage to include a function, eliminate_augmented(mode='main', l2=1e-3), for design building. \n\nWhen updating storage/praxis/brbg_augmented.py, I want to include both a\
  \ parameterized function and a default function. However, since I just wrote a file, I need to be careful about overwriting existing content to avoid confusion.**Crafting submission plans**\n\nI’m looking at the residualization target Zg shape, which is n×p. For the design matrix, I can include global covariates. I’m planning to create a function called eliminate_augmented(adata, mode='interactions', l2=1e-3) to handle different modes. I want to prepare two submissions: \n\n1) The first will test global covariates without interactions.\n2) The second will focus on adding stronger ridge when interactions are included. \n\nAfter that, I think I'll summarize everything in private memory and be careful with the code organization to ensure clarity! I’ll also take care to write actions in a clean format."
