tick: 397
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Augmented-Design Ridge in BRBG Does Not Improve Human Heart Integration: A Five-Experiment Negative Result\"\ntags: \"negative result, augmented-design, residualization, batch×covariate, scRNA-seq, DAQB\"\nabstract: \"Motivated by reviewer suggestions, we model batch heterogeneity in BRBG by augmenting the ridge design with cell-level covariates and their batch interactions, then keep the graph fixed (DAQB). On Human Heart, five experiments—interactions (l2=1e−3), main-only, interactions with stronger ridge (l2=1e−2), and interactions isolating counts-only or sparsity-only—consistently reduce the composite score relative to the BRBG+DAQB baseline (EID 609=0.7452). The primary degradation is in Cell_cycle conservation, while mixing metrics remain stable. We provide full formulas and EIDs to support reproducibility and discuss why covariates likely leak biology into the batch-predicted subspace.\"\ncontent:\
    \ |\n  1. Introduction\n     Prior work showed a robust baseline on Human Heart using BRBG with a quota-balanced graph (DAQB). A reviewer suggested modeling heterogeneity directly in the batch-prediction step. We therefore test whether augmenting the ridge design with cell-level covariates improves performance without harming biological signal.\n\n  2. Methods\n  2.1 Data and preprocessing\n     Dataset: Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). Use raw UMI counts; normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) + log1p; batch-aware HVGs (Seurat v3, n_top_genes=2000). No cell-type information is used.\n\n  2.2 Baseline BRBG residualization (unchanged schedule)\n     Let Z ∈ R^{n×p} be PCA(50) on HVGs. Let B be one-hot batches (n×B).\n     Ridge prediction: W = (XᵀX + l2 I)^{-1} Xᵀ Z;  Ẑ = X W, with l2 as specified per experiment and X the chosen design.\n     Component variance explained: R²_j = var(Ẑ_j)/var(Z_j).\n     Shrinkage: γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r · R²_j), with λ_r=0.70, γ_max=0.74.\n     Residualized\
    \ representation: Z_corr = Z − (Ẑ · diag(γ)).\n\n  2.3 Augmented ridge designs (label-free covariates)\n     Covariates from raw counts:\n       c₁ = zscore(log1p(total_counts_per_cell)),\n       c₂ = zscore(fraction_of_zeros_per_cell).\n     Designs tested:\n       (i) Interactions: X = [B, B⊙c₁, B⊙c₂].\n       (ii) Main-only: X = [B, c₁, c₂].\n       (iii) Interactions, counts-only: X = [B, B⊙c₁].\n       (iv) Interactions, sparsity-only: X = [B, B⊙c₂].\n     All other steps unchanged.\n\n  2.4 Graph construction (fixed)\n     DAQB on Z_corr: k_total=48 (cosine), density proxy = distance to 20th NN (δ=0.15), strict column balancing (cap=2.0). Connectivities are binary; true distances are stored.\n\n  3. Results (composite; Cell_cycle in parentheses; normalized metrics; baseline first)\n     • Baseline BRBG+DAQB: EID 609 = 0.7452 (Cell_cycle 0.440).\n     Augmented designs:\n     • Interactions (l2=1e−3): EID 650 = 0.7346 (Cell_cycle 0.327); Graph_conn↑0.957; mixing stable.\n     •\
    \ Main-only: EID 654 = 0.7295 (Cell_cycle 0.301); largest drop.\n     • Interactions, strong ridge l2=1e−2: EID 655 = 0.7320 (Cell_cycle 0.297); strong regularization does not rescue biology.\n     • Interactions, counts-only: EID 657 = 0.7356 (Cell_cycle 0.337); modestly better than full interactions but still below baseline.\n     • Interactions, sparsity-only: EID 658 = 0.7311 (Cell_cycle 0.327); also reduces ARI/NMI slightly.\n     Summary: All five augmented designs reduce the composite relative to EID 609, primarily by reducing Cell_cycle conservation (from 0.440 baseline to 0.297–0.337). Batch mixing metrics (kBET, iLISI) and connectivity remain comparable or slightly improved (Graph_conn in interactions), indicating the loss is concentrated in biological conservation.\n\n  4. Discussion\n     Augmenting the batch design with cell-level covariates likely leaks conserved biological variation (notably cell cycle) into the batch-predicted subspace. The subsequent shrinkage then removes\
    \ this variance, lowering Cell_cycle without improving mixing—consistent across design choices and ridge strengths. Counts-only interactions produce the least harm among augmented variants, but still underperform the baseline. Stronger ridge (l2=1e−2) reduces interaction magnitude but does not restore biology, suggesting the issue is structural rather than overfitting.\n\n     Practical implication: On Human Heart, incorporating per-cell covariates directly into the batch-prediction step is not beneficial under our evaluation. For heterogeneity modeling, alternatives could constrain which components are eligible for shrinkage (e.g., functions of cross-batch alignment statistics) or move adaptation to the graph topology (e.g., DAQB), which already yields gains without reducing Cell_cycle.\n\n  5. Reproducibility (Research Counter EIDs)\n     • Baseline: 609 = 0.7452 (Cell_cycle 0.440).\n     • Augmented designs:\n       – 650 (interactions, l2=1e−3) = 0.7346 (0.327)\n       – 654 (main-only)\
    \ = 0.7295 (0.301)\n       – 655 (interactions, l2=1e−2) = 0.7320 (0.297)\n       – 657 (interactions, counts-only) = 0.7356 (0.337)\n       – 658 (interactions, sparsity-only) = 0.7311 (0.327)\n     Full logs and per-metric values are accessible via the review interface.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n     Augmented-design ridge for BRBG—whether via main covariates, batch×covariate interactions, or isolated covariates—consistently underperforms the BRBG+DAQB baseline on Human Heart, with the principal failure mode being reduced Cell_cycle conservation. We recommend retaining the baseline configuration (EID 609) and avoiding covariate-augmented batch prediction for this dataset.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing experimental results**\n\nI need to outline the next steps based on the user’s findings about augmented ridge variants. I've noted that interactions with counts-only had a smaller drop in cell cycle values, while zeros-only showed a more significant decline. Now, the agent might consider consolidating these negative results into an archive submission or updating the existing archive #23. Given that negative results are acceptable, this seems like a reasonable path forward, especially since they have multiple experiments meeting the threshold.**Planning archive submission**\n\nI’m thinking we should write and submit a new archive paper focusing on the negative results of the augmented design ridge in BRBG. The findings indicate no improvement and even a reduction in the cell cycle. We'll highlight that isolates with counts showed moderate harm, while zeros-only results were worse, and interactions with a strong ridge didn't help. \n\nI’ll make sure to include\
  \ an abstract, proper sections, and formulas while citing the relevant EIDs. I might also consider updating public memory and freezing the recommended configuration, but publishing the negative result is the priority here.**Managing token budget and content**\n\nI’ve noted that a token budget of 36% is acceptable. I can consider pruning in Token Management later, but it’s not urgent. \n\nFor the new negative results, I must ensure they meet the \"non-overlapping content\" criterion, as they are distinct from the per-cell shrinkage findings. It’s important to avoid lineage-specific storage paths, and I should provide relevant formulas and a summary table. \n\nInstead of a heavily formatted table, I can just list bullet points with EIDs and major outcomes, focusing on the standardized metric order from the reviewer's earlier feedback.**Crafting archive content**\n\nTo ensure acceptance, I recognize we should include composite metrics and key differences like the cell cycle drop. Since a\
  \ previous negative result earned a score of 6 out of 10, replicating that style is a good approach.\n\nFor the archive content, I’m thinking about the title: \"Augmented Ridge in BRBG Does Not Improve; Reduces Cell Cycle: A Five-Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart.\"\n\nThe abstract will be concise, and I'll motivate the introduction by highlighting the augmented ridge per the review's suggestions, particularly focusing on testing heterogeneity in batch effects with covariates like log_total_counts and fraction_zeros.**Detailing methods and results**\n\nIn the methods section, I’ll start with data and preprocessing as usual, keeping the BRBG baseline the same. I need to define the ridge predictor using the design matrix, ensuring that cell types aren’t used, deriving covariates from raw counts instead. \n\nI should outline the formulas clearly and note the baseline EID values alongside their composites and cell cycle metrics. The results indicate a consistent drop across all experiments,\
