tick: 403
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DAQB v2: Unbiased quota rounding + reallocation before cap-break (lam=0.7, δ=0.15, k_density=20)\"\ntags: \"bbsg, density-adaptive, quota-reallocation, residualization, embed-then-correct\"\nabstract: \"We refine density-adaptive quota balancing (DAQB) by (1) unbiased stochastic rounding of per-cell cross-batch quotas to reduce discretization bias and (2) reallocation of unfilled cross-batch quotas across other batches before any cap-break fallback. Graph remains binary with true distances; BRBG unchanged (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50). Hypothesis: improved alignment of realized mixing to target f_cross with maintained balancing.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  # -------- Helpers --------\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata:\
    \ ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(rows, cols, dists,\
    \ n):\n      A = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D = sp.coo_matrix((dists.astype(np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      A_sym = A.maximum(A.T)\n      D_sym = D.maximum(D.T)\n      A_sym.eliminate_zeros(); D_sym.eliminate_zeros()\n      return A_sym, D_sym\n\n  # -------- DAQB v2 builder --------\n\n  def build_daqb_v2(\n      Zcorr: np.ndarray,\n      batches,\n      k_total: int = 48,\n      metric: str = 'cosine',\n      delta: float = 0.15,\n      k_density: int = 20,\n      f_bounds=(0.60, 0.90),\n      cap: float = 2.0,\n      rng_seed: int = 0\n  ):\n      \"\"\"\n      Improvements over DAQB (EID 609):\n        - Unbiased stochastic rounding for per-cell cross-batch quotas q_cross_total (deterministic via rng_seed).\n        - If a target batch j cannot fill its quota due to cap/candidates, reallocate leftover across other batches\n          (closest-first with available capacity) BEFORE any cap-breaking\
    \ fallback.\n        - Binary edges; true distances; column balancing enforced via inbound-first selection.\n      \"\"\"\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(rng_seed)\n      Z = np.asarray(Zcorr, dtype=np.float32, order='C')\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n_batches = len(cats)\n      base_cross = (n_batches - 1) / float(n_batches)\n      L, U = f_bounds\n\n      # Density proxy: distance to k_density-th NN (all batches)\n      nn_all = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_density + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute')\n      nn_all.fit(Z)\n      d_all, _ = nn_all.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      d_k = d_all[:, -1]  # kth neighbor; larger => sparser\n      d_min, d_max = float(np.min(d_k)), float(np.max(d_k) + 1e-8)\n      d_norm = (d_k - d_min) / (d_max - d_min + 1e-8)  # 0 dense ... 1 sparse\n      inv_dense = 1.0 - d_norm\n      f_cross = np.clip(base_cross + (inv_dense - 0.5) * 2.0 * delta, L, U)  # per-cell target\n\
    \n      # Unbiased stochastic rounding for per-cell cross quota\n      q_cross_float = f_cross * k_total\n      q_cross_floor = np.floor(q_cross_float).astype(int)\n      frac = q_cross_float - q_cross_floor\n      extra = (rng.random(n) < frac).astype(int)\n      q_cross_tot = q_cross_floor + extra\n      q_within_tot = k_total - q_cross_tot\n\n      # per-batch neighbor models (for within/cross queries)\n      cat_to_code = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(cats)}\n      batch_codes = np.array([cat_to_code[b] for b in batches], dtype=int)\n      B_other = max(n_batches - 1, 1)\n      k_within_max = int(np.ceil((1.0 - L) * k_total))\n      k_cross_per_max = int(np.ceil(U * k_total / B_other))\n      k_max = max(k_within_max, k_cross_per_max) + 8  # buffer; +1 self skip handled in code\n\n      nn_models, train_indices = {}, {}\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          mask = (batches == c)\n          train_indices[j] = np.where(mask)[0]\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_max,\
    \ metric=metric, algorithm='brute')\n          nn.fit(Z[mask])\n          nn_models[j] = nn\n\n      dists, idx_local = {}, {}\n      for j in range(n_batches):\n          d, ii = nn_models[j].kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n          dists[j] = d\n          idx_local[j] = ii\n\n      inbound = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.int32)\n      cap_thr = int(np.ceil(cap * k_total))\n      rows, cols, dvals = [], [], []\n\n      for i in range(n):\n          bi = batch_codes[i]\n          q_within = int(q_within_tot[i])\n          q_cross_total = int(q_cross_tot[i])\n\n          # Order other batches by closeness (smaller first-neighbor distance = closer)\n          other_batches = [j for j in range(n_batches) if j != bi]\n          batch_scores = [(j, dists[j][i][0]) for j in other_batches]\n          batch_scores.sort(key=lambda t: t[1])\n          ordered_others = [j for j, _ in batch_scores]\n\n          # Even split + remainder to closest batches\n          q_per_other = q_cross_total // B_other\n\
    \          rem = q_cross_total - q_per_other * B_other\n          q_map = {j: q_per_other for j in other_batches}\n          for k in range(rem):\n              q_map[ordered_others[k % len(ordered_others)]] += 1\n\n          # Helper to select neighbors from a candidate list with inbound-first, then distance\n          def select_from_candidates(global_ids, distances, need, allow_cap_break=False):\n              taken = 0\n              # order by (inbound, distance)\n              order = np.lexsort((distances, inbound[global_ids]))\n              # first pass: respect cap\n              for idx in order:\n                  g = int(global_ids[idx])\n                  if not allow_cap_break and inbound[g] >= cap_thr:\n                      continue\n                  rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(distances[idx]))\n                  inbound[g] += 1\n                  taken += 1\n                  if taken >= need: break\n              return taken\n\n          #\
    \ Within-batch selection (respect cap; if insufficient, re-try with cap-break as last resort)\n          d_w = dists[bi][i]; ii_w = idx_local[bi][i]\n          start_w = 1 if d_w[0] == 0.0 else 0\n          globals_w = train_indices[bi][ii_w[start_w:]]\n          cand_dist_w = d_w[start_w:]\n          taken_w = select_from_candidates(globals_w, cand_dist_w, q_within, allow_cap_break=False)\n          if taken_w < q_within:\n              # last resort to meet degree\n              rem_need = q_within - taken_w\n              select_from_candidates(globals_w, cand_dist_w, rem_need, allow_cap_break=True)\n\n          # Cross-batch selection with reallocation BEFORE any cap break\n          # First pass: try each batch j respecting cap\n          leftovers = 0\n          per_batch_positions = {}  # keep positions for possible second-pass fetch\n          for j in ordered_others:\n              need_j = q_map[j]\n              if need_j <= 0: \n                  per_batch_positions[j] =\
    \ 0\n                  continue\n              d_c = dists[j][i]; ii_c = idx_local[j][i]\n              globals_c = train_indices[j][ii_c]\n              cand_dist_c = d_c\n              taken_j = 0\n              # sorted indices by inbound then distance\n              order = np.lexsort((cand_dist_c, inbound[globals_c]))\n              per_batch_positions[j] = 0\n              for idx in order:\n                  g = int(globals_c[idx])\n                  if inbound[g] >= cap_thr:\n                      continue\n                  rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dist_c[idx]))\n                  inbound[g] += 1\n                  taken_j += 1\n                  per_batch_positions[j] = per_batch_positions[j] + 1\n                  if taken_j >= need_j: break\n              if taken_j < need_j:\n                  leftovers += (need_j - taken_j)\n\n          # Reallocate leftovers across other batches with capacity (still respecting cap)\n          if leftovers\
    \ > 0:\n              for j in ordered_others:\n                  if leftovers <= 0: break\n                  d_c = dists[j][i]; ii_c = idx_local[j][i]\n                  globals_c = train_indices[j][ii_c]\n                  cand_dist_c = d_c\n                  # continue from where we left off; scan further candidates\n                  order = np.lexsort((cand_dist_c, inbound[globals_c]))\n                  # pick beyond already considered ones\n                  taken_extra = 0\n                  for idx in order:\n                      g = int(globals_c[idx])\n                      if inbound[g] >= cap_thr:\n                          continue\n                      rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dist_c[idx]))\n                      inbound[g] += 1\n                      taken_extra += 1\n                      leftovers -= 1\n                      if leftovers <= 0:\n                          break\n\n          # If still short of total degree (rare), allow\
    \ cap-break as last resort across closest batches\n          deg_now = np.count_nonzero(np.array(rows[-(k_total+10):]) == i)  # heuristic window\n          # To ensure exactly k_total edges, we count edges for i explicitly:\n          # (More robust re-count)\n          # Build count by scanning recent additions for i\n          ptr = len(rows) - 1\n          count_i = 0\n          while ptr >= 0 and rows[ptr] == i:\n              count_i += 1\n              ptr -= 1\n          if count_i < k_total:\n              need_final = k_total - count_i\n              for j in ordered_others:\n                  if need_final <= 0: break\n                  d_c = dists[j][i]; ii_c = idx_local[j][i]\n                  globals_c = train_indices[j][ii_c]\n                  cand_dist_c = d_c\n                  # order by distance only (allow cap break)\n                  order = np.argsort(cand_dist_c)\n                  for idx in order:\n                      g = int(globals_c[idx])\n           \
    \           rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dist_c[idx]))\n                      inbound[g] += 1\n                      need_final -= 1\n                      if need_final <= 0:\n                          break\n\n      A_sym, D_sym = _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(\n          np.asarray(rows, dtype=np.int32),\n          np.asarray(cols, dtype=np.int32),\n          np.asarray(dvals, dtype=np.float32),\n          n\n      )\n      return A_sym.tocsr().astype(np.float32), D_sym.tocsr().astype(np.float32)\n\n  # -------- Main pipeline (EtC + BRBG + DAQB v2 graph) --------\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config (as in EID 609 baseline; DAQB v2 refinement only in graph builder)\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.7\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      delta = 0.15\n      k_density = 20\n      bounds = (0.60, 0.90)\n      alpha_var = 0.5\n\
    \n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # EtC embedding (analysis/QC only, kept for parity with prior submissions)\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n\
    \      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n\n      # Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization → Zcorr\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      # DAQB v2 graph\n      Cg, Dg = build_daqb_v2(\n          Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n          delta=delta, k_density=k_density, f_bounds=bounds, cap=2.0, rng_seed=0\n      )\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating next steps**\n\nI need to decide on the best next steps now that we're in the Praxis II state. We've made some progress, achieving a score of 0.7452 with the density-adaptive quota balancing technique. We've also published papers and shared our diagnostics module. Since the user hasn't asked any specific questions yet, I think we can either plan further validation or conduct additional experiments. Our token usage looks good right now, so pruning isn't necessary. We've got time to present the next steps!**Exploring dataset options**\n\nTypically, I'm dealing with just one dataset available. Right now, cross-organ data isn't an option for evaluation. I'm considering proposing generalization through out-of-sample cross-validation with bootstraps. Alternatively, I could suggest a \"label-free auto-parameter selection\" using our data to set δ and k_density values. However, I did find that global-budget proxies didn’t perform well. Another concept could be a \"\
  two-phase neighbor allocation\" to manage local cross-batch mixing while addressing sampling bias without relying on labels. There are various ideas to explore!**Considering graph edge variables**\n\nThe builder anticipates exactly k_total edges per cell, while the evaluator focuses more on connectivity and distances. Historically, the pipeline has required a constant k_total, but it's not strictly mandated. I wonder if varying degrees might decrease graph connectivity. One option could be \"edge pooling by per-cell margin,\" despite previous margin-adaptive quotas not performing well. Another idea is using \"two-scale neighbor candidate sets\" by preselecting candidates based on metrics like cosine and Euclidean distances. I need to ensure that any distances used are compatible with metrics like iLISI. So many considerations to balance!**Examining weighted distances**\n\nI’m considering whether weighted distances really matter here. Previously, replacing binary edges with Gaussian weights\
  \ altered connectivities to weighted connectivities, which caused iLISI to collapse. I need to remember that while connectivities were changed, the distances provided truthful data. It looks like the OpenProblems pipeline relies heavily on distances. One alternative could be \"quota smoothing across batches\" to address variable sizes since real datasets often have unequal batches. Allocating cross-batch quotas proportionally could enhance fairness, and I think mild improvements may still come from per-batch prior weighting. So many factors to balance!**Planning implementation improvements**\n\nTo maintain SOTA while avoiding random micro-tuning, I could draft a technical note for the Archive on fairness diagnostics or finalize improvements. Since we’re in a mature environment, publishing every 100 ticks seems vital; we’ve already accepted #22 and #23. One idea is a \"unified code release\" for replicability. I’m also considering \"Monte Carlo randomization of quotas\" to enhance correlation\
  \ between target f_cross and realized mixing, plus stochastic rounding for better alignment. Adjusting the handling of inbound cap fallback could further enhance fairness. I have lots of details to implement!**Preparing for submission**\n\nI'm looking at the previous edge-case with many loops where the algorithm runs in O(n * B * k log k) but still handles 20k cells within a reasonable time. I'll draft the submission code while ensuring it's memory efficient and ready for the Archive update. \n\nI plan to use helper functions for key processes, including distance calculations and quota rounding. There’s an emphasis on unbiased rounding and reallocation approaches. I’ll also ensure to track neighbor selections appropriately to avoid duplicates. Once complete, I’ll submit the improved algorithm and address any follow-ups based on results.**Implementing performance memory**\n\nI'm checking how to compute performance memory for neighbor queries, aiming for manageable array shapes like 20k\
  \ x 50, totaling around 4 million. I'll build per-batch models and pre-extract training indices for mapping. \n\nFor each item, I’ll determine `q_within` and gather neighbor candidates, skipping the unnecessary \"embedding neighbors\" step to save time and compute. Evaluation should focus on connectivities, so including embedding seems modestly necessary.\n\nI'll finalize the code with emphasis on unbiased rounding and implement leftover reallocation to optimize our approach. Let's do this!"
