parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{rank author}`\n`/execute_action{preview 669}`\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 12}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update @tick404: Submitted DAQB v2 (EID 669) — unbiased stochastic rounding of per-cell cross-batch quotas + pre-cap reallocation across batches; other pipeline parts unchanged (λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, δ=0.15, k_density=20, k=48, cosine).\n  Plan:\n  - If 669 ≥ 0.7452 (EID 609), freeze v2 as new rec and publish Archive addendum with diagnostics and rationale.\n  - If neutral/negative, retain 609 as recommended; document that quota discretization alignment did not translate to score gains.\n  - Regardless, keep diagnostics shared (storage/shared/diagnostics/daqb_diagnostics.py) and add size-normalized inbound analysis in a later patch.\n  - Next strategic step: cross-organ evaluation when available; otherwise theory note on quota discreteness vs. cap-induced competition (why corr(f_th,f_rl) ≈ 0.43 with 15% nodes at cap).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nI need to respond to the current situation regarding the user's DAQB v2 evaluation submission. It seems they want to plan their next steps, possibly publishing or running diagnostics. The station has given an automatic tip on novel ideas, so I should coordinate actions and provide brief commentary as needed. For now, we should wait for the evaluation results, which won’t come immediately, while potentially preparing an Archive update or planning a cross-organ validation with the available Human Heart dataset. Opening Public Memory to share our plan could also be beneficial.**Managing evaluation tasks**\n\nI can implement a version with deterministic randomized rounding using a stable RNG seed. While the evaluation runs, I should tidy storage by committing modules for community use, even though diagnostics are already done. I could create a minimal runner that builds DAQB v2 as a library module in storage for reusability. It's also an option to\
  \ set the rank author to view submissions and preview EID 669, although it’s not finished yet. I'll wait patiently to avoid spamming and consider logging the plan in Private Memory instead.**Managing tokens and potential pruning**\n\nI should consider going to Token Management to prune older ticks and lower token usage. However, there's a cooldown in place after the last prune at tick 402, which states there's another cooldown for 4 ticks. Now that we're at tick 404, I'm unsure if we can prune just yet. Interestingly, we didn't prune at tick 402, instead focusing on updates. I might try to prune at tick 401 since it seems not protected. The last message suggests reflecting on our approach, so while we can proceed, we might also explore testing adaptive caps or inbound-smoothing, but I must strategize carefully.**Assessing station status**\n\nThe station status is healthy, and we've already matured and published accepted Archive #22 and #23. It seems best to wait for the evaluation results\
  \ before taking further steps, like running the /execute_action for ranking the author and reviewing research logs. I might also consider using a smaller page size to see our information more clearly. Additionally, I can open Private Memory to draft a plan based on the evaluation outcome or implement a code improvement for inbound share, though that may not be critically necessary at this point.**Planning actions and recording strategy**\n\nI’m planning to rank the author and set the page size to 20. Unfortunately, I can’t preview EID 669 since previews are only applicable for submissions with evaluation IDs. However, I could use the action to check the status, perhaps finding it \"running.\"  \n\nI'll execute the ranking and preview actions, then move to Private Memory to jot down my next steps, keeping it concise. I need to handle special characters in YAML correctly, particularly any colons in titles. Ultimately, I’ll either create a new capsule or reply to the existing #12 pinned analysis\
  \ with the plan for DAQB v2 evaluation, considering reproducibility and library release as well. Let’s get to it!"
