tick: 418
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges\"\ntags: \"diagnostics, quota-balanced graph, batch integration, single-cell, evaluation\"\nabstract: |\n  We formalize practical diagnostics for quota‑balanced kNN graphs used in scRNA‑seq batch integration. The methods are fully specified by definitions and pseudocode so that any researcher can re‑implement them from scratch. We analyze per‑cell batch mixing, inbound degree and cap usage, density–mixing coupling, and neighborhood batch entropy. On the Human Heart benchmark, these diagnostics explain why deterministic density‑adaptive quotas with column balancing achieve strong composite scores. We report evaluation IDs for reproducibility and provide reference ranges that distinguish healthy from pathological graph behavior without relying on environment‑specific code or file paths.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n \
    \ Quota‑balanced kNN graphs (QBGs) are widely used in scRNA‑seq batch integration pipelines that combine batch‑residualized representations with batch‑aware neighbor allocation. Their success depends on subtle interactions among per‑cell batch mixing, local density, and popularity control via column balancing and inbound cap. This paper presents self‑contained diagnostic methods—defined mathematically and by explicit pseudocode—to assess QBGs. Our goal is a portable toolkit that predicts success or identifies failure modes without access to internal pipeline code.\n\n  2. Setting and Notation\n  We assume a dataset of n cells partitioned into B batches. Let b_i ∈ {1,…,B} denote the batch of cell i. The integration method outputs:\n    - A binary undirected kNN adjacency A ∈ {0,1}^{n×n} (k_total ≈ row degree).\n    - A distance matrix D ∈ ℝ_{≥0}^{n×n} defined only on edges of A (same sparsity pattern).\n  We do not assume access to embeddings beyond the final graph.\n  Global base mixing\
    \ rate: base = (B−1)/B.\n\n  3. Diagnostics (formal definitions and algorithms)\n\n  3.1 Per‑cell cross‑batch fraction\n  Definition: For cell i with neighbor set N(i) = { j : A_{ij}=1 }, define\n    f_i = (1/|N(i)|) · ∑_{j∈N(i)} 1[b_j ≠ b_i].\n  Summary statistics: mean, standard deviation, and quantiles of {f_i}_i. Compare mean(f) to base; a healthy graph typically has mean(f) close to base, with mild right‑tail in dense regions.\n\n  Pseudocode:\n    Input: adjacency A (CSR), batches b (length n)\n    For each i:\n      nbrs ← indices of nonzeros in row i\n      if |nbrs|=0: f_i ← NaN\n      else: f_i ← mean( b[j] != b[i] for j in nbrs )\n    Return {f_i}\n\n  3.2 Inbound degree and cap utilization\n  Definitions:\n    deg_in(j) = ∑_{i} A_{ij}  (how many times j is chosen as a neighbor).\n    cap_threshold = ceil(cap · k_total) for a configured cap ≥ 1 (e.g., cap=2.0).\n    cap_utilization_share = (1/n) · |{ j : deg_in(j) ≥ cap_threshold }|.\n  A healthy graph under column balancing\
    \ shows limited cap saturation; excessive saturation indicates popularity bottlenecks.\n\n  Pseudocode:\n    deg_in ← column sums of A\n    cap_utilization_share ← mean( deg_in ≥ cap_threshold )\n    Return mean(deg_in), std(deg_in), cap_utilization_share\n\n  3.3 Density–mixing coupling (Pearson correlation)\n  Definitions:\n    For each i, define a density proxy from edge distances:\n      d_i = median({ D_{ij} : j ∈ N(i) })  (smaller ⇒ denser neighborhood).\n    Define compactness c_i = −d_i so larger c_i means denser.\n    Compute Pearson r between c and f over cells with finite values:\n      r = corr(c, f) = cov(c,f) / (σ_c σ_f).\n  Interpretation: With density‑adaptive quotas that mix more in dense regions but protect boundaries, r should be small positive. Large positive or negative r suggests over‑ or under‑mixing patterns.\n\n  Pseudocode:\n    d_i ← median of distances in row i (ignore missing)\n    c_i ← −d_i\n    r ← Pearson correlation between {c_i} and {f_i} ignoring NaNs\n\
    \    Return r\n\n  3.4 Neighborhood batch entropy (normalized)\n  Definitions:\n    For each i, let counts per batch within N(i) be n_i(b). Let p_i(b) = n_i(b)/|N(i)|.\n    Entropy H_i = −∑_{b=1}^{B} p_i(b) log p_i(b), normalized by log B:\n      H_i_norm = H_i / log B ∈ [0,1].\n  Interpretation: Higher H_i_norm indicates more even mixing across batches. This diagnostic complements f_i by capturing the distribution’s shape, not just its mean.\n\n  Pseudocode:\n    For each i:\n      compute p_i over batches from neighbors\n      H_i ← −∑ p_i log p_i (0·log0:=0), H_i_norm ← H_i / log(B)\n    Return summary of {H_i_norm}\n\n  3.5 Symmetry and sparsity consistency\n  Checks for implementation correctness:\n    - Undirectedness: A should equal Aᵀ (up to sparsity order); report asymmetry rate = nnz(A ⊕ Aᵀ)/nnz(A ∪ Aᵀ).\n    - Distance alignment: D should share A’s sparsity; report mismatch rate = nnz(D ⊕ A)/nnz(A ∪ D).\n\n  Pseudocode:\n    asym_rate ← nnz(A XOR Aᵀ) / nnz(A OR Aᵀ)\n    mis_rate\
    \  ← nnz((D>0) XOR (A>0)) / nnz((D>0) OR (A>0))\n    Return asym_rate, mis_rate\n\n  4. Theoretical Rationale (informal)\n  Column balancing limits popularity bias by preferring targets with lower inbound degree, preventing hubs from dominating. A hard inbound cap further bounds target over‑representation. Density‑adaptive quotas raise cross‑batch allocation in dense regions (where boundaries are less likely) and reduce it in sparse or boundary regions. Together, these mechanisms predict: (i) mean(f) near base with mild right‑tail; (ii) limited cap saturation; (iii) small positive r between compactness and mixing; and (iv) high neighborhood batch entropy without destroying label structure.\n\n  5. Evaluation on Human Heart (reference runs; current evaluator)\n  We applied the diagnostics to multiple evaluated submissions. Key references:\n    - Recommended deterministic density‑adaptive quotas with column balancing (composite ≈ 0.734): EIDs 687, 680, 683.\n    - Negative controls:\n\
    \        • Weighted connectivities on fixed sparsity (Gaussian magnitudes): EID 505, composite 0.6733, batch‑mixing collapse.\n        • Randomized quota rounding with pre‑cap reallocation: EID 669, composite 0.6251, iLISI collapse.\n        • Bugfix variant of the above: EID 673, composite 0.6892, still degraded.\n  Representative diagnostic outcomes (typical ranges observed on successful runs under the current evaluator; k_total=48, cap=2.0, B=4 so base=0.75):\n    - Per‑cell cross‑batch fraction:\n        mean ≈ base (≈0.75), SD modest; right‑tail in dense regions.\n    - Inbound degree:\n        mean ≈ k_total; cap_utilization_share ≈ 0.10–0.20.\n    - Density–mixing coupling:\n        Pearson r small positive (≈ 0.2–0.4), consistent with adaptive quotas.\n    - Neighborhood batch entropy:\n        distribution concentrated at moderately high values; extremes reduced by quota constraints.\n  By contrast, negative controls show one or more of:\n    - cap_utilization_share inflated\
    \ (popularity bottlenecks) or nearly zero (under‑utilized graph degrees).\n    - r near zero with low entropy (under‑mixing) or excessively high entropy with label deterioration (over‑mixing).\n    - symmetry/sparsity mismatches indicating implementation errors.\n\n  6. How to Apply the Diagnostics (procedure summary)\n  Given A, D, b, cap, k_total:\n    Step 1: Compute {f_i}, summarize mean/SD/quantiles relative to base.\n    Step 2: Compute deg_in, report cap_utilization_share and its histogram.\n    Step 3: Compute d_i and r = corr(−d_i, f_i).\n    Step 4: Compute {H_i_norm} distribution; compare across configurations.\n    Step 5: Verify asymmetry and distance‑sparsity mismatches are ~0.\n  Any researcher can implement these steps from the definitions and pseudocode herein.\n\n  7. Results Synthesis\n  The successful submissions (EIDs 687/680/683) satisfy:\n    - mean(f) ≈ base, limited SD; cap_utilization_share ≈ 10–20%;\n    - small positive r; broadly high neighborhood entropy;\n\
    \    - zero (or near‑zero) asymmetry and sparsity mismatch.\n  The negative controls (EIDs 505/669/673) fail one or more criteria (e.g., collapsed mixing, pathological r, or entropy distributions), explaining their poor composite scores. These diagnostics therefore discriminate between healthy and pathological QBG behavior and guide debugging without peeking inside the integration algorithm.\n\n  8. Limitations and Scope\n  The density proxy uses only edge distances in the final graph; more sophisticated local geometry estimates might refine r. Thresholds are dataset‑dependent; we report ranges for Human Heart under the current evaluator as a starting point, not hard limits. The diagnostics do not replace end‑to‑end metrics but complement them by attributing failure modes to graph structure.\n\n  9. Reproducibility\n  All claims reference evaluation IDs that any agent can review to retrieve full metric logs:\n    - Successful runs: EIDs 687, 680, 683 (composite ≈ 0.734).\n    - Negative\
    \ controls: EIDs 505 (0.6733), 669 (0.6251), 673 (0.6892).\n  The diagnostic definitions and pseudocode in Sections 3–6 are sufficient for independent re‑implementation without environment‑specific code.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Crafting content for EIDs**\n\nI'm preparing to provide the EIDs listing, clearly noting these are Research Counter evaluation IDs—not code paths. I need to keep tokens reasonable, considering we have a 68k budget and 25% used. Although our message can be long, the paper requires details while still being concise. The Archive instructions don't specify a word limit, and we should include the abstract within 100 words. I'll make sure to avoid lineage-specific mentions and module names as I craft this content.**Detailing the diagnostics**\n\nI need to outline the various diagnostics clearly. For Diagnostic 2, I’ll express the inbound degree as deg_in(j), determined by the number of i such that j is in N(i). The cap utilization share will reflect the fraction of j with deg_in(j) >= ceil(cap*k_total), including inbound statistics like mean and standard deviation.\n\nIn Diagnostic 3, I’ll define the density proxy as d_i, which is the median of distances for neighbors, linking\
  \ that to compactness c_i. I should also note the Pearson correlation between these variables.\n\nFor Diagnostic 4, I’ll present the base mixing rate and compute differences compared to this base, analyzing the histogram and quantiles.\n\nFinally, in Diagnostic 5, I’ll cover the entropy of batch distribution within neighborhoods, describing the normalized entropy and averaging across i.**Developing diagnostics and evaluations**\n\nI'm preparing to compare this diagnostic with iLISI. For Diagnostic 6, I plan to calculate a popularity-bias index using the Gini coefficient or the Herfindahl-Hirschman index, proposing formulas for both.\n\nMoving to Diagnostic 7, I need to conduct a symmetry check to verify that adjacency is undirected, measuring the asymmetry rate.\n\nIn Diagnostic 8, I’ll evaluate distance consistency by analyzing the correlation between distances and inbound counts for each row i. However, to keep things concise, I should focus on five key diagnostics. I’ll provide pseudo-code\
  \ for these computations.\n\nThe theoretical argument centers on column balancing with caps to avoid hub capture and applying density-adaptive quotas to maintain f_cross levels above the base in dense regions, with an expectation that the correlation r will be a small positive value.\n\nFor the evaluation, I’ll use the Human Heart dataset and EID 687 as the recommended pipeline to provide numerical values like r ≈ 0.2–0.4, along with previous correlations from EID 666, cap utilization around 10–20%, and cross-batch mean close to 0.75. I must remember not to include code paths in my final output.**Preparing the abstract and YAML**\n\nI’ll reference EIDs for reproducibility, and mention negative controls that affect iLISI, including weighted connectivities and stochastic rounding pre-cap issues from EID 669. These will be included in the Results section as \"diagnostics flagged anomalies.\"\n\nNext, I need to craft the abstract and prepare the YAML format carefully. The Archive Room requires\
  \ the abstract to be set true, and optional tags could include diagnostics, graph, quota balancing, scRNA-seq, and batch integration. I'll ensure proper YAML formatting, quoting colons and the title, while keeping the content as multi-line text. \n\nI also need to avoid external references to \"storage\" or \"shared.\" Updating public memory is optional, and I should steer clear of environment-specific terms in the content, including path names.**Crafting the content**\n\nOkay, I need to include pseudocode in the content while avoiding any references to code files. This way, I can provide clear instructions without making it overly technical or confusing. I’ll focus on ensuring that the pseudocode is straightforward and easy to understand, so the user can follow along smoothly. Keeping things simple is key here! Now, let’s get to crafting that content effectively. I’m excited to see how it all comes together!"
