role: model
parts:
- text: "I will implement a label-free Graph Health Score (GHS) and an Auto-GHS tuner that selects δ, k_density, and λ on a batch-stratified subsample, then builds the final graph on all cells with the best settings. Then I’ll submit the method and append an addendum to the diagnostics paper with the formal GHS definition.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/diagnostics/graph_health.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  # --------- Core low-level helpers ---------\n  def _csr(M):\n      return M.tocsr() if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(M) else M\n\n  def cross_batch_fraction(C: sp.spmatrix, batches: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      C = _csr(C)\n      b = np.asarray(batches)\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      out = np.full(n, np.nan, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          s, e = C.indptr[i], C.indptr[i + 1]\n          if e <= s: continue\n          nbrs = C.indices[s:e]\n          out[i] = np.mean(b[nbrs]\
    \ != b[i])\n      return out\n\n  def inbound_degree(C: sp.spmatrix) -> np.ndarray:\n      C = _csr(C)\n      return np.asarray(C.tocsc().sum(axis=0)).ravel().astype(np.int32)\n\n  def row_median_distance(D: sp.spmatrix) -> np.ndarray:\n      D = _csr(D)\n      n = D.shape[0]\n      out = np.full(n, np.nan, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          s, e = D.indptr[i], D.indptr[i + 1]\n          if e <= s: continue\n          out[i] = np.median(D.data[s:e]).astype(np.float32)\n      return out\n\n  def neighborhood_batch_entropy(C: sp.spmatrix, batches: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      C = _csr(C)\n      b = np.asarray(batches)\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      cats = np.unique(b)\n      B = len(cats)\n      out = np.full(n, np.nan, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          s, e = C.indptr[i], C.indptr[i + 1]\n          if e <= s: continue\n          nbrs = C.indices[s:e]\n          if nbrs.size == 0: continue\n          cnts = np.array([(b[nbrs] == c).sum() for\
    \ c in cats], dtype=np.float32)\n          p = cnts / max(1.0, cnts.sum())\n          # entropy with 0*log0 := 0\n          nz = p > 0\n          H = -float(np.sum(p[nz] * np.log(p[nz])))\n          out[i] = H / np.log(B)\n      return out\n\n  def pearsonr(x: np.ndarray, y: np.ndarray) -> float:\n      x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float32)\n      y = np.asarray(y, dtype=np.float32)\n      m = np.isfinite(x) & np.isfinite(y)\n      if m.sum() < 3: return np.nan\n      xz, yz = x[m] - x[m].mean(), y[m] - y[m].mean()\n      sx = np.sqrt((xz * xz).sum() + 1e-12)\n      sy = np.sqrt((yz * yz).sum() + 1e-12)\n      return float((xz * yz).sum() / (sx * sy))\n\n  def asymmetry_rate(C: sp.spmatrix) -> float:\n      C = _csr(C)\n      X = C.sign()\n      Y = C.T.sign()\n      num = (X != Y).nnz\n      den = (X + Y).sign().nnz + 1e-12\n      return float(num / den)\n\n  def sparsity_mismatch_rate(C: sp.spmatrix, D: sp.spmatrix) -> float:\n      X = _csr(C).sign()\n      Y = _csr(D).sign()\n    \
    \  num = (X != Y).nnz\n      den = (X + Y).sign().nnz + 1e-12\n      return float(num / den)\n\n  # --------- Scoring utilities ---------\n  def _triangular(value: float, center: float, width: float) -> float:\n      if np.isnan(value): return 0.0\n      s = 1.0 - abs(value - center) / max(width, 1e-8)\n      return float(max(0.0, min(1.0, s)))\n\n  def _one_minus_scaled(x: float, scale: float) -> float:\n      if np.isnan(x): return 0.0\n      return float(max(0.0, min(1.0, 1.0 - x / max(scale, 1e-8))))\n\n  # --------- Graph Health Score (GHS) ---------\n  def compute_graph_health(\n      C: sp.spmatrix,\n      D: sp.spmatrix,\n      batches: np.ndarray,\n      k_total: int,\n      cap: float = 2.0\n  ):\n      b = np.asarray(batches)\n      B = len(np.unique(b))\n      base = (B - 1.0) / float(B)\n\n      # Core diagnostics\n      f = cross_batch_fraction(C, b)\n      indeg = inbound_degree(C)\n      d_med = row_median_distance(D)\n      Hn = neighborhood_batch_entropy(C, b)\n   \
    \   r = pearsonr(-d_med, f)  # compactness vs mixing\n      asym = asymmetry_rate(C)\n      mis = sparsity_mismatch_rate(C, D)\n\n      # Targets and sub-scores (0..1)\n      # mean(f) close to base within ±0.03\n      f_mean = float(np.nanmean(f))\n      s_mix = _one_minus_scaled(abs(f_mean - base), 0.03)\n\n      # cap utilization near 0.15 with width 0.10\n      cap_thr = int(np.ceil(cap * k_total))\n      cap_share = float(np.mean(indeg >= cap_thr))\n      s_cap = _triangular(cap_share, center=0.15, width=0.10)\n\n      # density–mixing coupling small positive ~0.3 with width 0.3\n      s_r = _triangular(r, center=0.30, width=0.30)\n\n      # entropy moderately high; map mean(Hn) 0.65→0, 0.90→1\n      H_mean = float(np.nanmean(Hn))\n      s_ent = float(np.clip((H_mean - 0.65) / 0.25, 0.0, 1.0))\n\n      # implementation correctness penalties\n      s_asym = _one_minus_scaled(asym, 0.05)\n      s_mis = _one_minus_scaled(mis, 0.05)\n\n      # Weighted aggregate\n      weights = {\n\
    \          \"mix\": 0.35, \"cap\": 0.20, \"r\": 0.20, \"ent\": 0.15, \"asym\": 0.05, \"mis\": 0.05\n      }\n      ghs = (\n          weights[\"mix\"] * s_mix +\n          weights[\"cap\"] * s_cap +\n          weights[\"r\"] * s_r +\n          weights[\"ent\"] * s_ent +\n          weights[\"asym\"] * s_asym +\n          weights[\"mis\"] * s_mis\n      )\n      return {\n          \"GHS\": float(ghs),\n          \"subscores\": {\n              \"s_mix\": s_mix, \"s_cap\": s_cap, \"s_r\": s_r,\n              \"s_entropy\": s_ent, \"s_asym\": s_asym, \"s_mis\": s_mis\n          },\n          \"diagnostics\": {\n              \"base\": base, \"f_mean\": f_mean, \"cap_share\": cap_share,\n              \"r\": r, \"H_mean\": H_mean, \"asym\": asym, \"mismatch\": mis,\n              \"cap_threshold\": cap_thr, \"k_total\": int(k_total), \"B\": int(B)\n          }\n      }\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/algorithms/auto_ghs_daqb.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\
    \n  import numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  # Proven DAQB builder (deterministic; binary edges + true distances)\n  from praxis.bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n  # Graph Health Score\n  from shared.diagnostics.graph_health import compute_graph_health\n\n  # ---------------- Utilities ----------------\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n\
    \      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def _batch_stratified_subsample(batches, max_per_batch=1500, rng=0):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(rng)\n      b = np.asarray(batches)\n      idx = np.arange(b.shape[0])\n      out = []\n      for c in np.unique(b):\n          I = idx[b == c]\n          if I.size <= max_per_batch:\n              out.append(I)\n          else:\n              out.append(rng.choice(I, size=max_per_batch, replace=False))\n      return np.sort(np.concatenate(out, axis=0))\n\n  # ---------------- Main ----------------\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Auto-GHS DAQB: Label-free tuning of density-adaptive\
    \ quotas and shrinkage.\n      Steps:\n        1) Build Zcorr via BRBG.\n        2) On a batch-stratified subsample, grid-search (λ, δ, k_density) with DAQB;\n           compute Graph Health Score (GHS) for each candidate; select best.\n        3) Rebuild DAQB on full data with best params; write binary connectivities and true distances.\n      \"\"\"\n      # Config (EtC for QC; BRBG defaults)\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam_grid = [0.7, 0.8]        # include small sweep per reviewer suggestion\n      delta_grid = [0.14, 0.15, 0.16]\n      kd_grid = [18, 20, 22]\n      k_total = 48\n      cap = 2.0\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      alpha_var = 0.5\n      emb_n_neighbors = 15\n      rng_seed = 0\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch',\n\
    \                                  n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # EtC embedding (QC neighbors only)\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n\n      # BRBG: PCA50 → batch ridge → adaptive shrinkage → Zcorr\n      Zg\
    \ = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n\n      # Subsample for tuning\n      sub_idx = _batch_stratified_subsample(batches, max_per_batch=1500, rng=rng_seed)\n      batches_sub = batches[sub_idx]\n      Zg_sub = Zg[sub_idx]\n      Zhat_sub = Zhat[sub_idx]\n      var_total_sub = var_total\n      var_pred_sub = var_pred\n\n      # Grid search\n      best = None\n      for lam in lam_grid:\n          R2 = np.clip(var_pred_sub / var_total_sub, 0.0, 1.0)\n          gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n          Zcorr_sub = Zg_sub - (Zhat_sub * gamma)\n          for delta in delta_grid:\n              for kd in kd_grid:\n                  C_sub, D_sub = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(\n                      Zcorr_sub.astype(np.float32), batches=batches_sub,\n                \
    \      k_total=k_total, metric=metric, delta=delta,\n                      k_density=kd, rng_seed=rng_seed\n                  )\n                  ghs = compute_graph_health(C_sub, D_sub, batches_sub, k_total=k_total, cap=cap)\n                  score = ghs[\"GHS\"]\n                  cand = (score, lam, delta, kd)\n                  if (best is None) or (score > best[0]):\n                      best = cand\n\n      # Best params\n      if best is None:\n          # Fallback to known-good defaults\n          lam_best, delta_best, kd_best = 0.7, 0.15, 20\n      else:\n          _, lam_best, delta_best, kd_best = best\n\n      # Full rebuild with best params\n      R2_full = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma_full = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam_best * R2_full)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma_full)\n      Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(\n          Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n          k_total=k_total, metric=metric, delta=delta_best,\n          k_density=int(kd_best),\
    \ rng_seed=rng_seed\n      )\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      # Optionally log chosen params\n      adata.uns['auto_ghs_params'] = {\n          'lambda': float(lam_best), 'delta': float(delta_best), 'k_density': int(kd_best),\n          'k_total': int(k_total)\n      }\n      return adata\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Auto‑GHS DAQB: Label‑free tuning of density‑adaptive quotas and shrinkage in EtC–BRBG\"\ntags: \"auto, diagnostics, bbsg, residualization, hyperparameter tuning\"\nabstract: \"We define a label‑free Graph Health Score (GHS) combining per‑cell cross‑batch mixing, cap utilization, density–mixing coupling, entropy, and implementation checks. On a batch‑stratified subsample, we grid‑search (λ, δ, k_density) for DAQB using GHS, then rebuild the full graph with the best settings. Graphs are binary with true distances; BRBG is unchanged. Hypothesis: match or exceed fixed δ=0.15, k_density=20 with improved\
    \ robustness across environments.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from shared.algorithms.auto_ghs_daqb import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 24}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum: Graph Health Score (GHS) and Auto‑tuning Procedure\n\n  Building on Sections 3–6, we define a single scalar objective—the Graph Health Score (GHS)—to guide label‑free hyperparameter selection:\n    GHS = 0.35·s_mix + 0.20·s_cap + 0.20·s_r + 0.15·s_entropy + 0.05·s_asym + 0.05·s_mis,\n  where\n    - s_mix = 1 − |mean(f) − base| / 0.03, clipped to [0,1], with base = (B−1)/B.\n    - s_cap = triangular(cap_share; center=0.15, width=0.10), cap_share = P[deg_in ≥ ceil(cap·k)].\n    - s_r = triangular(Pearson(−median_distance, f); center=0.30, width=0.30).\n    - s_entropy = linear map of mean neighborhood batch entropy (normalized by log B) from [0.65, 0.90] to [0, 1].\n    - s_asym = 1 − asymmetry_rate/0.05; s_mis = 1 − sparsity_mismatch_rate/0.05;\
    \ all scores clipped to [0,1].\n  All components come from the definitions and pseudocode in the main text; no environment‑specific code is required.\n\n  Auto‑tuning procedure (label‑free):\n    1) Build Zcorr via BRBG.\n    2) On a batch‑stratified subsample, grid‑search (λ, δ, k_density) for DAQB, compute GHS for each candidate graph.\n    3) Select the best by GHS; rebuild DAQB on full data with the chosen parameters; output binary connectivities and true distances.\n\n  This operationalizes the reviewer’s suggestion into a reproducible method: combine diagnostics into a single objective and use it inside a lightweight search loop without cell‑type labels.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering evaluation constraints**\n\nI'm figuring out that the evaluation environment only allows for one function. I could potentially implement it into the eliminate_batch_effect_fn to optimize automatically. However, the evaluation function needs to yield one final integrated graph. I’m weighing the option to tune parameters for GHS on trial graphs built quickly, but it might be heavy. The runtime limit is 30 minutes with a dataset of 20k cells, and building multiple graphs could push me over time. Maybe I should consider computing GHS from a cheap subsample or using small test sets?**Planning Auto-GHS Tuner**\n\nI’m considering a better plan to provide the “Auto-GHS Tuner” code. First, I'll compute Zcorr on the full dataset. Next, on a 20% stratified subsample of around 3-5k cells, I’ll experiment with a small grid of parameters: delta values of 0.12, 0.15, and 0.18; k_density values of 18, 20, and 22; and a shrinkage scale lam of 0.7, 0.8, and possibly 0.9. By\
  \ combining these, I could yield 27 candidate graphs on the subsample, which may save time by constructing neighbors per-batch. Then, I’ll compute GHS for each and choose the best before running the final graph on the full dataset with the selected parameters while keeping batch labels and gene expressions in check.**Evaluating import strategies**\n\nI’m assessing how to handle the import from `praxis.bbsg_density_adaptive`, which uses `build_density_adaptive_bbsg`. It might be wise to implement a local builder to avoid dependency on a lineage-specific path, although importing from storage is allowed. Since the research code can import from storage/praxis, I can go ahead and propose the new method for the Research Counter by converting it to code that implements AutoGHS. Maybe creating a Private Memory plan first would help, but I could also just code it and submit for research, then add to the Archive later. The current priority is to respond now.**Planning GHS actions**\n\nI’m realizing\
  \ that there's no direct \"help/test\" to tackle, so I can take some actions instead. I’ll write a shared module for the GHS scoring function and create a new algorithm for auto-tuning. Then, I can submit my findings to research. \n\nI also want to update the accepted paper in the Archive with the \"graph health\" addition, proposing to add a section about \"Graph Health Score (GHS) and auto-tuning scheme\" in update #24 while adhering to the YAML quoting rules. \n\nNext, I think I’ll craft an execute action to create a file for computing `compute_graph_health(adata, B, params)` while ensuring it’s self-contained by importing from an earlier diagnostics file.**Planning GHS implementation**\n\nI'm getting ready to work in the Research Counter environment to write the GHS scoring function and the auto-tuning algorithm. First, I’ll utilize the file from 'storage/shared/diagnostics'. This includes normalization, high variance gene selection, and grid tuning for parameters. I'll also implement\
  \ a subsampling function to ensure safe runtime, targeting a sample fraction of 0.2.\n\nNext, I’ll reuse the builder from 'praxis' or create a minimal version to avoid dependencies. I need to fix the grid size for practical limits and compute the GHS efficiently. \n\nOnce that’s done, I’ll prepare to submit my findings and update the Archive with the new section on the Graph Health Score methods. Keeping in mind the usage of tokens, I’ll ensure everything is formatted correctly as I craft this code.**Developing Auto GHS Code**\n\nI’m drafting the `auto_ghs_daqb.py` file, starting with the `eliminate_batch_effect_fn`. This function includes normalization, HVG, and a pipeline for calculating Zcorr. For sampling, I'll choose a fraction of 0.25, capped at around 6000 samples. I’ll set up parameter grids for lambda, delta, and k-density to evaluate each combination on a subset.\n\nOnce I identify the best scoring combination, I'll build the full graph and unify everything. Then, we’ll prepare\
  \ for a research submission titled “Auto-GHS DAQB: Label-free tuning of density-adaptive quotas” with an abstract covering the GHS. \n\nI'll ensure we adhere to documentation rules, keeping batch labels only, and craft the appropriate update for Archive #24. Finally, I’ll write the required functions for the `graph_health.py` file and maintain token efficiency as I submit the project."
