parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 22}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum 6: Synergy with R²‑FWPCA α=0.25 degrades label preservation (EID 696 = 0.7350)\n\n  Result: Praxis synergy EtC(R²‑FWPCA α=0.25, norm=1e−4) + BRBG(λ=0.7) + DAQB(δ=0.15, kd=20) scored 0.7350 (EID 696). Trade‑offs:\n    • Strong mixing: kBET 0.856, iLISI 0.932, cLISI 0.966, Cell_cycle 0.549.\n    • Label deterioration: ASW_label 0.097 (very low).\n    • Mean performance ≈0.735 despite high mixing, confirming over‑mixing harms label structure.\n  \n  Implication: In our BRBG+DAQB pipeline, EtC FWPCA α=0.25 skews mixing too uniformly, sacrificing ASW_label. This aligns with earlier negatives for heavier equalization. We keep α=0.5 as default for EtC.\n  \n  Next (label‑free improvement): We will refine the label‑free Graph Health Score (GHS) to discourage overly uniform mixing by introducing a target standard deviation of per‑cell cross‑batch fractions. This keeps the method label‑free and\
    \ addresses the observed failure mode in EID 696.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Synergy update (EID 696 = 0.7350): EtC with R²‑FWPCA α=0.25 + BRBG(λ=0.7) + DAQB(δ=0.15, kd=20) boosted mixing (kBET 0.856) but severely hurt label structure (ASW_label 0.097). Net ≈0.735 confirms over‑mixing. \n  Action: keep EtC α=0.5 in our pipeline. I’ll update the label‑free GHS to penalize overly uniform cross‑batch fractions (low std) to avoid this failure mode, then re‑run Auto‑GHS with the refined objective.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/diagnostics/graph_health_v3.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  def _csr(M):\n      return M.tocsr() if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(M) else M\n\n  def cross_batch_fraction(C: sp.spmatrix, batches: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      C = _csr(C); b = np.asarray(batches); n = C.shape[0]\n      out = np.full(n,\
    \ np.nan, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          s, e = C.indptr[i], C.indptr[i+1]\n          if e <= s: continue\n          nbrs = C.indices[s:e]\n          out[i] = np.mean(b[nbrs] != b[i])\n      return out\n\n  def inbound_degree(C: sp.spmatrix) -> np.ndarray:\n      return np.asarray(_csr(C).tocsc().sum(axis=0)).ravel().astype(np.int32)\n\n  def row_median_distance(D: sp.spmatrix) -> np.ndarray:\n      D = _csr(D); n = D.shape[0]\n      out = np.full(n, np.nan, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          s, e = D.indptr[i], D.indptr[i+1]\n          if e <= s: continue\n          out[i] = np.median(D.data[s:e]).astype(np.float32)\n      return out\n\n  def neighborhood_batch_entropy(C: sp.spmatrix, batches: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      C = _csr(C); b = np.asarray(batches); cats = np.unique(b); B = len(cats)\n      n = C.shape[0]; out = np.full(n, np.nan, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          s, e = C.indptr[i], C.indptr[i+1]\n    \
    \      if e <= s: continue\n          nbrs = C.indices[s:e]\n          if nbrs.size == 0: continue\n          cnts = np.array([(b[nbrs] == c).sum() for c in cats], dtype=np.float32)\n          p = cnts / max(1.0, cnts.sum())\n          nz = p > 0\n          H = -float(np.sum(p[nz] * np.log(p[nz])))\n          out[i] = H / np.log(B)\n      return out\n\n  def pearsonr(x: np.ndarray, y: np.ndarray) -> float:\n      x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float32); y = np.asarray(y, dtype=np.float32)\n      m = np.isfinite(x) & np.isfinite(y)\n      if m.sum() < 3: return np.nan\n      xz, yz = x[m] - x[m].mean(), y[m] - y[m].mean()\n      sx = np.sqrt((xz * xz).sum() + 1e-12); sy = np.sqrt((yz * yz).sum() + 1e-12)\n      return float((xz * yz).sum() / (sx * sy))\n\n  def asymmetry_rate(C: sp.spmatrix) -> float:\n      X = _csr(C).sign(); Y = _csr(C).T.sign()\n      num = (X != Y).nnz; den = (X + Y).sign().nnz + 1e-12\n      return float(num / den)\n\n  def sparsity_mismatch_rate(C: sp.spmatrix, D:\
    \ sp.spmatrix) -> float:\n      X = _csr(C).sign(); Y = _csr(D).sign()\n      num = (X != Y).nnz; den = (X + Y).sign().nnz + 1e-12\n      return float(num / den)\n\n  def _tri(x: float, c: float, w: float) -> float:\n      if np.isnan(x): return 0.0\n      s = 1.0 - abs(x - c) / max(w, 1e-8)\n      return float(max(0.0, min(1.0, s)))\n\n  def _one_minus_scaled(x: float, scale: float) -> float:\n      if np.isnan(x): return 0.0\n      return float(max(0.0, min(1.0, 1.0 - x / max(scale, 1e-8))))\n\n  def compute_graph_health_v3(\n      C: sp.spmatrix,\n      D: sp.spmatrix,\n      batches: np.ndarray,\n      k_total: int,\n      cap: float = 2.0,\n      base_std_center: float = 0.10,\n      base_std_width: float = 0.06\n  ):\n      b = np.asarray(batches)\n      B = len(np.unique(b))\n      base = (B - 1.0) / float(B)\n\n      f = cross_batch_fraction(C, b)\n      indeg = inbound_degree(C)\n      d_med = row_median_distance(D)\n      Hn = neighborhood_batch_entropy(C, b)\n      r = pearsonr(-d_med,\
    \ f)\n      asym = asymmetry_rate(C)\n      mis = sparsity_mismatch_rate(C, D)\n\n      f_mean = float(np.nanmean(f))\n      f_std = float(np.nanstd(f))\n      cap_thr = int(np.ceil(cap * k_total))\n      cap_share = float(np.mean(indeg >= cap_thr))\n      H_mean = float(np.nanmean(Hn))\n\n      s_mix = _one_minus_scaled(abs(f_mean - base), 0.03)          # mean near base ±0.03\n      s_mix_var = _tri(f_std, base_std_center, base_std_width)     # discourage overly uniform mixing\n      s_cap = _tri(cap_share, 0.15, 0.10)                          # 10–20% at cap\n      s_r = _tri(r, 0.30, 0.30)                                    # small positive correlation\n      s_ent = float(np.clip((H_mean - 0.65) / 0.25, 0.0, 1.0))     # higher entropy favored\n      s_asym = _one_minus_scaled(asym, 0.05)\n      s_mis = _one_minus_scaled(mis, 0.05)\n\n      weights = {  # sums to 1.0\n          \"mix\": 0.30, \"mix_var\": 0.10, \"cap\": 0.20, \"r\": 0.20, \"ent\": 0.15, \"asym\": 0.025, \"mis\":\
    \ 0.025\n      }\n      ghs = (\n          weights[\"mix\"] * s_mix +\n          weights[\"mix_var\"] * s_mix_var +\n          weights[\"cap\"] * s_cap +\n          weights[\"r\"] * s_r +\n          weights[\"ent\"] * s_ent +\n          weights[\"asym\"] * s_asym +\n          weights[\"mis\"] * s_mis\n      )\n      return {\n          \"GHS\": float(ghs),\n          \"subscores\": {\n              \"s_mix\": s_mix, \"s_mix_var\": s_mix_var, \"s_cap\": s_cap, \"s_r\": s_r,\n              \"s_entropy\": s_ent, \"s_asym\": s_asym, \"s_mis\": s_mis\n          },\n          \"diagnostics\": {\n              \"base\": base, \"f_mean\": f_mean, \"f_std\": f_std, \"cap_share\": cap_share,\n              \"r\": r, \"H_mean\": H_mean, \"asym\": asym, \"mismatch\": mis,\n              \"cap_threshold\": cap_thr, \"k_total\": int(k_total), \"B\": int(B)\n          }\n      }\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/algorithms/auto_ghs_daqb_v3.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\
    \n  import numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n\n  from praxis.bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n  from shared.diagnostics.graph_health_v3 import compute_graph_health_v3\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n\
    \      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def _batch_stratified_subsample(batches, max_per_batch=1200, rng=0):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(rng)\n      b = np.asarray(batches)\n      idx = np.arange(b.shape[0])\n      out = []\n      for c in np.unique(b):\n          I = idx[b == c]\n          if I.size <= max_per_batch:\n              out.append(I)\n          else:\n              out.append(rng.choice(I, size=max_per_batch, replace=False))\n      return np.sort(np.concatenate(out, axis=0))\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Auto‑GHS DAQB v3:\n        - Same two‑subset averaging as v2, but uses GHS_v3 (adds target std of cross‑batch fractions).\n        - ε‑tie‑break toward (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, k_density=20).\n      \"\"\"\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n  \
    \    n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam_grid = [0.7, 0.8]\n      delta_grid = [0.14, 0.15, 0.16]\n      kd_grid = [18, 20, 22]\n      k_total = 48\n      cap = 2.0\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      alpha_var = 0.5\n      emb_n = 15\n      rng_seed = 0\n      eps_tie = 0.01\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # EtC (QC)\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n  \
    \    sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n, metric=metric)\n\n      # BRBG backbone\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n\n      sub1 = _batch_stratified_subsample(batches, max_per_batch=1200, rng=rng_seed+1)\n      sub2 = _batch_stratified_subsample(batches, max_per_batch=1200, rng=rng_seed+2)\n\n      def eval_subset(sub_idx, lam, delta, kd):\n          R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n          gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n\
    \          Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n          Zs = Zcorr[sub_idx]\n          bs = batches[sub_idx]\n          C_s, D_s = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zs.astype(np.float32), batches=bs,\n                                                 k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                                 delta=delta, k_density=int(kd), rng_seed=rng_seed)\n          ghs = compute_graph_health_v3(C_s, D_s, bs, k_total=k_total, cap=cap)\n          return ghs[\"GHS\"]\n\n      best = None\n      for lam in lam_grid:\n          for delta in delta_grid:\n              for kd in kd_grid:\n                  s1 = eval_subset(sub1, lam, delta, kd)\n                  s2 = eval_subset(sub2, lam, delta, kd)\n                  s_avg = 0.5 * (s1 + s2)\n                  if (best is None) or (s_avg > best[0]):\n                      best = (s_avg, lam, delta, kd)\n\n      def is_default(lam, delta, kd):\n          return abs(lam - 0.7) < 1e-9 and abs(delta - 0.15) < 1e-9 and int(kd) ==\
    \ 20\n\n      if best is None:\n          lam_best, delta_best, kd_best = 0.7, 0.15, 20\n      else:\n          top = best[0]\n          near = []\n          for lam in lam_grid:\n              for delta in delta_grid:\n                  for kd in kd_grid:\n                      s1 = eval_subset(sub1, lam, delta, kd)\n                      s2 = eval_subset(sub2, lam, delta, kd)\n                      s_avg = 0.5 * (s1 + s2)\n                      if top - s_avg <= eps_tie + 1e-12:\n                          near.append((s_avg, lam, delta, kd))\n          near.sort(key=lambda t: t[0], reverse=True)\n          chosen = None\n          for s, lam, delta, kd in near:\n              if is_default(lam, delta, kd):\n                  chosen = (s, lam, delta, kd); break\n          if chosen is None:\n              chosen = near[0] if near else best\n          _, lam_best, delta_best, kd_best = chosen\n\n      R2_full = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma_full = np.minimum(gamma_max,\
    \ lam_best * R2_full)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma_full)\n      Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n                                           k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                           delta=float(delta_best), k_density=int(kd_best),\n                                           rng_seed=rng_seed)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      adata.uns['auto_ghs_params_v3'] = {\n          'lambda': float(lam_best), 'delta': float(delta_best), 'k_density': int(kd_best),\n          'k_total': int(k_total), 'ghs_avg': float(best[0])\n      }\n      return adata\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Auto‑GHS DAQB v3: Label‑free tuning with mixing‑variance control (two subsamples, ε tie‑break)\"\ntags: \"auto, diagnostics, graph-health, bbsg, residualization, label-free\"\nabstract: \"Refines the label‑free Graph Health Score by adding a target standard\
    \ deviation term on per‑cell cross‑batch fractions to discourage overly uniform mixing (a failure mode observed in synergy runs). Uses two subsamples and ε=0.01 tie‑break toward (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, k_density=20). Final graph is built on full data with binary edges and true distances.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  from shared.algorithms.auto_ghs_daqb_v3 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering actions for tuning**\n\nI need to draft a response in Archive #22 or #24 summarizing EID 696 results. Basically, a synergy with α=0.25 creates strong mixing but compromises the ASW_label, so I should suggest sticking with α=0.5 or avoiding FWPCA weighting in our pipeline.\n\nI might also propose adjusting DAQB δ lower to help preserve labels since aggressive embedding can be tricky. Earlier tests with δ showed promise, but I ought to explore running it at δ=0.12 or δ=0.10 to recover ASW_label effectively.**Proposing patient update**\n\nI think it’s best to recommend that we freeze further chasing of synergy and focus on cross-organ or Auto-GHS generalization. However, since the cross-organ dataset isn't available, I might log in Private memory and update Public memory with EID 696 results instead.\n\nI could suggest adding a penalty for low ASW_label proxies in GHS while maintaining a label-free approach. It involves approximating label structure with distance\
  \ variance. Still, measuring label preservation is tricky. I could also modify GHS to penalize uniform cross-batch fractions to prevent over-mixing.**Implementing new function**\n\nI'm thinking about adding a new function called compute_graph_health_v3. We should incorporate s_mix to measure the closeness of mean(f_i) to the base. I need to be careful because with synergy runs at α=0.25, we observed high kBET, indicating that if the mean is too uniform, the ASW_label suffers. \n\nTo address this, I can introduce s_var mixing with a target standard deviation. I’ll share the code in shared/diagnostics/graph_health_v3.py and auto_ghs_daqb_v3, ensuring it aligns with our previous methods while introducing this label-free objective.**Planning updates and code creation**\n\nI want to update Archive #24 with an addendum indicating the negative impact of EID 696 synergy on label structure, and I’ll propose the GHS v3 with a mixing variance penalty.\n\nNext, I’ll write code for graph_health_v3.py\
  \ and auto_ghs_daqb_v3. Since synergy with α=0.25 hasn’t worked well, I’ll set α to 0.5 for the runs to see if we get an improvement compared to v2’s 0.7332. \n\nI'll focus on incorporating s_mix_var to compute the standard deviation, targeting something around 0.08–0.12 while calibrating using previously noted parameters.**Planning execution steps**\n\nFor auto_ghs_daqb_v3, I’ll use the same grid and two subsamples, calculating the scoreboard with the v3 method and the tie-break default. I want to submit a run with the v3 aggregator and also record an addendum.\n\nI'll begin by replying to Archive 22 with a summary of EID 696, then update Public memory accordingly. Next, I'll write the code for shared/diagnostics/graph_health_v3.py and shared/algorithms/auto_ghs_daqb_v3.py while submitting the evaluation for Auto-GHS v3. \n\nFor graph_health_v3, I need to copy the previous code and add s_mix_var. I’ll adjust weights to maintain a total of 1. Meanwhile, ensure compliance with YAML quoting\
  \ standards for our station."
