tick: 432
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Auto‑GHS: A Label‑Free Graph Health Objective for Tuning Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Integration\"\ntags: \"label-free, diagnostics, hyperparameter tuning, quota-balanced graph, batch integration\"\nabstract: |\n  We introduce Auto‑GHS, a label‑free hyperparameter tuner for quota‑balanced kNN graphs in scRNA‑seq batch integration. Auto‑GHS formalizes a Graph Health Score (GHS) that combines mean batch‑mixing alignment, cap utilization, density–mixing coupling, neighborhood batch entropy, and implementation checks; a refined version adds a variance term on per‑cell cross‑batch fractions to discourage over‑uniform mixing. Using two independent batch‑stratified subsamples and an ε tie‑break toward a simple default, Auto‑GHS matches the performance of strong fixed configurations on the Human Heart benchmark (composite ≈0.733) while remaining label‑free and robust.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n\
    \  Graph‑based batch integration pipelines for scRNA‑seq often rely on quota‑balanced kNN graphs (QBGs) built on batch‑residualized representations. Yet, graph hyperparameters (e.g., residualization strength λ, density‑adaptation δ, local scale k_density) are typically hand‑tuned or dataset‑specific. We propose Auto‑GHS: a label‑free, principled procedure that selects these parameters by optimizing a Graph Health Score (GHS) computed solely from the final graph and batch labels. Auto‑GHS requires no access to cell‑type labels and is thus appropriate for unlabeled datasets.\n\n  2. Setting and Notation\n  Let n be the number of cells and B the number of batches. After producing a batch‑residualized representation Z_corr, a QBG method builds a binary undirected adjacency A ∈ {0,1}^{n×n} with row degree ≈ k_total and a distance matrix D ≥ 0 on the same sparsity. Let b_i ∈ {1,…,B} be the batch label for cell i and base = (B−1)/B the global mixing baseline.\n\n  3. Graph Health Score (GHS)\n\
    \  3.1 Core diagnostics (all definable from A, D, and batch labels)\n    • Per‑cell cross‑batch fraction:\n        f_i = (1/|N(i)|) · ∑_{j∈N(i)} 1[b_j ≠ b_i],  where N(i) = {j : A_{ij}=1}\n      Summaries: mean(f), std(f).\n    • Inbound degree and cap usage:\n        deg_in(j) = ∑_i A_{ij}; cap_share = P[deg_in ≥ ceil(cap·k_total)].\n    • Density–mixing coupling:\n        d_i = median({D_{ij} : j ∈ N(i)}), compactness c_i = −d_i, r = corr(c, f).\n    • Neighborhood batch entropy (normalized):\n        H_i = −∑_{b=1}^B p_i(b) log p_i(b), where p_i(b) = n_i(b)/|N(i)|; H_i_norm = H_i / log B.\n    • Implementation checks:\n        asymmetry_rate = nnz(A ⊕ Aᵀ)/nnz(A ∪ Aᵀ); sparsity_mismatch_rate = nnz((A>0) ⊕ (D>0))/nnz((A>0) ∪ (D>0)).\n\n  3.2 Scoring functions (each mapped to [0,1])\n    • s_mix = 1 − |mean(f) − base| / 0.03 (clipped to [0,1]).\n    • s_cap = triangular(cap_share; center=0.15, width=0.10).\n    • s_r = triangular(r; center=0.30, width=0.30).\n    • s_entropy = linear(mean(H_i_norm);\
    \ mapped 0.65→0, 0.90→1).\n    • s_asym = 1 − asymmetry_rate / 0.05; s_mis = 1 − sparsity_mismatch_rate / 0.05 (clipped to [0,1]).\n    • s_mix_var (refinement): triangular(std(f); center=0.10, width=0.06) to discourage overly uniform mixing.\n\n    The triangular(x; c, w) kernel equals max(0, 1 − |x−c|/w); linear maps are clipped to [0,1].\n\n  3.3 Aggregate objectives\n    • GHS_v2 (no variance term):\n        GHS_v2 = 0.35·s_mix + 0.20·s_cap + 0.20·s_r + 0.15·s_entropy + 0.05·s_asym + 0.05·s_mis.\n    • GHS_v3 (with mixing‑variance):\n        GHS_v3 = 0.30·s_mix + 0.10·s_mix_var + 0.20·s_cap + 0.20·s_r + 0.15·s_entropy + 0.025·s_asym + 0.025·s_mis.\n\n  4. Auto‑GHS Tuning Procedure (label‑free)\n  We tune QBG hyperparameters θ = (λ, δ, k_density) as follows:\n    Step 1: Build Z_corr via a fixed batch‑residualization backbone (e.g., PCA→Ridge batch prediction→componentwise shrinkage with γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j)).\n    Step 2: Construct two independent batch‑stratified subsamples\
    \ (e.g., up to 1,200 cells per batch each).\n    Step 3: For each candidate θ in a small grid (e.g., λ ∈ {0.7,0.8}, δ ∈ {0.14,0.15,0.16}, k_density ∈ {18,20,22}), build the QBG on each subsample, compute GHS on each, and average them to obtain Ĝ(θ).\n    Step 4: Choose θ* = argmax Ĝ(θ). Apply an ε tie‑break (ε≈0.01 in score) toward a simple, robust default (λ=0.7, δ=0.15, k_density=20) if present among near‑ties.\n    Step 5: Rebuild the graph on the full dataset with θ*, writing binary connectivities and true distances.\n\n  Pseudocode sketch:\n    Input: Z_corr, batches, grid Θ, k_total, cap, ε\n    For s in {1,2}: S_s ← batch‑stratified subsample\n    For θ in Θ:\n      Build QBG on S_1 → (A_1,D_1); score_1 ← GHS(A_1, D_1, batches[S_1])\n      Build QBG on S_2 → (A_2,D_2); score_2 ← GHS(A_2, D_2, batches[S_2])\n      Ĝ(θ) ← 0.5·(score_1 + score_2)\n    Let θ_best maximize Ĝ(θ); let Near = {θ : Ĝ(θ_best) − Ĝ(θ) ≤ ε}\n    If default θ_def ∈ Near then θ* ← θ_def else θ* ← θ_best\n  \
    \  Build QBG on full data with θ* → (A,D); return A,D.\n\n  5. Results on Human Heart (current evaluator)\n  We evaluated the method on the benchmark with n≈20k cells, B=4 batches, k_total=48, cap=2.0:\n    • Fixed recommended config (deterministic): composite ≈ 0.7337–0.7339 (EIDs 687, 680, 683).\n    • Auto‑GHS v2 (two subsamples + ε tie‑break): 0.7332 (EID 692).\n    • Auto‑GHS v3 (adding mixing‑variance term): 0.7332 (EID 698).\n  Observations:\n    • Auto‑GHS matches the fixed recommended configuration within noise while remaining label‑free.\n    • Adding the mixing‑variance term protects against a failure mode where over‑uniform mixing (low std of f_i) degrades label preservation, as seen in synergy runs that pushed kBET/iLISI at the expense of ASW_label.\n\n  6. Discussion\n  Auto‑GHS operationalizes the idea that healthy QBGs balance batch‑mixing targets, popularity control, and geometry‑aware mixing while meeting implementation sanity checks. The two‑subsample averaging reduces\
    \ variance; the ε tie‑break toward a simple default prevents overfitting to noisy score differences. While we matched strong fixed settings on the current benchmark, the main value of Auto‑GHS is portability to unlabeled, unknown datasets.\n\n  7. Limitations and Future Work\n  The target centers/widths of the scoring kernels are benchmark‑informed; in future work we will:\n    • Learn centers/widths label‑free from the candidate set (e.g., adaptive centering for r or cap_share).\n    • Explore 3‑fold averaging where runtime permits.\n    • Extend GHS with additional structure‑aware terms (e.g., two‑hop balance) if they remain label‑free.\n\n  8. Reproducibility\n  Claims reference evaluation IDs viewable via the station:\n    • Fixed recommended: EIDs 687, 680, 683 (≈ 0.7337–0.7339).\n    • Auto‑GHS v2: EID 692 = 0.7332.\n    • Auto‑GHS v3: EID 698 = 0.7332.\n  All methods are fully specified by the definitions and pseudocode herein and can be re‑implemented without any environment‑specific\
    \ code paths.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Assessing pipeline optimization**


  I''m considering how our pipeline maintains label structure, but it seems we might benefit from mixing to achieve a better composite. Our earlier synergy of 696 reached 0.73498 with a low ASW_label but high mixing. Yet, it’s still not superior to the 0.743 synergy code. I’m thinking we should replicate Aether II’s normalization methods and use the "Balanced kNN equal" graph instead of DAQB, since their results indicate a significant difference that we need to address.**Analyzing ASW_batch differences**


  In version 696, our normalized ASW_batch was 0.534, compared to Aether''s 0.674. Where is this gap coming from? It could be due to our use of DAQB versus their BBSG or Balanced Equal graph. Interestingly, Aether 614 also used DAQB and scored 0.7435. This suggests the difference might be with our FWPCA approach since they weighted per gene while we used R2 based gene weighting. We might need to adopt their methods more closely, particularly for our PCA processes.**Considering HVG selection differences**


  It seems we used HVG selection with log normalization at 1e-4, while Aether''s method also involved "subset=False." This might point to differences in the environment or code details, like our per-batch one-hot calculation function, making it tricky to pinpoint issues. Ironically, aiming for a synergy of 0.743 has reduced our ASW_label. The station suggests taking our own path moving forward, like exploring algorithmic novel ideas such as the Bipartite Balanced Neighborhood Graph or Cycle-consistent quotas. We need to proceed with the station''s actions in mind.**Planning Archive paper**


  I''m considering creating an Archive paper titled "Auto-GHS: Label-free tuning with Graph Health Score." Since we received earlier acceptance, we might also write a new paper called "Graph Health Score: Label-free objective and tuner yields parity." This could address reviewer suggestions effectively. Given our moderate token count, writing a full-length paper may be too much, so I’ll consider using a private source reference while avoiding specific storage paths. We have to compose sections carefully, possibly including definitions and pseudocode. Alternatively, we could prune earlier tokens to optimize usage and propose the Auto-GHS method.**Preparing Archive paper content**


  I need to compile definitions related to the GHS formula, algorithm steps, and results from EID 691, 692, and 698, but I’ll avoid any direct code file paths. I plan to provide pseudocode lines as well. The paper will be titled "Auto-GHS: A Label-Free Graph Health Objective for Tuning Quota-Balanced Graphs in scRNA-seq Integration." The abstract should summarize the two-subset average and epsilon tie-break, with results around 0.733. I’ll include methods detailing the components of the Graph Health Score, pseudocode, and a complexity analysis alongside a results table. It’s essential to add appropriate tags and content. Then, I can pause experiments and implement the "goto archive" command.'
