  I think we can queue additional experiments to test different tau values, like 1.2, 1.3, 1.5, and even infinity. I want to provide a smaller code adaptation for the tau parameter. Instead of embedding the full code each time, we could import our module directly from storage to speed things up. Writing the geometry gating code into a stored path lets us easily run multiple experiments while varying tau, but I''ll need to think about parameterization for that function.**Implementing a module for experiments**


  I can create a module with a function called ''build_and_return'' so I can import ''eliminate_batch_effect_fn_tau_1_3'' easily in different submissions. It’s straightforward to write a factory function, ''make_eliminate_batch_effect_fn(tau)'', and then define it in submissions, which keeps my code concise. I want to remember the station restrictions and note that the code executed has access to the storage. I’ll write the necessary functions and prepare to run submissions for various tau values, including inf as a control.**Considering evaluation limits**


  I need to be mindful of the limit on concurrent runs, which allows a maximum of 2 evaluations per agent. Currently, evaluation 701 is running, so I can submit one more. After queuing it, I’ll implement the ''storage write'' command and then execute the submission for tau=1.2. Once that''s in the queue, I can optionally handle tau=1.6 later, but for now, I should focus on logging the results. I''ll also save the module and include a brief abstract to keep the content minimal.**Building the final module**


  I need to make sure the code implements a robust pipeline for various tasks, including QC and gating. I want to reuse previously written code in my new module, embedding it without too many comments for brevity. I also consider importing from ''praxis.bbsg_density_adaptive'' to reduce size, but since it lacks a geometry gate, I need to include that in my module. I''ll write the necessary code, save it, and submit it with the appropriate title and YAML formatting, while noting that page ranking changes aren’t essential.**Preparing to write code**


  I need to ensure the code stays concise while effectively using YAML with the ''content: |'' format. I won''t include the builder code in the submission to keep it shorter. First, I’ll write the storage file code, including the builder code we discussed earlier. I also need to implement ''make_eliminate_batch_effect_fn'' to return a function using the builder with a tau parameter. I''ll set it up with the values for tau, delta, k_density, lam, and gamma_max. Now, I’ll generate the commands to proceed.'
