tick: 446
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Protocol: Cross‑organ Validation and Reporting for DAQB (EtC–BRBG + Density‑Adaptive Balanced Graph)\"\ntags: \"protocol, reproducibility, single-cell, batch integration, graph, diagnostics\"\nabstract: |\n  We provide a concise, evaluator‑compatible protocol to validate the DAQB pipeline across organs and report results uniformly. The protocol fixes preprocessing, graph construction, and diagnostics; specifies a small, label‑free grid (δ, k_density); and standardizes reporting of full metric vectors and EIDs. It references a shared import‑ready wrapper for reproducibility and defines acceptance criteria and failure modes. This enables rapid, comparable validation across datasets while preserving scientific rigor and minimizing token cost.\ncontent: |\n  1. Objective\n     Validate the DAQB pipeline (EtC–BRBG + Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced kNN graph) across organs and report full metric vectors and\
    \ EIDs with minimal configuration drift.\n\n  2. Fixed Configuration (do not change)\n     - Preprocessing: normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p; HVGs via Seurat v3, batch-aware, n_top_genes=2000.\n     - EtC embedding (for QC/analysis only; not for graph):\n       • FWPCA α=0.5 → PCA(60) → ComBat on PC scores; save obsm['X_emb'].\n     - BRBG (graph representation):\n       • PCA(50) → ridge batch prediction (l2=1e−3).\n       • Per‑PC shrinkage: γ_j = min(0.74, 0.7·R²_j). Zcorr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ.\n     - DAQB graph:\n       • k_total=48, cosine distances, binary edges with true distances, strict column balancing (cap=2.0).\n       • Split neighbors by per‑cell cross‑batch fraction f_cross and even per‑batch quotas with remainder to closest batches.\n\n  3. Label‑Free Validation Grid\n     - Vary only:\n       • δ ∈ {0.14, 0.15, 0.16}\n       • k_density ∈ {18, 20, 22}  (density proxy = distance to the k_density‑th nearest neighbor in Zcorr across all batches)\n     - Keep all else fixed\
    \ (Section 2).\n\n  4. How to Run (shared wrapper; optional but recommended)\n     - Import the shared wrapper that matches the fixed config:\n       • storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_recommended.py\n       Example:\n         import sys; sys.path.append('storage/shared/algorithms')\n         from daqb_recommended import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n         # Submit eliminate_batch_effect_fn to the Research Counter.\n     - Reference parity: EID 687 ≈ 0.7337 on Human Heart using the shared wrapper.\n\n  5. Reporting Standard (include in your Public post)\n     - Provide: Composite score and full metric vector in this order:\n       [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle]\n     - Include: δ, k_density, k_total (=48), metric (=cosine), cap (=2.0), and the Research Counter EID(s).\n     - Optional plots (no labels required): \n       • f_cross vs local density (distance‑to‑k_density‑th NN in Zcorr).\n       • Inbound‑degree histogram per batch with\
    \ cap utilization (% nodes at cap).\n\n  6. Acceptance Criteria and Interpretation\n     - “Ridge region” evidence: δ=0.15 and k_density=20 should be near‑optimal on Human Heart; other organs may shift slightly.\n     - Improvements must be consistent across δ×k_density with no large regressions in ASW_label/cLISI.\n     - Beware of “mixing‑only” gains (kBET/iLISI↑) that collapse label structure (ASW_label↓); document trade‑offs explicitly.\n\n  7. Known Negatives to Avoid (Human Heart evidence)\n     - Geometry gates / hard candidate filtering (EIDs 701, 702 ~0.697; iLISI collapse).\n     - Global‑budget‑recentered density proxies (EID 629 ~0.720).\n     - Heavy embedding equalizations (rank‑based or whitening) before ComBat.\n     - Gaussian edge weights on a fixed sparsity pattern (EID 505 ~0.673; iLISI collapse).\n\n  8. References and Provenance\n     - Recommended DAQB (fixed): parity runs ≈0.733–0.734; historical best: 0.7452 (EID 609).\n     - Diagnostics and ranges: Archive\
    \ #24.\n     - DAQB methods and ablations: Archive #22 (+ addenda).\n\n  9. Reproducibility and Transparency\n     - Use only official evaluator outputs. Report all EIDs. When sharing code, prefer shared modules to minimize drift.\n     - If deviations from Section 2 occur, state them clearly and justify; otherwise, results may be non‑comparable.\n\n  10. Next Steps\n      - After organ‑wise results accumulate, we will publish a summary ridge map per organ with recommended (δ, k_density) and diagnostic ranges, and assess cross‑organ generality of the fixed configuration.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  New protocol published (Archive): “Protocol: Cross‑organ Validation and Reporting for DAQB” — ID pending in Archive list.\n  - Fixed config (EtC α=0.5 QC; BRBG λ_r=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50; DAQB k=48, cosine, cap=2.0).\n  - Minimal label‑free grid: δ∈{0.14,0.15,0.16} × k_density∈{18,20,22}.\n  - Report full metric vectors\
    \ + EIDs; include diagnostics (f_cross vs density; cap utilization).\n  We’ll maintain an organ‑wise ridge map and summarize findings in a follow‑up Archive note.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering submission strategies**\n\nI'm thinking about preparing a new Archive submission that compiles the DAQB improvements and negative lines into a proper methods paper. But we already have Archive #22 accepted with improvements, which scored 9/10. We could propose moving to Token Management to prune again after a cooldown, though it might not be necessary. Additionally, I might suggest creating a code module in shared storage for a testing harness or diagnostics. The new prompt also expects specific actions, so I need to consider checking for replies in the Mail Room.**Exploring next steps**\n\nIn the Mail Room, the available actions include reading, previewing, replying, and forwarding, but I don't see any new replies right now. I can head to the Archive Room and submit a short research note summarizing the α=0.25 replication negative as a negative result paper. However, since we've already posted addendum #10 in Archive #22, another full paper might be redundant.\
  \ \n\nI could create a brief Public Memory update instead. Alternatively, I might propose setting up code to generalize the pipeline for cross-organ validation, which could be beneficial for future projects.**Considering improvements for SOTA**\n\nI'm aiming for a small improvement to achieve state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance. The best recorded score is 0.7452 with DAQB settings of λ=0.7, δ=0.15, and kd=20, while the current parity runs around 0.7337. It seems the environment might have changed since previous evaluations. \n\nTo improve, I might tune the gamma_max or lambda synergy, testing values like 0.65 or 0.75. While earlier tweaks with λ=0.7 and δ=0.15 yielded 0.7365, I could also explore using R2 transformation for density adjustments and consider proportionally allocating cross quotas based on similarity weights among batches.**Evaluating modifications for metrics**\n\nI'm considering ways to increase kBET and iLISI without harming label integrity, but I need to be cautious since\
  \ this is a non-local modification. Alternatively, I could test the 'euclidean' metric for calculating neighbor distances instead of 'cosine.' The previous DMN union was neutral, and many top results defaulted to 'cosine.' \n\nGiven that the earlier DMN union 477 only achieved 0.7327, that’s not ideal. I can incorporate noise-robust tie-breaking when selecting neighbors by using inbound-first then distance strategy and possibly adding a 1e-6 jitter. Using a re-weighted inbound cap based on per-batch size could be another option to adjust inbound counts.**Planning Archive submissions and protocols**\n\nI'm considering various improvements while focusing on achieving SOTA performance. Dynamic balancing and neighbor distance adjustments could be beneficial, and I should propose to redirect efforts toward writing a proper Archive paper about density-adaptive DAQB generalization. We’ve already had Archive #22 accepted, so maybe we’ll work on the cross-organ validation method.\n\nI'm thinking\
  \ about crafting a \"Cross-organ Validation Protocol\" for a new Archive capsule with structured methods for collaboration. We can include components like data selection, preprocessing, and reporting standards, ensuring everything is well-documented. It’s crucial to maintain clarity in our processes and support ongoing research. Let's move forward with this idea!**Updating Archive submissions and protocols**\n\nIt looks like we didn’t see acceptance for Archive #21, but it’s still listed, indicating all capsules might be visible regardless of their scores. However, I’m not sure if it's accepted yet. Given our current timeline, I’ll focus on creating a \"Protocol: Cross-Organ DAQB Validation & Reporting\" in the Archive.\n\nI’ll include sections with necessary details like predetermined parameters and debugging instructions. We’ll also provide a reference to the shared algorithms. Finally, I'll post a public memory reply to share the new protocol. Let's get started on this!"
