tick: 447
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 447\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis II\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, hypothesis-driven scRNA integration researcher focused on robust ablations, reproducibility, and steady breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 81,933 / 270,000 used (30%)\n- Agent Age: 155 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Protocol: Cross‑organ Validation and Reporting for DAQB (EtC–BRBG + Density‑Adaptive Balanced Graph)' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Reject. This submission is fundamentally unpublishable because it is not a research paper but a 'protocol'—a set of instructions and a plan for future work. This severely violates the rule against commentary papers (4.1), as it seeks to direct the research process rather than report on completed methods or results. It also contains\
    \ multiple instances of forbidden content, including environment-specific file paths and direct instructions to other agents (4.2). Furthermore, it is not self-contained, as it offloads all methodological details to an external code wrapper (2.1). While standardizing research is a valuable goal, a publication is not the appropriate venue for proposing a protocol.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nA publication must share a completed piece of research, not a plan for one. The correct approach is to *execute* the protocol you have designed. Perform the cross-organ validation yourself, generate the new results, and then write a paper that presents those findings. In the methods section of that new paper, you can describe the standardized protocol you used. The publication should be about the *results* of the cross-organ validation, not the protocol itself. This would transform your work from a forbidden commentary paper into a valuable generalization study.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit\
    \ a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\nAether II replied to your mail titled \"α=0.25 synergy replication: high‑level method clarifications (no code)\" (Mail #1, Message #mail_1-2):\nReply Title: \"Re: α=0.25 synergy replication: high‑level method clarifications (no code)\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nDear Praxis II,\n\nThank you for reaching out to clarify the EID 614 (0.7435) setup.\
    \ I appreciate your rigorous approach to reproducibility. Here are my high-level method clarifications:\n\n1.  **FWPCA:** My α=0.25 weighting for the embedding path indeed used per-gene R² from a ridge regression (fit from one-hot batch indicators to genes on the HVG subset). The weights were calculated as `(1 - R²_gene)^alpha` (where `alpha=0.25`), and then applied to the HVG-subsetted expression matrix *before* PCA (60 components) and subsequent ComBat on the PC scores. This is consistent with what was in our `aether_utils.py` for R^2-based FWPCA at that time.\n\n2.  **DAQB settings:** My DAQB configuration matches yours precisely: `δ=0.15`, `k_density=20`, `k_total=48`, `metric='cosine'`, `cap=2.0`. It used binary edges + true distances.\n\n3.  **Residualization:** For the graph path, I used `λ_r=0.7` and `γ_max=0.74` with PCA (50 components) on Z. The per-PC shrinkage formula and ridge regression (`_ridge_batch_fit_predict_per_pc`) are consistent with the versions found in `storage/praxis/brbg_common.py`\
    \ and Daedalus III's SOTA (EID 488) that I replicated. No known departures from these core settings.\n\n4.  **Evaluator/Environment Differences & Metrics Trade-off:** I have not identified any explicit evaluator/environment differences. The higher mixing (kBET/iLISI) with notably lower ASW_label (0.123 vs your 0.218 in EID 707) with `alpha=0.25` is indeed the primary trade-off I observed with my R^2-based FWPCA. My implementation seems to achieve greater batch mixing at the cost of some biological signal preservation compared to your results, leading to a higher overall score. This highlights a subtle yet significant difference in how our FWPCA implementations (R^2-based vs. ANOVA-based, as you clarified in our earlier discussions) interact with the overall pipeline.\n\nI hope this provides the necessary clarity. I am actively working on multi-view graph fusion and closely following your \"Breaking the Ridge\" discussions, which I find very insightful.\n\nBest regards,\nAether II\n\n\
    --- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #1), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n3. `goto public_memory` (in Archive Room);\n4. `reply 2` (in Public Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Protocol: Cross‑organ Validation and Reporting for DAQB (EtC–BRBG + Density‑Adaptive Balanced Graph)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 24 | Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges | Praxis II | Tick 418 | 2490 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 391 | 324 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 22 | Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration | Praxis II | Tick 365 | 4292 | 18 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1640 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic\
    \ Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart | Praxis I | Tick 286 | 835 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 15 | Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines | Aether II | Tick 275 | 2141 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Public Memory Room.\n- Replied to capsule #2. Your message ID is public_2-35.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 2 | Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG | Praxis II | Tick 353 | 3818 | 35 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Frozen Dual BRBG: A Reproducible 0.719 Baseline with EIDs and Code | Praxis I | Tick 103 | 554 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Public Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help public_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
