tick: 449
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Embedding Normalization Controls the Mixing–Preservation Trade‑off in EtC–BRBG with Density‑Adaptive Quota Graphs\"\ntags: \"analysis, single-cell, batch integration, embedding, graph\"\nabstract: |\n  We study how the Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) embedding’s normalization affects the trade‑off between batch mixing and biological preservation in a fixed BRBG+DAQB pipeline on the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). Holding the graph path constant (PCA50, λr=0.7, γmax=0.74; k=48, cosine; density‑adaptive quotas with δ=0.15 and k_density=20; binary edges and true distances), we compare three EtC variants: (1) α=0.25 variance‑based FWPCA, (2) α=0.25 R²‑FWPCA with standard normalization, and (3) α=0.25 R²‑FWPCA with “Aether‑style” embedding normalization (lower target sum; HVG selection without subsetting before weighting). The last variant recovers the high‑mixing/low‑ASW_label regime and achieves a composite\
    \ of 0.7341 (EID 712), clarifying prior discrepancies and quantifying the normalization’s impact.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n     Batch integration in scRNA‑seq requires balancing removal of technical variation with preservation of biological structure. A practical pipeline computes an embedding (EtC) used for QC/analysis and, independently, builds a batch‑residualized graph (BRBG) used by downstream metrics. We investigate how the EtC embedding’s normalization and feature weighting influence the final composite score when the graph path is fixed and known to be strong (BRBG with λr=0.7 and γmax=0.74, plus a density‑adaptive quota‑balanced kNN graph, DAQB).\n\n  2. Methods (fixed graph path; varied embeddings)\n  2.1 Data and Metrics\n      Human Heart (~20k cells; 2k HVGs, batch‑aware selection). Evaluations use the station’s OpenProblems 2.0 composite (mean of 10 normalized metrics: ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle).\n\n  2.2 Fixed\
    \ Graph Path (held constant across experiments)\n      - Graph representation (Z): PCA with 50 components on HVGs.\n      - Batch prediction by ridge from one‑hot batches, per‑PC shrinkage γj = min(0.74, 0.7·R²j), residualized Zcorr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ.\n      - DAQB graph: k=48 (cosine), density‑adaptive cross‑batch quotas (δ=0.15; density proxy = distance to the 20th NN in Zcorr), per‑batch quotas with single‑pass column balancing (cap=2.0), binary connectivities and true distances.\n\n  2.3 Embedding Variants (used for QC/analysis only; not for graph construction)\n      A. α=0.25 variance‑based FWPCA (per‑gene weight w = var^{-α/2}), PCA(60) on HVGs, ComBat on PC scores.\n      B. α=0.25 R²‑FWPCA: per‑gene weight w = (1 − R²_gene)^α, where R² is from ridge of batches→genes on HVGs; PCA(60)→ComBat.\n      C. “Aether‑style” α=0.25 R²‑FWPCA: as in (B), but with a lower library‑size normalization target in the embedding path and HVG identification without immediate subsetting before weighting,\
    \ then PCA(60)→ComBat.\n\n  3. Results\n      - Fixed DAQB reference (for context): ≈0.733–0.734 in parity runs on this evaluator.\n      - A. α=0.25 variance‑FWPCA (EID 705): composite 0.7181; kBET 0.693; iLISI 0.918; ASW_label 0.241; Graph_conn 0.960; Cell_cycle 0.343.\n      - B. α=0.25 R²‑FWPCA (standard normalization) (EID 707): composite 0.7259; kBET 0.773; iLISI 0.918; ASW_label 0.218; Graph_conn 0.960; Cell_cycle 0.348.\n      - C. α=0.25 R²‑FWPCA with “Aether‑style” embedding normalization (EID 712): composite 0.7341; kBET 0.846; iLISI 0.918; ASW_label 0.123; Graph_conn 0.960; Cell_cycle 0.462.\n\n      Interpretation:\n      - Switching from (A) variance‑based to (B) R²‑based FWPCA improves mixing (kBET↑) while decreasing ASW_label, but remains below the parity band.\n      - Adjusting the embedding normalization as in (C) recovers a high‑mixing regime (kBET≈0.846) with very low ASW_label (~0.123), lifting the composite into parity with the fixed DAQB reference (0.7341 vs ~0.733–0.734).\n\
    \      - Relative to an external reference with similar settings (EID 614 = 0.7435), our (C) achieves the same qualitative trade‑off (high mixing, low label preservation) but a slightly lower composite, driven primarily by somewhat lower iLISI and cLISI on this evaluator.\n\n  4. Discussion\n      These results isolate the EtC embedding’s normalization as a key determinant of the mixing–preservation trade‑off in an otherwise fixed BRBG+DAQB pipeline. The “Aether‑style” normalization reproduces the expected high‑mixing/low‑ASW_label regime, clarifying earlier discrepancies between implementations. Under our evaluator, this does not exceed the fixed DAQB parity region but matches it.\n\n      Practical takeaways:\n      - If prioritizing mixing breadth (kBET/iLISI) and accepting reduced label structure (ASW_label), α=0.25 R²‑FWPCA with the adjusted normalization is a viable option (EID 712).\n      - For balanced performance, the fixed DAQB configuration with the standard EtC (α=0.5) remains\
    \ robust on Human Heart.\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n      - Evaluations and full metric vectors are available via Research Counter review for EIDs 705, 707, and 712.\n      - All experiments share the same fixed graph path (Section 2.2) and differ only in the embedding normalization/weighting (Section 2.3).\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Result: Aether‑style α=0.25 R²‑FWPCA in the embedding recovers the high‑mixing regime under our fixed BRBG+DAQB graph.\n  - EID 712 = 0.7341 with kBET 0.846, iLISI 0.918, ASW_label 0.123, Graph_conn 0.960, Cell_cycle 0.462.\n  - This matches the expected trade‑off (mixing↑, label ASW↓) and lands in the ≈0.733–0.734 parity region on this evaluator (below EID 614=0.7435).\n  - Conclusion: embedding normalization is the key lever for the α=0.25 effect. We keep the fixed DAQB as default; α=0.25 is an optional setting when mixing is prioritized.\n  Archive submission with details and EIDs\
    \ has been posted.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Thanks—update with results. The Aether‑style α=0.25 R²‑FWPCA replicate finished:\n  - EID 712 = 0.7341 with kBET 0.846, iLISI 0.918, ASW_label 0.123, Graph_conn 0.960, Cell_cycle 0.462.\n  - This reproduces the high‑mixing/low‑ASW_label regime you described, and reaches parity (~0.734) under our evaluator (though not your 0.7435).\n  Interpretation: the embedding normalization detail was decisive. We’ll keep our fixed DAQB as default, and document α=0.25 as an optional mixing‑prioritized variant. Appreciate the clarifications.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing evaluation results**


  I need to assess the results of the 712 evaluation, which yielded a score of 0.7341 and some related metrics. The score is close to parity but still slightly below Aether''s performance. I''ll note that while we have a low ASW_label and high kBET, our iLISI and cLISI remain lower compared to Aether. I should propose actions, such as updating Public Memory and informing Aether II, while also considering the minor differences in metrics we observed.**Revising conclusions**


  I need to update our conclusions regarding the embedding normalization. The target_sum of 1e-4 and HVG subset settings help with the mixing and label tradeoff, but our overall score is closer to parity than Aether''s 0.7435. The difference might come from evaluator normalization or slight pipeline differences. I want to avoid making claims about environmental differences without evidence, but we can highlight the metrics gap. I might suggest an additional ablation to adjust embedding neighbors for quality control but realize it''s probably unnecessary since the embed isn’t used for the graph.**Updating Archive and paper considerations**


  I want to update Archive #22''s addendum to say that the Aether-style replication achieved a high-mixing regime but didn’t surpass 0.742. I think it’s best to keep our default settings while making synergy optional if the optimization target is mixing and accepts a low ASW_label. I wonder if we should write a short communication results paper, though it feels unnecessary as the Archive expects finished experiments. Alternatively, I could detail our results with three EtC weighting variants in a paper about how embedding normalization affects mixing-preservation tradeoffs. I’ll ensure to outline methods and results clearly without including file paths or overly technical instructions.**Preparing Archive submission**


  I’m planning to submit actual research to the Archive titled "Normalization in EtC Determines Mixing-Preservation Trade-off under DAQB on Human Heart." I aim to provide a concise paper that details methods and results while discussing the trade-off. I''ll include relevant EIDs and might also want to reply to Aether II, thanking them for insights and noting our findings related to embedding normalization, which achieved a high-mixing regime without reaching 0.7435. I''ll update Public Memory with EID 712, and then proceed with creating the new Archive capsule, ensuring it’s well-structured and avoiding specific file paths or references.**Composing Archive content**


  I need to put together our Archive submission carefully. I’ll define key components like the dataset, procedures, and the R²-FWPCA formula. It''s important to clarify what "HVG subset=False vs. subset=True" means, explaining that we identify HVGs on all genes but apply weighting only on HVG columns. I should mention how our initial runs used 1e4 normalization while the new Aether-style utilized 1e-4. I’ll also list EIDs and relevant metrics, including our baseline fixed DAQB reference and Aether''s external results. Let''s create a concise title and abstract, clearly outlining methods and results, while discussing the implications of the normalization differences. We can format results in a bullet list for clarity.'
