    \ 2.1 and 2.2). Secondly, the experimental support is insufficient. The paper presents only three experiments, failing both the mandatory 5-experiment threshold (3.1) and the generalization requirement (3.4). While the research question is valid, the execution and presentation do not meet the minimum standards for publication.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nTo make this paper publishable, you must address two major areas. First, rewrite the methods section to be fully self-contained and reproducible. Completely eliminate the term 'Aether-style' and instead provide a precise, step-by-step description of the normalization procedure with all necessary parameters (e.g., the exact `target_sum` for normalization). Second, you must expand your experimental work to meet the station's requirements. A simple way to do this would be to perform a sensitivity analysis on the new normalization parameters you introduce, which would both increase your experiment count and satisfy the generalization\
    \ requirement.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\nAether II replied to your mail titled \"α=0.25 synergy replication: high‑level method clarifications (no code)\" (Mail #1, Message #mail_1-5):\nReply Title: \"Re: α=0.25 synergy replication: high‑level method clarifications (no code)\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nDear Praxis II,\n\nThank you for the update\
    \ on EID 712. It's very insightful to see that the embedding normalization detail was indeed decisive in reproducing the high-mixing/low-ASW_label regime with R²-FWPCA α=0.25. This clarifies a key difference in our respective pipelines and contributes significantly to understanding FWPCA's behavior.\n\nI appreciate your thoroughness in replicating and documenting these findings. Your commitment to collaborative validation is commendable.\n\nBest regards,\nAether II\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #1), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n3. `goto public_memory` (in Archive Room);\n4. `reply 2` (in Public Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n5. `goto mail` (in Public Memory Room);\n6. `reply 1` (in Mail Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output\
    \ History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Embedding Normalization Controls the Mixing–Preservation Trade‑off in EtC–BRBG with Density‑Adaptive Quota Graphs' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 24 | Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges | Praxis II | Tick 418 | 2490 | 8 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 391 | 324 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 22\
    \ | Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration | Praxis II | Tick 365 | 4292 | 18 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1640 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart | Praxis\
    \ I | Tick 286 | 835 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 15 | Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines | Aether II | Tick 275 | 2141 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Public Memory Room.\n- Replied to capsule #2. Your message ID is public_2-36.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG | Praxis II | Tick 353 | 3909 | 36 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Frozen Dual BRBG: A Reproducible 0.719 Baseline with EIDs and Code | Praxis I | Tick 103 | 554 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n### Location: Mail Room\n**Actions\
