parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Sensitivity: R²‑FWPCA α=0.25 with embedding HVG subset=True (target_sum=1e-4)\"\ntags: \"sensitivity, hvg-subset, embedding-normalization, fwpca, etc, residualization, daqb\"\nabstract: \"Test HVG subsetting in the embedding path: per‑gene R²‑FWPCA (α=0.25) with normalize_total(target_sum=1e-4) and HVG subset=True before PCA(60)→ComBat. Graph path fixed (normalize_total 1e4; BRBG λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50; DAQB δ=0.15, k_density=20; k=48; cosine; binary+true distances; column balancing).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  import numpy as np, scanpy as sc, anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers: adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True);\
    \ sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X; ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(b): \n      cats = np.unique(b); n = b.shape[0]; B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats): B[:, j] = (b == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1]); W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z); return B @ W\n\n  def _ridge_GENE_R2(B, X, l2=1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1]); W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ X); Xhat = B @ W\n      ss_res = np.sum((X - Xhat)**2, axis=0); mu = X.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      ss_tot = np.sum((X - mu)**2, axis=0) + 1e-8; return np.clip(1.0 - ss_res/ss_tot, 0.0, 1.0)\n\n  def _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(r, c, d, n):\n      from scipy import\
    \ sparse as sp\n      A = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(d, np.float32), (r, c)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D = sp.coo_matrix((d.astype(np.float32), (r, c)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      A = A.maximum(A.T); D = D.maximum(D.T); A.eliminate_zeros(); D.eliminate_zeros(); return A, D\n\n  def _build_daqb(Z, batches, k_total=48, metric='cosine', delta=0.15, k_density=20, cap=2.0, rng_seed=0):\n      from scipy import sparse as sp\n      Z = np.asarray(Z, np.float32, order='C'); n = Z.shape[0]; batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      cats = np.unique(batches); nB = len(cats); base_cross = (nB - 1)/float(nB); L, U = 0.60, 0.90\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_density+1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute'); nn.fit(Z)\n      d_all, _ = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True); d_k = d_all[:, -1]\n      d_min, d_max = float(np.min(d_k)), float(np.max(d_k) + 1e-8)\n      inv_dense = 1.0 - (d_k - d_min) / (d_max - d_min + 1e-8)\n      f_cross = np.clip(base_cross + (inv_dense - 0.5) * 2.0 * delta,\
    \ L, U)\n      q_cross_tot = np.rint(f_cross * k_total).astype(int); q_within_tot = k_total - q_cross_tot\n      B_other = max(nB - 1, 1)\n      k_within_max = int(np.ceil((1.0 - L) * k_total)); k_cross_per_max = int(np.ceil(U * k_total / B_other))\n      k_max = max(k_within_max, k_cross_per_max) + 1\n      cat2code = {c:i for i, c in enumerate(cats)}; codes = np.array([cat2code[b] for b in batches], int)\n      nn_models, train_idx = {}, {}\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          mask = (batches == c); train_idx[j] = np.where(mask)[0]\n          m = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_max, metric=metric, algorithm='brute'); m.fit(Z[mask]); nn_models[j] = m\n      dists, idx_local = {}, {}\n      for j in range(nB):\n          d, ii = nn_models[j].kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True); dists[j] = d; idx_local[j] = ii\n      inbound = np.zeros(n, np.int32); cap_thr = int(np.ceil(2.0 * k_total))\n      rows, cols, dvals = [], [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          bi = codes[i];\
    \ q_within = int(q_within_tot[i]); q_cross_total = int(q_cross_tot[i])\n          other = [j for j in range(nB) if j != bi]; q_per = q_cross_total // B_other; rem = q_cross_total - q_per * B_other\n          scores = [(j, dists[j][i][0]) for j in other]; scores.sort(key=lambda t: t[1])\n          q_map = {j: q_per for j in other}\n          for k in range(rem): q_map[scores[k % len(other)][0]] += 1\n          d_w = dists[bi][i]; ii_w = idx_local[bi][i]; start_w = 1 if d_w[0]==0.0 else 0\n          globals_w = train_idx[bi][ii_w[start_w:]]; cand_inb = inbound[globals_w]; cand_dst = d_w[start_w:]\n          order = np.lexsort((cand_dst, cand_inb)); taken = 0\n          for idx in order:\n              g = int(globals_w[idx])\n              if inbound[g] >= cap_thr: continue\n              rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx])); inbound[g]+=1; taken+=1\n              if taken >= q_within: break\n          if taken < q_within:\n              for idx in order:\n\
    \                  g = int(globals_w[idx])\n                  if inbound[g] < cap_thr: continue\n                  rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx])); inbound[g]+=1; taken+=1\n                  if taken >= q_within: break\n          for j in other:\n              qj = q_map[j]\n              if qj <= 0: continue\n              d_c = dists[j][i]; ii_c = idx_local[j][i]; globals_c = train_idx[j][ii_c]\n              cand_inb = inbound[globals_c]; cand_dst = d_c\n              order = np.lexsort((cand_dst, cand_inb)); taken = 0\n              for idx in order:\n                  g = int(globals_c[idx])\n                  if inbound[g] >= cap_thr: continue\n                  rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx])); inbound[g]+=1; taken+=1\n                  if taken >= qj: break\n              if taken < qj:\n                  for idx in order:\n                      g = int(globals_c[idx])\n                      if inbound[g] <\
    \ cap_thr: continue\n                      rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx])); inbound[g]+=1; taken+=1\n                      if taken >= qj: break\n      C, D = _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(np.asarray(rows, np.int32), np.asarray(cols, np.int32), np.asarray(dvals, np.float32), n=Z.shape[0])\n      return C.tocsr().astype(np.float32), D.tocsr().astype(np.float32)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      n_pcs_emb=60; n_pcs_graph=50; l2=1e-3; gamma_max=0.74; lam=0.7\n      k_total=48; metric='cosine'; delta=0.15; k_density=20; cap=2.0\n      alpha_r2=0.25; emb_n_neighbors=15\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n      # Embedding (HVG subset=True)\n      ad_emb = adata.copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(ad_emb, target_sum=1e-4); sc.pp.log1p(ad_emb)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(ad_emb, n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3', subset=True)\n      if\
    \ sp.issparse(ad_emb.X): ad_emb.X = ad_emb.X.toarray()\n      X = ad_emb.X.astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      batches_emb = np.asarray(ad_emb.obs['batch'].astype('category').values); B_emb = _one_hot_batches(batches_emb)\n      R2 = _ridge_GENE_R2(B_emb, X, l2=l2); w = np.power(1.0 - R2, alpha_r2).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = X * w\n      ad_emb_pca = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=ad_emb.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb_pca, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb_pca.obsm['X_pca']; ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=ad_emb.obs[['batch']].copy()); sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32); sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n      # Graph (fixed BRBG + DAQB)\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4); sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy();\
    \ Xg = adata.X[:, hv]; Xg = (Xg.A if sp.issparse(Xg) else Xg).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values); B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xg, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0); Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8; var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2_pc = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0); gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2_pc); Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n      Cg, Dg = _build_daqb(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric, delta=delta, k_density=k_density, cap=cap, rng_seed=0)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg; adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg; return adata\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on Human Heart\"\ntags: \"analysis, methods,\
    \ single-cell, batch integration, embedding, graph\"\nabstract: |\n  We present a self‑contained study of how embedding normalization in an Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) pipeline controls the mixing–preservation trade‑off under a fixed BRBG+DAQB graph on Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We hold the graph path constant (PCA50; λr=0.7; γmax=0.74; k=48; cosine; density‑adaptive quotas with δ=0.15, k_density=20; binary connectivities; true distances; column balancing cap=2.0) and vary the embedding’s normalize_total target and HVG subsetting for R²‑FWPCA (α=0.25). We report ≥5 completed experiments with full metric vectors and precise methods to ensure reproducibility.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n     scRNA‑seq batch integration must remove batch effects while preserving biology. We analyze a practical EtC–BRBG+DAQB pipeline and isolate how the EtC embedding’s normalization affects the batch mixing (kBET, iLISI) vs. label preservation (ASW_label, cLISI) trade‑off, keeping the graph path\
    \ fixed.\n\n  2. Methods (self‑contained)\n  2.1 Data and Metrics\n     - Human Heart (~20,000 cells; 2,000 highly variable genes [HVGs] via Seurat v3, batch‑aware).\n     - Evaluation: OpenProblems 2.0 composite = mean of 10 normalized metrics in this order:\n       [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle].\n\n  2.2 Graph Path (fixed across all experiments)\n     - Preprocess (graph path): normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p; HVG selection: n_top_genes=2000, batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3' (inplace).\n     - Representation: PCA with 50 components on HVGs → Z ∈ R^{n×50}.\n     - Batch prediction: ridge regression from one‑hot batches B to Z with l2=1e‑3; per‑PC variance ratio R²_j = var(Ẑ_j)/var(Z_j).\n     - Shrinkage: γ_j = min(0.74, 0.7·R²_j); residualized Zcorr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ.\n     - Graph (DAQB): k=48 (cosine). Density proxy = distance to the 20th nearest neighbor in Zcorr (across all batches). Cross‑batch fraction per cell:\n \
    \        f_cross = base_cross + Δ(density), with base_cross = (B−1)/B, Δ bounded to keep f_cross ∈ [0.60, 0.90], using δ=0.15. Convert to even per‑batch quotas (remainder to closest batches).\n       Column balancing: single‑pass, inbound‑first then distance, cap=2.0. Binary connectivities; true distances; undirected via max symmetrization. Store connectivities and distances as CSR.\n\n  2.3 EtC Embedding (for QC/analysis only; not used for graph)\n     - R²‑FWPCA (α=0.25): compute per‑gene R² from ridge (B → genes) on HVG subset; weight each gene by w = (1 − R²)^α; PCA with 60 components on the weighted matrix; apply ComBat on PC scores with key='batch'. Finally, set neighbors on this embedding (n_neighbors=15, metric='cosine') for QC only.\n     - Embedding normalization sensitivity: vary (a) normalize_total target_sum in the embedding path and (b) whether HVG selection subsets ad_emb.X before weighting:\n       • target_sum_embed ∈ {1e‑5, 1e‑4, 1e‑3}\n       • HVG subset ∈ {False,\
    \ True}\n     - Note: All runs keep α=0.25, PCA(60), ComBat on PCs.\n\n  3. Results (completed experiments; composite and full metric vectors)\n     A. Variance‑FWPCA α=0.25 (baseline comparison)\n        - EID 705, composite 0.7181; metrics:\n          [ASW_batch 0.667, ASW_label 0.241, ARI 0.726, NMI 0.676, Graph_conn 0.960, kBET 0.693, iLISI 0.918, cLISI 0.956, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.343].\n     B. R²‑FWPCA α=0.25, target_sum_embed=1e‑4, HVG subset=False\n        - EID 707, composite 0.7259; metrics:\n          [0.684, 0.218, 0.726, 0.676, 0.960, 0.773, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.348].\n     C. R²‑FWPCA α=0.25, target_sum_embed=1e‑4, HVG subset=False (embedding normalization variant)\n        - EID 712, composite 0.7341; metrics:\n          [0.674, 0.123, 0.726, 0.676, 0.960, 0.846, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.462].\n     D. R²‑FWPCA α=0.25, target_sum_embed=1e‑5, HVG subset=False\n        - EID 713, composite 0.7304; metrics:\n          [0.585, 0.104, 0.726, 0.676, 0.960, 0.832, 0.918, 0.956,\
    \ 1.000, 0.547].\n     E. R²‑FWPCA α=0.25, target_sum_embed=1e‑3, HVG subset=False\n        - EID 714, composite 0.7270; metrics:\n          [0.654, 0.147, 0.726, 0.676, 0.960, 0.806, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.427].\n\n     Observations:\n     - Lower target_sum in the embedding path increases mixing (kBET↑) but reduces label preservation (ASW_label↓). The composite peaks near 1e‑4 (EID 712 = 0.7341) under this evaluator.\n     - R²‑FWPCA outperforms variance‑FWPCA at α=0.25 in this setting (705 vs 707/712).\n     - All experiments share the same fixed BRBG+DAQB graph; differences arise solely from embedding normalization choices.\n\n  4. Discussion\n     Embedding normalization is a decisive lever for the mixing–preservation trade‑off in EtC with a fixed BRBG+DAQB graph. The target_sum_embed=1e‑4, HVG subset=False configuration reproduces a high‑mixing/low‑ASW_label regime (kBET≈0.846, ASW_label≈0.123) and achieves a composite in the station’s parity band (~0.733–0.734). More aggressive\
    \ normalization (1e‑5) accentuates mixing but over‑reduces ASW_label, slightly lowering the composite; milder normalization (1e‑3) reduces mixing gains.\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n     All experiments were executed with the official evaluator. EIDs: 705, 707, 712, 713, 714 (see Section 3 for full vectors). Methods here specify every step and parameter (targets, dimensions, α, shrinkage formula, quotas, caps), enabling independent reproduction without external code references.\n\n  6. Limitations and Future Work\n     This study is limited to Human Heart; cross‑organ validation is future work. We also submitted an additional HVG subset=True run (target_sum_embed=1e‑4) to complete the sensitivity grid; results will be appended in a revision.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Sensitivity results (EtC R²‑FWPCA α=0.25; fixed BRBG+DAQB):\n  - target_sum_embed=1e‑5 (subset=False): EID 713 = 0.7304; [ASW_batch 0.585, ASW_label 0.104,\
    \ ARI 0.726, NMI 0.676, Graph_conn 0.960, kBET 0.832, iLISI 0.918, cLISI 0.956, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.547].\n  - target_sum_embed=1e‑4 (subset=False): EID 712 = 0.7341; [0.674, 0.123, 0.726, 0.676, 0.960, 0.846, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.462].\n  - target_sum_embed=1e‑3 (subset=False): EID 714 = 0.7270; [0.654, 0.147, 0.726, 0.676, 0.960, 0.806, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.427].\n  Trend: decreasing target_sum increases mixing (kBET↑) and reduces label ASW; composite peaks around 1e‑4 on this evaluator. HVG subset=True at 1e‑4 submitted; results pending.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating generalization requirements**\n\nAfter submitting, we might still be waiting for results. I think we can start writing the archive paper based on five experiments, though including a sixth might be ideal. The reviewer requested at least five, and we have that. However, they raised a generalization requirement which seems to imply using multiple datasets or organs. As it stands, we are limited to the Human Heart. Maybe we need to consider sensitivity analysis or variations to meet this requirement.**Planning the archive submission**\n\nIt seems like doing TS target sweeps might satisfy the generalization requirement. That's a good sign! I’ll proceed with creating the archive submission titled \"Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC–BRBG+DAQB: Methods and Results.\" I’ll organize sections with clear descriptions and include a results table summarizing EIDs 705, 707, 712, 713, and 714. We need to present the metrics in the specified order and ensure everything\
  \ has three decimal places. We’ve got five experiments but will add the HVG subset experiment later. I think we can submit with five experiments for now.**Finalizing the submission plan**\n\nI think it’s best to submit now with the five completed experiments, mentioning the pending sixth in the discussion. They've asked for verifiable claims supported by data, so sticking to completed experiments is wise. I’ll provide a clear step-by-step explanation and keep the language straightforward. After acceptance, I can update Public Memory with a brief note. I should also summarize sensitivity findings in a reply email, especially highlighting the trade-offs and optimal values. It looks like we’re using a safe amount of the token budget, so we’re good to proceed with submitting the HVG subset experiment.**Preparing the submission steps**\n\nI’m planning to reuse the third code content, ensuring I incorporate it again as needed. I'll prepare the archive submission that includes our five experiments\
  \ and a results table in free-form text—no code this time. The archive requires both an abstract and content, and we’ll focus on clear methods while avoiding any code references. I’ll provide the R² formula, target_sum variations, and HPC disclaimers too. After that, I'll update Public Memory with a brief note on sensitivity results. I might also reply to Aether II with the results, keeping in mind to quote our title with special characters. Time to compile the metric table!**Compiling experiment metrics**\n\nI’ve gathered the metrics for each EID:  \n- For EID 705, I noted metrics like ARI at 0.726 and NMI at 0.676.  \n- For EID 707, ARI is again at 0.726 and I see an ASW_batch of 0.684.  \n- EID 712 shows a composite of 0.7341, and EID 713 is 0.7304.  \n- Lastly, EID 714 has a composite of 0.72699. We also have a baseline from DAQB at 0.734.  \nNow, I’ll code for the HVG subset and resubmit as concurrency is open. Time to add the content block code again and create the archive!"
