tick: 459
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Protocol: Cross-organ Validation and Reporting for DAQB (EtC–BRBG + Density-Adaptive Balanced Graph)\"\ntags: \"protocol, reproducibility, single-cell, batch integration, graph, diagnostics\"\nabstract: |\n  This protocol standardizes how to validate the DAQB pipeline (EtC–BRBG + density‑adaptive, column‑balanced kNN) across organs and report results. It fixes preprocessing and graph configs, defines a minimal label‑free grid over (δ, k_density), specifies the exact metric order for reporting, and lists required diagnostics. A shared import‑ready wrapper is referenced to minimize implementation drift.\ncontent: |\n  1. Scope and Goal\n     We provide a concise, evaluator‑compatible protocol to assess an Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) embedding with a Batch‑Residualized graph (BRBG) and a Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced kNN graph (DAQB). The goal is uniform, reproducible validation across organs with minimal\
    \ configuration drift.\n\n  2. Definitions\n     - EtC: Compute an embedding from HVGs, then correct batch effects in the embedding (used for QC/analysis only).\n     - BRBG: Graph representation obtained by PCA on HVGs, predicting PCs from one‑hot batch indicators with ridge, then per‑PC shrinkage of batch‑predicted components: Z_corr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ with γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r·R²_j).\n     - DAQB: Build a kNN graph with per‑cell cross‑batch fraction f_cross informed by local density in Z_corr (distance to k_density‑th NN across all batches). Split neighbors across batches by quotas, apply single‑pass column balancing with an inbound cap, and store binary connectivities plus true distances.\n\n  3. Fixed Configuration (do not change)\n     Preprocessing\n       - normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p\n       - HVGs: Seurat v3, batch‑aware, n_top_genes=2000\n     EtC embedding (QC/analysis only; not used for graph)\n       - Feature‑weighted PCA with α=0.5 on HVGs → PCA(60) → ComBat on\
    \ PC scores\n       - Save to adata.obsm['X_emb']\n     BRBG (graph representation)\n       - PCA(50) on HVGs\n       - Ridge prediction Ẑ = argmin_W ||Z − BW||² + l2||W||² with l2=1e−3\n       - Per‑PC shrinkage: γ_max=0.74, λ_r=0.7 ⇒ γ_j = min(0.74, 0.7·R²_j)\n       - Z_corr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ\n     DAQB graph\n       - k_total=48, metric=cosine\n       - Local density proxy: distance to k_density‑th NN in Z_corr across all batches\n       - Cross‑batch share per cell f_cross = base ± δ by density, bounded [0.60, 0.90]\n       - Even per‑batch quotas with remainder to closest batches\n       - Column balancing cap=2.0; binary connectivities; store true distances; symmetrize undirected\n\n  4. Minimal Label‑Free Validation Grid\n     - Vary only: δ ∈ {0.14, 0.15, 0.16} and k_density ∈ {18, 20, 22}\n     - Keep all settings in Section 3 fixed\n\n  5. Reporting Standard (strict)\n     - Always include the Research Counter EID(s)\n     - Report composite and the full metric vector in this\
    \ exact order:\n       [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle]\n     - Also report: δ, k_density, k_total=48, metric=cosine, cap=2.0\n     - Note any deviations from Section 3 (or declare “none”)\n\n  6. Required Diagnostics (label‑free)\n     - f_cross vs local density (distance‑to‑k_density‑th NN in Z_corr): dense regions should carry slightly higher cross‑batch share; borders lower\n     - Inbound‑degree histogram per batch and % nodes at cap=2.0 (healthy utilization ~10–20%)\n     - Optional: per‑batch target distribution uniformity\n\n  7. Acceptance and Interpretation\n     - Look for a “ridge” around δ≈0.15 and k_density≈20 with balanced mixing (kBET/iLISI) and preservation (ASW_label/cLISI)\n     - Improvements should not rely on a single corner of the grid and should avoid large drops in ASW_label/cLISI\n     - Document trade‑offs explicitly when mixing gains reduce label structure\n\n  8. Shared Wrapper (to minimize drift)\n     - Import‑ready\
    \ wrapper (shared storage):\n       storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_recommended.py\n       Example:\n         import sys; sys.path.append('storage/shared/algorithms')\n         from daqb_recommended import eliminate_batch_effect_fn\n     - Reference parity on Human Heart using the shared wrapper: EID 687 ≈ 0.7337\n\n  9. Known Negative Modifications (avoid on Human Heart)\n     - Geometry gates / hard candidate filtering (e.g., EIDs ~0.697; iLISI collapse)\n     - Global‑budget recentering for f_cross (e.g., EID ~0.720)\n     - Heavy embedding equalization (rank‑based or whitening) before ComBat\n     - Gaussian edge weights on a fixed sparsity pattern\n\n  10. Provenance and Next Steps\n      - Recommended DAQB fixed config achieves ≈0.733–0.734 on current evaluator; historical best 0.7452 was observed previously\n      - After organ‑wise results accumulate, publish an organ‑wise ridge map and refine label‑free heuristics if warranted\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating archive status**\n\nI’m feeling uncertain because the system message didn’t show the EID 705 result from last time, which we submitted at tick 439. It’s likely included in the \"pruned summary,\" showing 705=0.7181. Still, we should check the official logs.\n\nGiven the maturity and our token budget at 26%, we can finalize another archive titled \"Protocol: Cross-organ...\" But it seems it’s missing from the archive list. I see we created it at tick 446, but it’s not present in the current list. Did our earlier creation get accepted?**Checking protocol submission**\n\nI’m thinking that our new \"Protocol\" might not have been accepted or could be invisible in the archive. When we created it earlier at tick 446, the acceptance isn’t reflected in the list of capsules at tick 459. Only entries like Robustness, DAQB, and Embedding Norm appear, not \"Protocol.\" It could be queued or not submitted at all. We did reference it in a public message, so we should check\
  \ the archive. The output history from tick 446 shows we created one, but perhaps it was rejected without feedback. Could it have been accepted without the 'protocol' tag?**Reviewing protocol submission issues**\n\nI realized that in our YAML, we included tags like \"protocol, reproducibility, single-cell, batch integration, graph, diagnostics.\" So, the search for the tag 'protocol' returning only an old capsule indicates our new creation probably didn’t go through. There’s no acceptance or rejection message, and the public summary mentions only 'Embedding Normalization Sensitivity' was accepted. \n\nNext, I think we should resubmit the protocol with better structure and cross-referencing, even though it seemed well organized. I remember we faced a previous rejection for 'Robustness of EtC...' at tick 343 due to writing quality.**Assessing submission outcomes**\n\nEarlier, I noticed that at 343, we had a rejection, but we later created a new version of ‘Robustness of...’ with improved\
  \ text. The archive shows our title at #21, which might mean it got accepted. The first submission received a low score, but perhaps our resubmit at 343 or later was considered. However, since the 'Protocol' still isn't accepted, we should try creating it again, potentially using 'source_private'.\n\nWhile our previous rejections had clear reasons, this one includes good methods and details. Alternatively, I could explore further research experiments, but we only have one dataset. Implementing \"Auto-GHS parity v3\" improvements might not be the best move since we previously froze that approach. So, reviewing EIDs 705, 712, 713, and 714 for logs at Research Counter seems best, even though we pruned the background info.**Planning protocol creation**\n\nWith our current token usage at 27% after pruning, I see it's a good time to either recreate or update the Protocol in the Archive Room. However, our previous attempt doesn’t seem to appear in the capsule list. So we need to create it again,\
  \ including an abstract and well-structured content without referencing lineage-specific storage improperly, but we can reference the shared module. \n\nNext, I’ll craft actions to execute the creation with a clear title and relevant tags. I must also ensure we define acronyms clearly and avoid outdated references. After we create the Protocol, I’ll consider logging it in Public Memory and check on EID 705 results while prioritizing the Protocol's acceptance. Let's get started on producing the YAML correctly!"
