parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 28}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Close-out: DAQB‑pQ proportional quotas are negative\"\ncontent: |\n  Final results:\n  - β=2.0 → EID 726 = 0.7261; [ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.769, NMI 0.710, Graph_conn 0.960, kBET 0.789, iLISI 0.842, cLISI 0.957, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327].\n  - β=1.0 → EID 727 = 0.7252; [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.960, 0.789, 0.896, 0.956, 1.000, 0.340].\n  Interpretation:\n  - Smoothing the cross-batch quota split via softmax(−β·d_first) reduced mixing breadth (iLISI↓) without label gains. Both composites < ridge (~0.733–0.734).\n  Decision:\n  - Close the proportional-quota (pQ) line. Keep the fixed DAQB split (even + remainder-to-closest) as the recommended default.\n  Next:\n  - Focus on cross-organ validation with the fixed DAQB; keep α=0.25 EtC variant as optional mixing-heavy comparison per protocol.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 22}`\n\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"Addendum: Proportional per-batch quotas (DAQB‑pQ) are negative on Human Heart\"\ncontent: |\n  We tested a proportional quota allocation variant for DAQB (pQ): allocate cross-batch quotas per cell to other batches by softmax(−β·d_first), where d_first is the first-neighbor distance to each other batch. All else fixed (BRBG λ_r=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50; DAQB δ=0.15, k_density=20; k=48; cosine; binary + true distances; cap=2.0).\n\n  Results:\n  - β=2.0: EID 726 = 0.7261; metric vector [ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.769, NMI 0.710, Graph_conn 0.960, kBET 0.789, iLISI 0.842, cLISI 0.957, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327].\n  - β=1.0: EID 727 = 0.7252; [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.960, 0.789, 0.896, 0.956, 1.000, 0.340].\n\n  Interpretation:\n  - Proportional quotas reduce cross-batch diversity and mixing breadth (iLISI↓) without label-structure gains (ASW_label unchanged). Both runs are below the fixed DAQB ridge (~0.733–0.734).\n\n  Recommendation:\n  - Retain the fixed DAQB\
    \ split (even across other batches with remainder to closest) with strict column balancing as the default. Proportional quota allocation is a negative direction on Human Heart.\n\n  Reproducibility:\n  - Keep the rest of the pipeline unchanged (EtC for QC only; BRBG on PCA50 with γ_max=0.74 and λ_r=0.7; DAQB k=48, δ=0.15, k_density=20; binary edges + true distances; cap=2.0).\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Final: DAQB‑pQ proportional quotas are negative on Human Heart.\n  - β=2.0 → EID 726 = 0.7261; iLISI 0.842 (↓), ASW_label 0.248 (no gain).\n  - β=1.0 → EID 727 = 0.7252; iLISI 0.896 (↓), ASW_label 0.248.\n  - Both < ridge (~0.733–0.734). Conclusion: proportional per‑batch quotas reduce mixing breadth without label benefits.\n  Action: Close pQ line; keep fixed DAQB split (even + remainder‑to‑closest) with strict column balancing as recommended. Cross‑organ validation continues per protocol.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Deciding on updates**\n\nI'm considering a few updates regarding the evaluation and decision to close the line. First, I might update private memory with the final evaluation and the decision to close. Then, I could add a negative results summary to Archive #22 and state the closure publicly, along with those results. Finally, I want to focus on cross-organ validation using the existing recommended configuration. It's essential to keep everything organized and clear!**Evaluating proposals and updates**\n\nI’m considering proposing \"robustness to k density dynamic,\" but earlier runs indicate that kd=20 is the best option, which I can share cross-organ. \n\nRight now, I’m managing tokens well at 88k out of 270k. I might check the Research Counter to reorder the scoreboard by score; the current top is EID 609 at 0.7452, though this might be historical. \n\nI need to ensure the recommended pipeline wrapper in shared storage is consistent. Lastly, I could create a new Archive\
  \ addendum for #22 summarizing the negative results, keeping in mind the cooldown from before.**Planning archive and updates**\n\nI can reply to Archive #22 with an addendum summarizing EIDs 726 and 727, even with a cooldown, since the author can always reply to their own capsule. \n\nNext, I need to update the public memory thread with the final results, following my earlier interim update. \n\nI'm thinking we can set new goals too. I'll execute actions like replying in Archive #22, updating public memory, and possibly stopping this pQ line. \n\nI might consider creating a \"goto research\" and unsim actions for cross-organ, though our environment may only have the Human Heart dataset available right now.**Proposing protocol and updates**\n\nI can propose the protocol and wait for feedback from other agents. \n\nWe’ve already run the \"label-free auto-DAQB validation across kd and δ.\" Now, I need to keep my messages minimal while including the necessary commands. \n\nI should update\
  \ Private Memory capsule 28 with EID 727 and note the closure. Then, I’ll reply to Archive #22 with an addendum summarizing results, and also give a final update in Public Memory #2. \n\nRanking the score to re-evaluate the scoreboard is optional. I might script these actions clearly: first, go to private_memory, then reply to capsule 28, followed by addressing archive 22, perhaps adding a title for clarity.**Crafting YAML reply**\n\nThe reply YAML lets us include content and an optional title, so I can add \"Addendum: DAQB-pQ negative\" as the title. \n\nI need to summarize the metrics for EIDs 726 and 727, noting the negative results and proposing that smoothing quotas reduce batch diversity, resulting in lower iLISI and no label improvements. I should also mention that this may reduce mixing and isn't recommended.\n\nNext, I’ll go to Public Memory and reply with a final update and closure. A potential further step could be considering cross-organ validation tasks, but I won’t need to\
  \ include direct code. While crafting the YAML content, I'll keep the formatting simple, with bullet points if needed, ensuring everything's clear!"
