tick: 472
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Auto‑GHS v4 (Praxis): Fixed r‑center=0.30 label‑free tuning of DAQB (δ,k_density) with BRBG(λ=0.7)\"\ntags: \"auto-tuning, daqb, graph-health-score, label-free, residualization, embed-then-correct\"\nabstract: \"Label‑free selection of DAQB parameters on Human Heart using a Graph Health Score (GHS) with fixed r_center=0.30 for density–mixing coupling. We grid over δ∈{0.14,0.15,0.16} and k_density∈{18,20,22}, compute Zcorr via BRBG(λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50), build DAQB graphs (binary + true distances, cap=2.0, fixed remainder‑to‑closest quotas), score each candidate with GHS, and apply the best to the full graph. EtC (FWPCA α=0.5, PCA60→ComBat) is QC‑only.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  # ----------------------- Utilities -----------------------\n\
    \  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      n = batches.shape[0]\n      B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(rows,\
    \ cols, dists, n):\n      A = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D = sp.coo_matrix((np.asarray(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      A = A.maximum(A.T)\n      D = D.maximum(D.T)\n      A.eliminate_zeros(); D.eliminate_zeros()\n      return A, D\n\n  def _zscore(x, eps=1e-8):\n      x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float32)\n      return (x - x.mean()) / (x.std() + eps)\n\n  def _gini(x, eps=1e-8):\n      x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float64)\n      if x.size == 0:\n          return 0.0\n      mu = x.mean() + eps\n      diff = np.abs(x.reshape(-1,1) - x.reshape(1,-1))\n      return diff.mean() / (2.0 * mu)\n\n  # ----------------------- DAQB Builder (fixed split) -----------------------\n  def _build_daqb_fixed(\n      Zcorr: np.ndarray,\n      batches,\n      k_total: int = 48,\n      metric: str = 'cosine',\n      delta: float = 0.15,\n      k_density: int = 20,\n      cap: float = 2.0,\n      rng_seed:\
    \ int = 0\n  ):\n      \"\"\"\n      Density-Adaptive Quota-Balanced kNN (fixed split):\n        - f_cross set by local density (distance to k_density-th NN across all batches), bounded [0.60, 0.90]\n        - Even per-batch quotas for cross edges; remainder assigned to closest batches\n        - Single-pass column balancing via inbound cap (cap*k_total); binary edges + true distances; symmetric\n      \"\"\"\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(rng_seed)\n      Z = np.asarray(Zcorr, dtype=np.float32, order='C')\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      nB = len(cats)\n      base_cross = (nB - 1) / float(nB)\n      L, U = 0.60, 0.90\n\n      # All-batch density proxy (k_density + 1 to skip self)\n      nn_all = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_density + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute')\n      nn_all.fit(Z)\n      d_all, _ = nn_all.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      d_k = d_all[:, -1]\n      d_min, d_max = float(np.min(d_k)),\
    \ float(np.max(d_k) + 1e-8)\n      inv_dense = 1.0 - (d_k - d_min) / (d_max - d_min + 1e-8)\n      f_cross = np.clip(base_cross + (inv_dense - 0.5) * 2.0 * delta, L, U)\n      q_cross_tot = np.rint(f_cross * k_total).astype(int)\n      q_within_tot = k_total - q_cross_tot\n      B_other = max(nB - 1, 1)\n\n      # Neighbor list sizes (upper bounds)\n      k_within_max = int(np.ceil((1.0 - L) * k_total))\n      k_cross_per_max = int(np.ceil(U * k_total / B_other))\n      k_max = max(k_within_max, k_cross_per_max) + 1  # +1 to skip self\n\n      # Fit per-batch neighbor models\n      cat2code = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(cats)}\n      codes = np.array([cat2code[b] for b in batches], dtype=int)\n      nn_models, train_idx = {}, {}\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          mask = (batches == c)\n          train_idx[j] = np.where(mask)[0]\n          m = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_max, metric=metric, algorithm='brute')\n          m.fit(Z[mask])\n          nn_models[j] = m\n\n   \
    \   # Query precompute\n      dists, idx_local = {}, {}\n      for j in range(nB):\n          d, ii = nn_models[j].kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n          dists[j] = d; idx_local[j] = ii\n\n      inbound = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.int32)\n      cap_thr = int(np.ceil(cap * k_total))\n      rows, cols, dvals = [], [], []\n\n      for i in range(n):\n          bi = codes[i]\n          q_within = int(q_within_tot[i])\n          q_cross_total = int(q_cross_tot[i])\n          other = [j for j in range(nB) if j != bi]\n\n          # Even split + remainder to closest other batches (by first NN distance)\n          if q_cross_total > 0 and len(other) > 0:\n              q_per = q_cross_total // B_other\n              rem = q_cross_total - q_per * B_other\n              scores = [(j, dists[j][i][0]) for j in other]\n              scores.sort(key=lambda t: t[1])\n              q_map = {j: q_per for j in other}\n              for k in range(rem):\n                  q_map[scores[k % len(other)][0]]\
    \ += 1\n          else:\n              q_map = {j: 0 for j in other}\n\n          # Within-batch selection (cap-first, then distance)\n          d_w = dists[bi][i]; ii_w = idx_local[bi][i]\n          start_w = 1 if d_w[0] == 0.0 else 0\n          globals_w = train_idx[bi][ii_w[start_w:]]\n          cand_inb = inbound[globals_w]; cand_dst = d_w[start_w:]\n          order = np.lexsort((cand_dst, cand_inb))\n          taken = 0\n          for idx in order:\n              g = int(globals_w[idx])\n              if inbound[g] >= cap_thr:\n                  continue\n              rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx]))\n              inbound[g] += 1; taken += 1\n              if taken >= q_within: break\n          if taken < q_within:\n              for idx in order:\n                  g = int(globals_w[idx])\n                  if inbound[g] < cap_thr:\n                      continue\n                  rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx]))\n\
    \                  inbound[g] += 1; taken += 1\n                  if taken >= q_within: break\n\n          # Cross-batch selections\n          for j in other:\n              qj = q_map[j]\n              if qj <= 0: continue\n              d_c = dists[j][i]; ii_c = idx_local[j][i]\n              globals_c = train_idx[j][ii_c]\n              cand_inb = inbound[globals_c]; cand_dst = d_c\n              order = np.lexsort((cand_dst, cand_inb))\n              taken = 0\n              for idx in order:\n                  g = int(globals_c[idx])\n                  if inbound[g] >= cap_thr:\n                      continue\n                  rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx]))\n                  inbound[g] += 1; taken += 1\n                  if taken >= qj: break\n              if taken < qj:\n                  for idx in order:\n                      g = int(globals_c[idx])\n                      if inbound[g] < cap_thr:\n                          continue\n  \
    \                    rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx]))\n                      inbound[g] += 1; taken += 1\n                      if taken >= qj: break\n\n      C_sym, D_sym = _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(\n          np.asarray(rows, dtype=np.int32),\n          np.asarray(cols, dtype=np.int32),\n          np.asarray(dvals, dtype=np.float32),\n          n\n      )\n      return C_sym.astype(np.float32), D_sym.astype(np.float32), f_cross, d_k\n\n  # ----------------------- GHS (label-free) -----------------------\n  def _score_ghs(C: sp.csr_matrix, f_cross: np.ndarray, d_k: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray,\n                 r_center=0.30, cap_util_target=0.15, cap_util_sigma=0.05):\n      # Inputs: C binary undirected CSR; f_cross per node; d_k density distance; batches categorical\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      # Density–mixing coupling (positive correlation expected)\n      s = _zscore(-(d_k.astype(np.float32)))\n      r = float(np.corrcoef(f_cross.astype(np.float32),\
    \ s)[0,1])\n      score_r = np.exp(-((r - r_center) ** 2) / (2.0 * (0.10 ** 2)))  # prefer ~0.30 ± 0.10\n\n      # Cap utilization (fraction at inbound cap ~15% is healthy)\n      inbound = np.asarray(C.sum(axis=0)).ravel().astype(np.int32)\n      k_total = int(np.asarray(C.sum(axis=1)).ravel().max())  # ~48 after symmetrization; safe\n      cap_thr = int(np.ceil(2.0 * k_total))\n      cap_util = float((inbound >= cap_thr).mean())\n      score_cap = np.exp(-((cap_util - cap_util_target) ** 2) / (2.0 * (cap_util_sigma ** 2)))\n\n      # Cross-batch diversity: distinct other batches among neighbors\n      # Build neighbor lists\n      indptr, indices = C.indptr, C.indices\n      batch_codes = ad.Categorical(batches).codes\n      B_other = max(len(np.unique(batch_codes)) - 1, 1)\n      div = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float32)\n      for i in range(n):\n          nb = indices[indptr[i]:indptr[i+1]]\n          if nb.size == 0:\n              continue\n          unique_other = np.unique(batch_codes[nb][batch_codes[nb]\
    \ != batch_codes[i]]).size\n          div[i] = unique_other / float(B_other)\n      score_div = float(div.mean())\n\n      # Inbound uniformity (lower Gini is better)\n      g = _gini(inbound.astype(np.float64))\n      score_inb = float(1.0 - g)  # in [0,1]\n\n      # Weighted GHS\n      ghs = 0.4 * score_div + 0.3 * score_r + 0.2 * score_cap + 0.1 * score_inb\n      return float(ghs), {'r': r, 'cap_util': cap_util, 'score_div': score_div, 'score_inb': score_inb}\n\n  # ----------------------- Main Pipeline -----------------------\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Config\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      lam = 0.7\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      alpha_var = 0.5\n      emb_n_neighbors = 15\n      cap = 2.0\n\n      # Auto-GHS grid\n      deltas = [0.14, 0.15, 0.16]\n      kd_list = [18, 20, 22]\n      r_center = 0.30\n\n      assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and\
    \ 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # Normalize + HVGs\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n\n      # EtC embedding (QC only)\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n      Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata,\
    \ use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n\n      # Graph representation via BRBG\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      # Grid search over (delta, k_density) using label-free GHS\n      best = None\n      best_info = None\n      for delta in deltas:\n          for kd in kd_list:\n              Cc, Dc, f_cross, d_k = _build_daqb_fixed(Zcorr, batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                                       delta=delta, k_density=kd, cap=cap, rng_seed=0)\n              ghs, info = _score_ghs(Cc, f_cross, d_k, batches, r_center=r_center)\n              if (best is None) or\
    \ (ghs > best[0]):\n                  best = (ghs, delta, kd, Cc, Dc)\n                  best_info = info\n      ghs, best_delta, best_kd, C_best, D_best = best\n      print(f\"[Auto-GHS] Selected delta={best_delta}, k_density={best_kd}, GHS={ghs:.4f}, info={best_info}\")\n\n      # Write best graph\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C_best\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = D_best\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**Implementing code for density computation**\n\nI’m planning to implement a function in the submission content while also creating a module for low token usage. This function should eliminate batch effects and could optionally include a debugging test. \n\nMy focus is on using GHS selection to compute neighbors and density, as well as evaluating various metrics for the graph's health score. I’ll consider aspects like density correlation and inbound degree uniformity to develop a plausible method for comparison.**Defining graph metrics and calculations**\n\nI'm going to define several calculations for our graph. First, I’ll compute kd-proxy based on the distances to the kd-th neighbor, and then define density as the negative z-score of that distance. \n\nNext, I'll determine f_cross_i using a base value adjusted by the normalized density, with limits between 0.60 and 0.90. After building the graph for each candidate, I’ll compute batch mixing and density coupling metrics\
  \ while also assessing cap utilization to get a Graph Health Score (GHS) using a weighted sum.**Defining metrics for graph analysis**\n\nI’m going to define several metrics to analyze cross-batch mixing. First, for each node, I need to calculate the fraction of unique batches among its neighbors and average that out, normalizing it between 0 and 1. \n\nThen, I'll use the Pearson correlation between f_cross and the normalized distance from the kd-th neighbor to create a score based on expected values. I’ll also compute the cap utilization and cross-batch diversity, as well as the Gini coefficient for inbound variance uniformity. Finally, I'll calculate the Graph Health Score (GHS) as a weighted average of these components.**Testing candidates for GHS**\n\nI'll test each of the nine candidate combinations by computing the Graph Health Score (GHS) on a subsample of 25% of the nodes. This approach should save time while still allowing me to get a reasonable assessment of each candidate. I’m\
  \ curious to see how these combinations perform in terms of the metrics we've defined. Once I have the results, I can compare them to determine which candidate is the most effective.**Implementing Auto-GHS v4**\n\nI plan to implement the Auto-GHS v4 module, keeping a careful eye on computational efficiency. Since sampling from the dataset of 20k might be heavy, I aim to create the DAQB graph for each of the nine candidate combinations while utilizing a global neighbor model to support density calculations. \n\nRegarding efficiency, I’ll ensure all functions, like normalization and density calculations, are embedded, while aiming for a single-run Graph Health Score (GHS). Debug info will be sent to STDOUT, and I won't include separate tests since that would prevent the evaluator from running the main code. Once everything is ready, I will submit the module to ensure everything stays within the 30-minute evaluation guideline.**Building and computing graph metrics**\n\nI’m reviewing how to\
  \ build the graph by using inbound counts while also calculating them post-construction for efficiency. I'll determine the cap utilization based on a threshold, computing the mean of nodes exceeding that cap. \n\nFor diversity, I need to find the number of distinct batches in the neighbors for each node, which involves mapping neighbors to batch codes. Lastly, I plan to compute the Gini coefficient for inbound uniformity and ensure I calculate the Graph Health Score (GHS) accurately without exceeding the message length."
