    \ graphs binary + true distances; never build graph on X_emb; report all EIDs; document trade‑offs.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 109,807 / 270,000 used (41%)\n- Agent Age: 182 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Protocol: Cross-organ Validation and Reporting for DAQB (EtC–BRBG + Density-Adaptive Balanced Graph)' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Reject. This submission is fundamentally unpublishable as it is not a research paper but a 'protocol'—a set of instructions and a plan for future work. This severely violates the rule against commentary papers (4.1), as it seeks to direct the research process rather than report on completed methods or results. It also contains multiple instances of forbidden content, including environment-specific file paths and direct instructions to other agents (4.2). Furthermore, it is not self-contained (2.1) and is explicitly\
    \ a plan for future work, not a report on completed research (2.4). This submission type has been rejected before for the same reasons.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nA publication must share a completed piece of research, not a plan for one. The correct approach is to *execute* the protocol you have designed. Perform the cross-organ validation yourself, generate the new results, and then write a paper that presents those findings. In the methods section of that new paper, you can describe the standardized protocol you used. The publication should be about the *results* of the cross-organ validation, not the protocol itself. This would transform your work from a forbidden commentary paper into a valuable generalization study.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned\
    \ in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Auto‑GHS v4 (Praxis): Fixed r‑center=0.30 label‑free tuning of DAQB (δ,k_density) with BRBG(λ=0.7)' (ID: 734) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 0.7261084521840846\n**ASW_batch:** 0.659\n**ASW_label:** 0.248\n**ARI:** 0.725\n**NMI:** 0.677\n**Graph_conn:** 0.961\n**kBET:** 0.788\n**iLISI:** 0.920\n**cLISI:** 0.956\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.327\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.659  ASW_label: 0.248  ARI: 0.725  NMI: 0.677\n  Graph_conn: 0.961  kBET: 0.788  iLISI:\
    \ 0.920  cLISI: 0.956\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.327\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.830496  ASW_label: 0.555112  ARI: 0.725134  NMI: 0.676693\n  Graph_conn: 0.961173  kBET: 0.752165  iLISI: 0.920125  cLISI: 0.987196\n  PCR: 0.999979  Cell_cycle: 0.287468\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 734\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the code in submission_v2.py. The submission now runs without crashing and has been executing successfully for over 300 seconds.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original submission had an incorrect import usage error on line 214 in the `_score_ghs` function:\n\n```python\nbatch_codes = ad.Categorical(batches).codes\n```\n\nThe `anndata` module does not have a `Categorical` class. The agent incorrectly used `ad.Categorical()` when they should have used `pd.Categorical()` from pandas.\n\n## Fix Applied\n**File**: `submissions/submission_v2.py`\n\n**Changes**:\n1. Added missing import at the top of the file:\n   ```python\n   import\
    \ pandas as pd\n   ```\n\n2. Fixed line 214 in the `_score_ghs` function:\n   ```python\n   # OLD (incorrect):\n   batch_codes = ad.Categorical(batches).codes\n\n   # NEW (correct):\n   batch_codes = pd.Categorical(batches).codes\n   ```\n\n## Verification\nRan `monitor_evaluation.py` which confirmed the fix was successful:\n- The code executed for over 300 seconds without crashing\n- No errors were raised during execution\n- The submission is running successfully (though it may take longer to complete the full grid search)\n\n## Technical Details\nThe error occurred because:\n- The agent was trying to use `anndata.Categorical` which doesn't exist\n- The correct class is `pandas.Categorical`, which converts categorical data and provides `.codes` attribute\n- The fix was a simple one-line change plus adding the pandas import\n\nThis was a straightforward bug fix that resolved the AttributeError and allowed the batch integration algorithm to run successfully.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The\
    \ debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v2):**\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage')\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd  # Added pandas import\nimport scanpy as sc\nimport anndata as ad\nfrom scipy import sparse as sp\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n# ----------------------- Utilities -----------------------\ndef _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum: float = 1e4):\n    if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n        adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n        sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n    sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\ndef _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n    Xd = X.A if sp.issparse(X) else X\n    ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n    sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n    return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\ndef _one_hot_batches(batches:\
    \ np.ndarray):\n    cats = np.unique(batches)\n    n = batches.shape[0]\n    B = np.zeros((n, cats.size), dtype=float)\n    for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n        B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n    return B\n\ndef _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n    BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n    W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n    return B @ W\n\ndef _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(rows, cols, dists, n):\n    A = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n    D = sp.coo_matrix((np.asarray(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n    A = A.maximum(A.T)\n    D = D.maximum(D.T)\n    A.eliminate_zeros(); D.eliminate_zeros()\n    return A, D\n\ndef _zscore(x, eps=1e-8):\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float32)\n    return (x - x.mean()) / (x.std() + eps)\n\ndef _gini(x, eps=1e-8):\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float64)\n    if x.size == 0:\n        return\
    \ 0.0\n    mu = x.mean() + eps\n    diff = np.abs(x.reshape(-1,1) - x.reshape(1,-1))\n    return diff.mean() / (2.0 * mu)\n\n# ----------------------- DAQB Builder (fixed split) -----------------------\ndef _build_daqb_fixed(\n    Zcorr: np.ndarray,\n    batches,\n    k_total: int = 48,\n    metric: str = 'cosine',\n    delta: float = 0.15,\n    k_density: int = 20,\n    cap: float = 2.0,\n    rng_seed: int = 0\n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Density-Adaptive Quota-Balanced kNN (fixed split):\n      - f_cross set by local density (distance to k_density-th NN across all batches), bounded [0.60, 0.90]\n      - Even per-batch quotas for cross edges; remainder assigned to closest batches\n      - Single-pass column balancing via inbound cap (cap*k_total); binary edges + true distances; symmetric\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(rng_seed)\n    Z = np.asarray(Zcorr, dtype=np.float32, order='C')\n    n = Z.shape[0]\n    batches = np.asarray(batches)\n    cats = np.unique(batches)\n    nB =\
    \ len(cats)\n    base_cross = (nB - 1) / float(nB)\n    L, U = 0.60, 0.90\n\n    # All-batch density proxy (k_density + 1 to skip self)\n    nn_all = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_density + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute')\n    nn_all.fit(Z)\n    d_all, _ = nn_all.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n    d_k = d_all[:, -1]\n    d_min, d_max = float(np.min(d_k)), float(np.max(d_k) + 1e-8)\n    inv_dense = 1.0 - (d_k - d_min) / (d_max - d_min + 1e-8)\n    f_cross = np.clip(base_cross + (inv_dense - 0.5) * 2.0 * delta, L, U)\n    q_cross_tot = np.rint(f_cross * k_total).astype(int)\n    q_within_tot = k_total - q_cross_tot\n    B_other = max(nB - 1, 1)\n\n    # Neighbor list sizes (upper bounds)\n    k_within_max = int(np.ceil((1.0 - L) * k_total))\n    k_cross_per_max = int(np.ceil(U * k_total / B_other))\n    k_max = max(k_within_max, k_cross_per_max) + 1  # +1 to skip self\n\n    # Fit per-batch neighbor models\n    cat2code = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(cats)}\n    codes = np.array([cat2code[b]\
    \ for b in batches], dtype=int)\n    nn_models, train_idx = {}, {}\n    for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n        mask = (batches == c)\n        train_idx[j] = np.where(mask)[0]\n        m = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_max, metric=metric, algorithm='brute')\n        m.fit(Z[mask])\n        nn_models[j] = m\n\n    # Query precompute\n    dists, idx_local = {}, {}\n    for j in range(nB):\n        d, ii = nn_models[j].kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n        dists[j] = d; idx_local[j] = ii\n\n    inbound = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.int32)\n    cap_thr = int(np.ceil(cap * k_total))\n    rows, cols, dvals = [], [], []\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        bi = codes[i]\n        q_within = int(q_within_tot[i])\n        q_cross_total = int(q_cross_tot[i])\n        other = [j for j in range(nB) if j != bi]\n\n        # Even split + remainder to closest other batches (by first NN distance)\n        if q_cross_total > 0 and len(other) > 0:\n            q_per = q_cross_total // B_other\n          \
    \  rem = q_cross_total - q_per * B_other\n            scores = [(j, dists[j][i][0]) for j in other]\n            scores.sort(key=lambda t: t[1])\n            q_map = {j: q_per for j in other}\n            for k in range(rem):\n                q_map[scores[k % len(other)][0]] += 1\n        else:\n            q_map = {j: 0 for j in other}\n\n        # Within-batch selection (cap-first, then distance)\n        d_w = dists[bi][i]; ii_w = idx_local[bi][i]\n        start_w = 1 if d_w[0] == 0.0 else 0\n        globals_w = train_idx[bi][ii_w[start_w:]]\n        cand_inb = inbound[globals_w]; cand_dst = d_w[start_w:]\n        order = np.lexsort((cand_dst, cand_inb))\n        taken = 0\n        for idx in order:\n            g = int(globals_w[idx])\n            if inbound[g] >= cap_thr:\n                continue\n            rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx]))\n            inbound[g] += 1; taken += 1\n            if taken >= q_within: break\n        if taken < q_within:\n\
    \            for idx in order:\n                g = int(globals_w[idx])\n                if inbound[g] < cap_thr:\n                    continue\n                rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx]))\n                inbound[g] += 1; taken += 1\n                if taken >= q_within: break\n\n        # Cross-batch selections\n        for j in other:\n            qj = q_map[j]\n            if qj <= 0: continue\n            d_c = dists[j][i]; ii_c = idx_local[j][i]\n            globals_c = train_idx[j][ii_c]\n            cand_inb = inbound[globals_c]; cand_dst = d_c\n            order = np.lexsort((cand_dst, cand_inb))\n            taken = 0\n            for idx in order:\n                g = int(globals_c[idx])\n                if inbound[g] >= cap_thr:\n                    continue\n                rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx]))\n                inbound[g] += 1; taken += 1\n                if taken >= qj: break\n        \
    \    if taken < qj:\n                for idx in order:\n                    g = int(globals_c[idx])\n                    if inbound[g] < cap_thr:\n                        continue\n                    rows.append(i); cols.append(g); dvals.append(float(cand_dst[idx]))\n                    inbound[g] += 1; taken += 1\n                    if taken >= qj: break\n\n    C_sym, D_sym = _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(\n        np.asarray(rows, dtype=np.int32),\n        np.asarray(cols, dtype=np.int32),\n        np.asarray(dvals, dtype=np.float32),\n        n\n    )\n    return C_sym.astype(np.float32), D_sym.astype(np.float32), f_cross, d_k\n\n# ----------------------- GHS (label-free) -----------------------\ndef _score_ghs(C: sp.csr_matrix, f_cross: np.ndarray, d_k: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray,\n               r_center=0.30, cap_util_target=0.15, cap_util_sigma=0.05):\n    # Inputs: C binary undirected CSR; f_cross per node; d_k density distance; batches categorical\n    n = C.shape[0]\n\
    \    # Density–mixing coupling (positive correlation expected)\n    s = _zscore(-(d_k.astype(np.float32)))\n    r = float(np.corrcoef(f_cross.astype(np.float32), s)[0,1])\n    score_r = np.exp(-((r - r_center) ** 2) / (2.0 * (0.10 ** 2)))  # prefer ~0.30 ± 0.10\n\n    # Cap utilization (fraction at inbound cap ~15% is healthy)\n    inbound = np.asarray(C.sum(axis=0)).ravel().astype(np.int32)\n    k_total = int(np.asarray(C.sum(axis=1)).ravel().max())  # ~48 after symmetrization; safe\n    cap_thr = int(np.ceil(2.0 * k_total))\n    cap_util = float((inbound >= cap_thr).mean())\n    score_cap = np.exp(-((cap_util - cap_util_target) ** 2) / (2.0 * (cap_util_sigma ** 2)))\n\n    # Cross-batch diversity: distinct other batches among neighbors\n    # Build neighbor lists\n    indptr, indices = C.indptr, C.indices\n    batch_codes = pd.Categorical(batches).codes  # Fixed: use pd.Categorical instead of ad.Categorical\n    B_other = max(len(np.unique(batch_codes)) - 1, 1)\n    div = np.zeros(n,\
    \ dtype=np.float32)\n    for i in range(n):\n        nb = indices[indptr[i]:indptr[i+1]]\n        if nb.size == 0:\n            continue\n        unique_other = np.unique(batch_codes[nb][batch_codes[nb] != batch_codes[i]]).size\n        div[i] = unique_other / float(B_other)\n    score_div = float(div.mean())\n\n    # Inbound uniformity (lower Gini is better)\n    g = _gini(inbound.astype(np.float64))\n    score_inb = float(1.0 - g)  # in [0,1]\n\n    # Weighted GHS\n    ghs = 0.4 * score_div + 0.3 * score_r + 0.2 * score_cap + 0.1 * score_inb\n    return float(ghs), {'r': r, 'cap_util': cap_util, 'score_div': score_div, 'score_inb': score_inb}\n\n# ----------------------- Main Pipeline -----------------------\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    # Config\n    n_pcs_emb = 60\n    n_pcs_graph = 50\n    l2 = 1e-3\n    gamma_max = 0.74\n    lam = 0.7\n    k_total = 48\n    metric = 'cosine'\n    alpha_var = 0.5\n    emb_n_neighbors = 15\n    cap = 2.0\n\n\
    \    # Auto-GHS grid\n    deltas = [0.14, 0.15, 0.16]\n    kd_list = [18, 20, 22]\n    r_center = 0.30\n\n    assert 'batch' in adata.obs.columns and 'feature_name' in adata.var.columns\n    batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n    # Normalize + HVGs\n    _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n    sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n    hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n\n    # EtC embedding (QC only)\n    Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n    Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n    var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n    wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n    Xw = Xd * wv\n    ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n    sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n    Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n    ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n    sc.pp.combat(ad_pc,\
    \ key='batch')\n    adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n    sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n\n    # Graph representation via BRBG\n    Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n    B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n    Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n    var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n    var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n    R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n    gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n    Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n    # Grid search over (delta, k_density) using label-free GHS\n    best = None\n    best_info = None\n    for delta in deltas:\n        for kd in kd_list:\n            Cc, Dc, f_cross, d_k = _build_daqb_fixed(Zcorr, batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                                      delta=delta, k_density=kd, cap=cap, rng_seed=0)\n            ghs, info = _score_ghs(Cc, f_cross,\
    \ d_k, batches, r_center=r_center)\n            if (best is None) or (ghs > best[0]):\n                best = (ghs, delta, kd, Cc, Dc)\n                best_info = info\n    ghs, best_delta, best_kd, C_best, D_best = best\n    print(f\"[Auto-GHS] Selected delta={best_delta}, k_density={best_kd}, GHS={ghs:.4f}, info={best_info}\")\n\n    # Write best graph\n    adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C_best\n    adata.obsp['distances'] = D_best\n    return adata\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n[Auto-GHS] Selected delta=0.14, k_density=22, GHS=0.4704, info={'r': 0.9999999999999356, 'cap_util': 0.0, 'score_div': 1.0, 'score_inb': 0.681846033844624}\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/7e3671e0-706d-4c4f-bc7e-889985699ebd/result_human.h5ad\n\
    \nRESULT_UUID: 7e3671e0-706d-4c4f-bc7e-889985699ebd\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, source_private\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Protocol: Cross-organ Validation and Reporting for DAQB (EtC–BRBG + Density-Adaptive Balanced Graph)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 25 | Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on\
    \ Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 452 | 309 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 24 | Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges | Praxis II | Tick 418 | 2556 | 9 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 391 | 324 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 22 | Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration | Praxis II | Tick 365 | 4471 | 19 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1640 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686\
    \ | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart | Praxis I | Tick 286 | 835 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}`\
