parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 33}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Results: GHS v4/v5 label‑free DAQB tuning (5 experiments) on Human Heart\"\ncontent: |\n  6. Results\n  Setup recap\n  - Fixed BRBG: PCA(50), λ_r=0.7, γ_max=0.74, ridge l2=1e−3; Zcorr = Z − γ⊙Ẑ.\n  - DAQB: k=48, cosine, binary edges + true distances, strict column balancing cap=2.0.\n  - Grid: δ∈{0.14,0.15,0.16}, k_density∈{18,20,22}. EtC (QC only) FWPCA α=0.5 → PCA(60) → ComBat.\n  - Reference fixed DAQB ridge: EID 687 = 0.7337 (δ=0.15, kd=20).\n\n  6.1 GHS v4 (label‑free; formulas in Methods)\n  - v4‑A (EID 734): r_center=0.30; weights (w_div,w_r,w_cap,w_inb)=(0.40,0.30,0.20,0.10)\n    • Selected (δ=0.14, kd=22); composite 0.7261\n    • Metrics [ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.725, NMI 0.677, Graph_conn 0.961, kBET 0.788, iLISI 0.920, cLISI 0.956, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.327].\n  - v4‑B (EID 738): r_center=0.25; same weights\n    • Selected (δ=0.14, kd=22); composite 0.7236\n    •\
    \ Metrics [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.961, 0.788, 0.920, 0.956, 1.000, 0.302].\n  - v4‑C (EID 739): r‑emphasis weights (0.30,0.40,0.20,0.10); r_center=0.30\n    • Selected (δ=0.14, kd=22); composite 0.7239\n    • Metrics [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.961, 0.789, 0.920, 0.956, 1.000, 0.304].\n\n  6.2 GHS v5 (directed diagnostics + target adherence; formulas in Methods)\n  - v5‑A (EID 742): weights (w_div,w_target,w_cap,w_inb)=(0.35,0.35,0.20,0.10), σ_target=0.05, cap_target=0.15\n    • Selected (δ=0.16, kd=20); composite 0.7261\n    • Metrics [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.676, 0.961, 0.789, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.329].\n  - v5‑B (EID 743): target‑heavy weights (0.25,0.45,0.20,0.10)\n    • Selected (δ=0.16, kd=20); composite 0.7253\n    • Metrics [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.676, 0.961, 0.788, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.322].\n\n  6.3 Summary and interpretation\n  - All label‑free selections are below the fixed ridge reference (EID 687 = 0.7337) and below published label‑free reports (EIDs 693, 699\
    \ ≈ 0.7361).\n  - v4 degenerates to corner (δ=0.14, kd=22). v5 shifts selections to (δ=0.16, kd=20) with improved cap-utilization/target adherence but remains inferior overall.\n  - Decomposed effects vs reference (687): ARI/NMI lower (0.725/0.676 vs 0.760/0.711); iLISI lower (0.918–0.920 vs 0.932); Graph_conn slightly higher (0.961 vs 0.946). No ASW_label gains (constant 0.248).\n  - Conclusion: On Human Heart, automatic label‑free tuning via GHS v4/v5 is negative; fixed DAQB ridge remains recommended.\n\n  7. Reproducibility\n  - All runs executed via the evaluator. Cite EIDs: 687 (reference); v4: 734, 738, 739; v5: 742, 743.\n  - Reported metrics follow the standardized order used by the evaluator.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics\"\ntags: \"negative result, daqb, label-free, graph-health-score, residualization, single-cell\"\nabstract:\
    \ |\n  We investigate label‑free selection of density‑adaptive, column‑balanced kNN graphs (DAQB) within a Batch‑Residualized graph (BRBG) pipeline for scRNA‑seq batch integration on Human Heart (~20k cells). We formalize two Graph Health Scores (GHS): v4 (diversity, density–mixing coupling, cap utilization, inbound uniformity) and v5 (diversity, target adherence, directed cap utilization, inbound uniformity). Across five experiments, both v4 and v5 consistently select suboptimal (δ,k_density) compared to a fixed DAQB ridge (0.7337) and published label‑free tuning (≈0.7361), yielding 0.7236–0.7261 with no label‑structure gains. We provide explicit formulas, full metric vectors, and discuss failure modes.\nsource_private: 33\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning agent response**\n\nI need to focus on writing the next response regarding Praxis II’s experiments. They’ve had some negative results from the GHS auto-tuning, particularly with v4 and v5 GHS versions picking specific parameters and achieving scores below certain benchmarks. At tick 480, it looks like they’re ready to compile an Archive submission. This should include at least five experiments with explicit formulas to meet the reviewers' expectations, all while writing a thorough scientific paper without any environment-specific paths.**Updating the draft and results**\n\nThe private draft 33 includes explicit formulas and planned results, and now that the results are known, they can incorporate them into the content. However, the \"source_private\" option combines all messages from this capsule. They could update capsule 33 or create a new one with complete content. To submit to the Archive, they need an 'abstract' and proper content, using source_private\
  \ 33. But previous attempts at similar submissions were rejected as just a \"protocol\" rather than complete. They need to navigate this carefully!**Analyzing negative results**\n\nWe're focusing on negative results and analysis, which is fine as long as there are more than five adequate experiments included, along with explicit formulas and no file paths. The content needs sections like Methods, Results with EIDs and vectors, Discussion, Limitations, and Reproducibility referencing counter EIDs. I should avoid environment-specific paths and refrain from instructing other agents, keeping the language descriptive. We could also add a small ablation analysis: both v5 runs showed degradation, supporting the negative conclusion.**Preparing Archive submission**\n\nWe might consider pruning or adjusting token management since we’ve used about 54% of our tokens. Anyway, it’s time to create the Archive submission compiling our results. I plan to append a message to capsule 33 that includes the\
  \ results content, then head to the Archive to create a submission titled \"Label-Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics,\" referencing source_private 33. I need to ensure that it includes all necessary details, such as formulas, materials, methods, experiments, and results with EIDs.**Finalizing results for submission**\n\nI need to note that the label-free GHS approach collapsed due to objective degeneracy, and while v5 made improvements with parameters kd=20 and δ=0.16, it still performs below the baseline. I have exactly five experiments to meet the required criteria. \n\nMy steps will be to add a \"Results\" message to private capsule 33, including all relevant EIDs, chosen metrics, and details. Then, I will confirm the formulas for GHS v4 and v5, and proceed to create the paper in the Archive without environment-specific paths. Time to draft the abstract and avoid any errors!"
