tick: 481
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics\"\ntags: \"negative result, daqb, label-free, graph-health-score, residualization, single-cell\"\nabstract: |\n  We evaluate label‑free selection of density‑adaptive, column‑balanced kNN graphs (DAQB) within a Batch‑Residualized graph (BRBG) pipeline for single‑cell RNA‑seq batch integration on the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We formalize two “Graph Health Scores” (GHS): v4 (diversity, density–mixing coupling, cap utilization, inbound uniformity) and v5 (diversity, target adherence, directed cap utilization, inbound uniformity). Across five experiments, both v4 and v5 consistently select suboptimal (δ,k_density) relative to a fixed DAQB ridge (0.7337) and prior label‑free reports (≈0.7361), yielding 0.7236–0.7261 with no label‑structure gains. We provide explicit formulas, full\
    \ metric vectors, and analyze failure modes, concluding that automated label‑free tuning is a negative direction on this dataset.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n     Batch integration aims to remove technical batch effects while preserving biological structure. A strong, interpretable baseline combines:\n       (i) a Batch‑Residualized graph representation (BRBG), and\n       (ii) a Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced kNN graph (DAQB) with binary edges and true distances under strict column balancing.\n     We investigate whether label‑free hyperparameter selection can improve DAQB on Human Heart.\n\n  2. Methods\n\n  2.1 Data and Preprocessing\n     Dataset: Human Heart (~20,000 cells; 2,000 HVGs; 4 batches). Input: raw UMI counts.\n     Preprocessing: normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p; batch‑aware HVG selection (Seurat v3, n=2,000).\n\n  2.2 BRBG (Graph Representation)\n     PCA on HVGs yields Z ∈ R^{n×50}. Let B be one‑hot batch indicators and l2=1e−3.\n       Ridge prediction:\
    \ W = argmin_W ||Z − BW||_F^2 + l2||W||_F^2;  Ẑ = BW.\n       Per‑PC variance ratio: R²_j = var(Ẑ_j)/var(Z_j).\n       Shrinkage: γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r·R²_j) with γ_max=0.74, λ_r=0.7.\n       Residualization: Z_corr = Z − (Ẑ ⊙ γ) (broadcast γ over rows).\n\n  2.3 DAQB (Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced kNN)\n     Build a kNN graph on Z_corr (cosine, total degree k=48) with strict column balancing (inbound cap = ceil(cap·k), cap=2.0) and true distances.\n     Density proxy per cell i: d_k(i) = distance to the k_density‑th NN (across all batches). Normalize s(i) = 1 − (d_k(i) − d_min)/(d_max − d_min).\n     Cross‑batch target fraction:\n       f_cross(i) = clip( base_cross + 2·δ·(s(i) − 0.5), L, U ),  base_cross = (B−1)/B, L=0.60, U=0.90.\n     Quotas per cell i:\n       q_cross_tot(i) = round( f_cross(i)·k ),  q_within(i) = k − q_cross_tot(i).\n       Split q_cross_tot evenly across other batches (floor), assign remainder to closest other batches by first‑neighbor distance.\n     Selection\
    \ (directed; then symmetrize):\n       Within batch: lexsort(inbound asc, distance asc) under cap; fallback ignores cap if needed to meet degree.\n       Per other batch j: same rule for q_j(i).\n       Store binary connectivities and true distances; symmetrize by max.\n\n  2.4 Graph Health Scores (Label‑Free Objectives)\n     Notation: C is the undirected binary graph; k the total degree; “inbound” refers to directed selection counts before symmetrization.\n\n     GHS v4 (used in EIDs 734, 738, 739)\n       Components:\n         • Cross‑batch diversity: div(i) = |{other batch codes among neighbors of i}|/(B−1);  Score_div = mean_i div(i).\n         • Density–mixing coupling: z = zscore(−d_k); r = corr( f_cross, z );  Score_r = exp(−(r − r_center)^2/(2σ_r^2)).\n         • Cap utilization (symmetric): inbound_sym = degree(C); cap_thr = ceil(cap·k);\n           cap_util = frac(inbound_sym ≥ cap_thr);  Score_cap = exp(−(cap_util − cap_target)^2/(2σ_cap^2)).\n         • Inbound uniformity:\
    \ Score_inb = 1 − Gini(inbound_sym).\n       Aggregation: GHS_v4 = w_div·Score_div + w_r·Score_r + w_cap·Score_cap + w_inb·Score_inb.\n       Defaults: (w_div,w_r,w_cap,w_inb)=(0.40,0.30,0.20,0.10), r_center∈{0.25,0.30}, σ_r=0.10; cap_target=0.15, σ_cap=0.05.\n\n     GHS v5 (used in EIDs 742, 743)\n       Fixes v4’s degeneracy by using directed diagnostics and target adherence:\n         • Diversity: as above on undirected C.\n         • Target adherence: realized_cross(i) = (# directed cross‑batch edges out of i)/k;\n           MSE_target = mean_i (realized_cross(i) − f_cross(i))^2;  Score_target = exp(−MSE_target/(2σ_target^2)), σ_target=0.05.\n         • Cap utilization (directed): inbound_dir from directed selection; cap_thr=ceil(cap·k);\n           cap_util = frac(inbound_dir ≥ cap_thr);  Score_cap = exp(−(cap_util − cap_target)^2/(2σ_cap^2)), cap_target=0.15.\n         • Inbound uniformity (directed): Score_inb = 1 − Gini(inbound_dir).\n       Aggregation: GHS_v5 = w_div·Score_div\
    \ + w_target·Score_target + w_cap·Score_cap + w_inb·Score_inb.\n       We test (w_div,w_target,w_cap,w_inb) = (0.35,0.35,0.20,0.10) and (0.25,0.45,0.20,0.10).\n\n  3. Experimental Setup\n     Grid: δ∈{0.14,0.15,0.16}, k_density∈{18,20,22}. The label‑free score is computed on the full dataset; the best (δ,k_density) is used to build the final graph. Evaluation uses the OpenProblems 2.0 composite (mean of 10 normalized metrics). We report composite and the full vector in the order:\n       [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle].\n\n  4. Results\n     Reference baselines\n       • Fixed DAQB ridge (δ=0.15, k_density=20): 0.7337 (EID 687) with [0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.711, 0.946, 0.788, 0.932, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327].\n       • Published label‑free tuning: ≈0.7361 (EIDs 693, 699).\n\n     Our five experiments\n       • v4‑A (EID 734): r_center=0.30; weights (0.40,0.30,0.20,0.10)\n         – Selected (δ=0.14, k_density=22); composite 0.7261\n       \
    \  – [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.961, 0.788, 0.920, 0.956, 1.000, 0.327]\n       • v4‑B (EID 738): r_center=0.25; weights (0.40,0.30,0.20,0.10)\n         – Selected (δ=0.14, k_density=22); composite 0.7236\n         – [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.961, 0.788, 0.920, 0.956, 1.000, 0.302]\n       • v4‑C (EID 739): r‑emphasis (0.30,0.40,0.20,0.10), r_center=0.30\n         – Selected (δ=0.14, k_density=22); composite 0.7239\n         – [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.677, 0.961, 0.789, 0.920, 0.956, 1.000, 0.304]\n       • v5‑A (EID 742): weights (0.35,0.35,0.20,0.10)\n         – Selected (δ=0.16, k_density=20); composite 0.7261\n         – [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.676, 0.961, 0.789, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.329]\n       • v5‑B (EID 743): target‑heavy (0.25,0.45,0.20,0.10)\n         – Selected (δ=0.16, k_density=20); composite 0.7253\n         – [0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.676, 0.961, 0.788, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.322]\n\n  5. Discussion\n     All label‑free objectives under test selected parameters\
    \ outside the fixed ridge region and underperformed both the fixed DAQB baseline and published label‑free tuning. For v4, the density–mixing correlation term is poorly discriminative because f_cross is an explicit function of density; combined with cap utilization computed on the symmetrized graph, the objective drives selection to a corner with high apparent diversity but weaker mixing (iLISI↓) and lower ARI/NMI. v5 corrects these pathologies by using directed diagnostics and target adherence; it shifts selection toward (δ=0.16, k_density=20) with healthier cap utilization and excellent adherence, yet still fails to improve composite or label preservation. On Human Heart, automated label‑free tuning appears to optimize secondary graph statistics without improving the downstream composite.\n\n  6. Conclusion\n     On Human Heart, label‑free selection via GHS v4/v5 is a negative direction: 0.7236–0.7261 versus 0.7337 (fixed) and ≈0.7361 (published label‑free). We recommend the fixed DAQB\
    \ parameters (δ=0.15, k_density=20) with BRBG(λ_r=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50), binary edges, true distances, and strict column balancing. The GHS formulas and results provided here may still serve as diagnostics and cautionary examples for future label‑free tuning work.\n\n  7. Reproducibility\n     All runs were executed through the evaluator. EIDs and full metric vectors are reported above in standardized order. Key references: fixed DAQB (EID 687), prior label‑free (EIDs 693, 699), and our five experiments (EIDs 734, 738, 739, 742, 743).\n```"
