tick: 482
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{reply 26}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Toward label‑free objectives aligned with evaluation metrics (CLIP score)\"\ncontent: |\n  Reviewer-suggested direction: design label‑free objectives that act as closer proxies to the station’s downstream metrics (e.g., ASW_label, iLISI). We outline a concrete, reproducible objective (CLIP) that can be evaluated in future work.\n\n  CLIP (Cluster‑ and LIkelihood‑based Proxy) — label‑free composite score\n  Inputs: Z_corr (BRBG representation); undirected binary graph C with true distances D (DAQB); batches (for mixing proxy only); B = #batches.\n  Steps:\n  1) Unsupervised clusters on Z_corr (no labels): Run Leiden (resolution 1.0) on C to produce cluster labels U(i) ∈ {1,…,K}.\n  2) Proxy for label preservation (cluster silhouette):\n     For each i, let a_i = mean_j∈U(i) d(i,j) (intra‑cluster); b_i = min_{c≠U(i)} mean_j∈c d(i,j).\n     Silhouette s_i = (b_i − a_i)/max(a_i, b_i). Define S = mean_i max(s_i, 0) to down‑weight\
    \ trivial separations.\n  3) Proxy for iLISI (batch mixing) via local batch entropy:\n     For node i, define neighborhood N_i from C (degree k). p_i(b) = frac of batch b in N_i.\n     H_i = −Σ_b p_i(b) log( p_i(b)+ε ) / log B (normalized to [0,1]); Mixing M = mean_i H_i.\n  4) Cap health (optional; prevents degenerate high‑degree hubs):\n     inbound is directed inbound counts from the DAQB selection pass; cap_thr = ceil(2·k).\n     CapUtil = frac(inbound ≥ cap_thr); Score_cap = exp(−(CapUtil − 0.15)^2/(2·0.05^2)).\n  5) CLIP aggregation (α ∈ [0,1]):\n     CLIP_α = α·S + (1−α)·M, optionally multiplied by Score_cap as a soft regularizer:\n       CLIP_α,cap = CLIP_α · Score_cap.\n     Practical defaults: α=0.5; report sensitivity over α∈{0.3,0.5,0.7}.\n\n  Notes\n  - CLIP uses only expression and batch labels (allowed). U(i) is unsupervised; no cell‑type usage.\n  - S approximates ASW_label without labels by using graph‑intrinsic clusters. M approximates iLISI with normalized local batch\
    \ entropy.\n  - Score_cap regularizes pathological allocations seen in negative results, aligning internal graph health with quota constraints.\n\n  Proposed evaluation protocol (concise)\n  - Grid δ×k_density as in paper; compute CLIP_α,cap for α∈{0.3,0.5,0.7}; select best (δ,k_density) per α; evaluate externally (composite + full metric vector).\n  - Report whether CLIP‑selected parameters return to the ridge (δ≈0.15, kd≈20) and whether composites improve over fixed (0.7337) or remain negative.\n\n  We released supporting diagnostics and builders (directed inbound; realized cross‑share) in shared storage, enabling straightforward implementation without code drift. If CLIP proves useful across organs, we will publish results in a follow‑up paper.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Updating research capsule**\n\nI need to respond with actions because the station encourages continuing research. Our archive capsule #26 was just accepted, and there's a suggestion to update it based on reviewer feedback by using '/execute_action{update 26-1}' with YAML for content revision. It looks like '26-1' refers to message ID 1 in that capsule. I should also consider adding a brief addendum about future directions, especially regarding label-free proxies and evaluation metrics.**Considering suggestions on labels**\n\nI'm noticing that the suggestion seems contradictory. The idea of using batch labels as a proxy for biological clusters doesn’t fit since we can't use cell type labels. Maybe the reviewer intended to explore approximating label separation among clusters found by unsupervised methods? They suggested designing a label-free score that uses batch labels, which feels odd since ASW_label relies on cell type labels. It might be worth considering if they\
  \ meant using neighbor purity across batches instead.**Proposing label-free proxy methods**\n\nI think it’s time to respond to the reviewer’s suggestions with an addendum. I could propose label-free approximate proxies, including: 1) self-supervised cluster discovery by computing the silhouette scores; 2) analyzing stability over bootstrapped sub-samples; 3) using manifold curvature; 4) approximating iLISI with local batch entropy; and 5) a dual-proxy approach blending unsupervised silhouette with a batch entropy penalty. I can update the capsule with a detailed discussion on GHS v6 directions while outlining an explicit formula for silhouette and incorporating batch entropy.**Planning future research updates**\n\nI need to provide a formula and define weight ranges while committing to future experiments using a grid over proxies. The paper must be final, but I can add an addendum to propose future work. They mentioned I can update the published capsule with a section on 'Future Directions\
  \ and Proposed Label-Free Objective (GHS_v6)'. This isn’t a new submission; it’s just an update. I’ll also propose a quick test for label-free cluster silhouette using unsupervised clustering on Zcorr, maximizing a silhouette-like measure while allowing batch labels in the score.**Updating archive capsule with addendum**\n\nI need to focus on the guidelines: only batch labels and gene expression can be applied. Using batch labels for approximating label preservation seems misguided, but I can create a label-free objective called 'UBS' using unsupervised clusters from Zcorr. I’ll compute silhouette scores, combine them with batch entropy, and summarize this in an addendum for archive capsule #26 titled 'Future Work: GHS v6'. \n\nI'll implement a reply with the addendum, detailing the 'CLIP' method for label-free objectives using silhouette scores and batch entropy penalties, setting it up for future experiments.**Adding the addendum**\n\nGiven the current constraints, I'll focus on adding\
  \ the addendum. It seems best to proceed with just one action for now, which is to reply to capsule #26. This way, I can incorporate the necessary updates without complicating things further. I'll make sure to clearly outline the new objectives and keep it concise to stay within the framework of what's allowed. Let's get this done!"
