parts:
- text: "Tick 3 — Tighten theory, define falsification tests, and set crisp go/no‑go gates\n\n1) Theory of why DAQB works (operational model)\n- Mechanism: Couple cross‑batch share f_cross to local density in Zcorr. Dense regions are safer to mix (structure redundant), borders/sparse regions need restraint to preserve labels.\n- Sweet spot: kd≈20 approximates a meso‑scale where within‑manifold neighborhoods are stable but not too global; δ≈0.15 nudges mixing without erasing boundaries.\n- Failure modes seen: Non‑local gates (hard geometry/candidate filters) and proportional per‑batch splits reduce mixing breadth (iLISI↓) without label gains because they either (a) suppress long‑range connectors that column balancing would otherwise admit or (b) over‑concentrate cross‑batch edges into a single batch, harming diversity.\n\n2) Falsification tests (diagnostics-first, no graph surgery)\n- Scale sensitivity: If kd truly captures a meso‑scale, then kd±2 should not move composite >0.002 but should\
    \ nudge iLISI/ASW_label in opposite directions; otherwise our “meso‑scale” story is weak.\n- Density coupling sanity check: Shuffle f_cross across cells (preserve marginal) — expect iLISI↓ and/or ASW_label↓; if not, coupling is not doing the work we think.\n- Column balancing necessity: Remove cap=2.0 (keeping quotas) — predict iLISI↑ but Graph_conn/ASW_label/cLISI degrade; confirm column balancing is the guardrail.\n- Reciprocity as a soft signal: Mutual‑edge rate should correlate positively with ASW_label at fixed δ,kd; if correlation ~0, reciprocity tie‑break likely won’t help.\n\n3) One “safe” structural tweak (de‑risked)\n- Reciprocity tie‑break only (no gating, no quota change):\n  order = lexsort(inbound asc, reciprocity desc, distance asc) within each per‑batch list.\n- Go/no‑go:\n  - Go if composite ≥0.734 with iLISI ≥0.930 and ASW_label ≥0.15 vs fixed ridge on Heart.\n  - Stop after 2 runs (kd=20, δ∈{0.14,0.16}); if both <0.734 or iLISI/ASW_label regress, close line.\n\n4)\
    \ Proxy–metric alignment: make or break\n- Compute Spearman/Kendall ρ with CIs for GHS/CLIP subcomponents vs composite, iLISI, ASW_label across the 3×3 grid.\n- Set a hard bar: |ρ| ≥0.4 and top‑1 pick sits on the iLISI–ASW_label Pareto front in ≥2/3 subsamples. If not met, proxies remain diagnostics‑only on Heart.\n\n5) Cross‑organ ridge map: operational rules\n- Intake must include: full 10‑metric vector (ordered), δ,kd,k,metric,cap, EIDs; diagnostics (cap utilization %, f_cross–density corr).\n- Recommendation per organ: pick any point within Δcomposite ≤0.002 of the best that avoids large ASW_label/cLISI drops; document trade‑offs.\n- Publish once ≥3 organs; revisions only with new organs or consistent shifts.\n\n6) Resource and risk plan\n- Token safety: prune in blocks when >70% usage; keep shared artifacts central to avoid duplication.\n- Avoid further Heart‑only tuners unless proxy alignment passes the bar.\n- Always log EIDs, runtime/memory, and guardrails (binary + true distances;\
    \ never graph on X_emb)."
thinking_content: "**Exploring diagnostic proposals**\n\nI'm thinking about organizing my thoughts into bullet points. Topics could include stress tests for assumptions, concerns about data quality, proposals for non-local structures, and experiments aimed at verification. \n\nFor stress-test invariances, I wonder if we can shuffle batch labels or use negative controls to analyze metric effects. Regarding efficiency, could we implement approximate neighbors to scale across organs? Some libraries are allowed, like scanpy and scikit-learn, but I’m unsure about availability. There might be a need to consider NMSlib.**Proposing improvements and checks**\n\nI should discuss using a brute-force method to confirm memory. \n\nOne suggestion is to check if iLISI collapse correlates with cross-batch fractions near boundaries and to apply boundary-aware checks by measuring density gradients. \n\nAnother theory involves handling the composite as an average of normalized metrics; ideally, we want to\
  \ enhance iLISI without damaging ASW_label or cLISI. \n\nThere's also a notion of using second-stage rebalancing to equalize inbound data across batches. Finally, I’m considering a Balanced Local Delaunay approach, but it might be expensive to construct across numerous nodes.**Planning for connectivity and outcomes**\n\nI’m thinking about building a small cross-batch connector set to ensure connectivity while tracking quotas. It's crucial to keep k filled. These changes might be seen as non-local, so I could propose them as future steps. \n\nFor a long-term plan, creating a cross-organ ridge map for publication sounds good but keeping track of token usage is essential. We need to manage our tokens, aiming to stay below 75% usage and pruning older dialogues as needed. \n\nLastly, I’ll set measurable outcomes for the next 30 ticks while ensuring compliance with safety rules.**Reflecting on tick 3**\n\nI want to focus on crafting a reflection for tick 3, emphasizing a few key aspects. First,\
  \ I need a robust proxy evaluation plan that ensures thorough assessments. Then, I see value in creating unified diagnostics to streamline our approach. \n\nAdditionally, it's important to develop a conceptual model that explains why DAQB works effectively. Lastly, I’ll outline a plan to test invariances that could uncover any vulnerabilities. This should give us a solid foundation for future work!**Drafting a reflection for tick 3**\n\nI’m aiming to provide a concise reflection. \n\nFirst, the mental model suggests that DAQB couples f_cross to density, with cross-batch mixing being advantageous in dense zones. We need to keep borderline cells within-batch to preserve labels, weighing the trade-off between δ and kd, noting that heavy δ or low kd can degrade labeling. I'll look into whether kd=20 aligns with changes in manifold curvature, though computing curvature could be heavy. \n\nNext, I want to estimate a label-free local margin, examining local differences between within-batch and\
  \ cross-batch distances. \n\nFinally, while thinking about quota smoothing, it seems that could introduce local inconsistencies, so I should propose three crisp next steps with clear acceptance criteria and shut-off triggers."
