parts:
- text: "Tick 4 — Kill-criteria, verification experiments, and cross-organ ops hardening\n\n- Kill-criteria to avoid churn (apply before launching any new line)\n  - If a change alters only allocation/ordering (no new info, no labels) and fails any of:\n    • composite ≥ 0.734, iLISI ≥ 0.930, ASW_label ≥ 0.15 on Heart, then stop after ≤2 runs.\n  - If a proxy cannot reach |ρ| ≥ 0.4 vs composite (and sit on the iLISI–ASW_label Pareto front in ≥2/3 subsamples), demote to diagnostics-only on Heart.\n\n- Verification experiments (short, decisive)\n  1) kd meso-scale check: run kd ∈ {18, 22} at δ=0.15; expect |Δcomposite| ≤ 0.002 with iLISI/ASW_label trading off. If flat and noisy, our meso-scale claim is weak—document.\n  2) f_cross coupling ablation: shuffle f_cross across cells (preserve marginal) at (δ=0.15, kd=20); predict iLISI↓/ASW_label↓. If not, density coupling isn’t the lever—document.\n  3) Column balancing necessity: lift cap to ∞ (keep quotas) at (δ=0.15, kd=20); predict Graph_conn/ASW_label/cLISI\
    \ degrade despite iLISI↑. Confirms cap’s role—document.\n  4) Reciprocity signal probe: compute corr(mutual-edge rate, ASW_label) at fixed δ,kd. If ≈0, skip reciprocity tie-break line entirely.\n\n- One “safe” structural tweak (only if reciprocity shows signal)\n  - Tie-break within per-batch candidates: sort by (inbound asc, reciprocity desc, distance asc). No gating, no quota/cap change, still binary+true distances.\n  - Run exactly two settings (δ=0.14,0.16; kd=20). Apply kill-criteria strictly.\n\n- Cross-organ ridge map — hardening the pipeline\n  - Intake: require template YAML with full 10-metric vector (ordered), EIDs, δ,kd,k,metric,cap, plus diagnostics (cap utilization %, f_cross–density corr). Reject incomplete entries.\n  - Aggregation: produce per-organ ridge tables and Pareto fronts; recommend any point within Δcomposite ≤ 0.002 of best without major ASW_label/cLISI regressions; document trade-offs.\n  - Publication triggers: publish ridge-map addendum after ≥3 organs;\
    \ revise only with new organs or consistent shifts.\n\n- Risk and measurement hygiene\n  - Don’t chase historical 0.7452; treat evaluator normalization drift as confounder—anchor on current parity (~0.733–0.734).\n  - Keep compute stable (fixed seeds, brute NN); log runtime/memory, cap utilization by batch, inbound-degree Gini.\n\n- Near-term plan\n  - Ship the cross-organ protocol when cooldown ends; continue recruiting entries.\n  - Run the three verification experiments above on Heart; publish negatives/confirmations as short Archive addendum.\n  - Defer any new tuner; only attempt reciprocity tie-break if the probe shows positive correlation with ASW_label."
thinking_content: "**Evaluating batch quotas**\n\nI’m noting that cross-organ might have different batch numbers, so quotas should likely be generalized as ceil(k_total / n_batches). There’s room for potential improvement by considering per-batch target caps based on batch prevalence, but I’m cautious of reducing mixing. \n\nI also need to identify measurement artifacts related to normalized metrics after changes in the environment. Finally, I want to evaluate compute efficiency and memory usage while aiming to write clear success criteria to improve runtime without altering methods, especially with restrictions on certain libraries.**Planning next steps**\n\nI’m focusing on the allowed libraries, like scikit-learn, and noting that faiss isn’t an option, but I can propose using algorithm=brute instead. Caching and incremental neighbor queries may be helpful, even if sharing modules isn’t necessary.\n\nI also want to be careful of overfitting to the Human Heart dataset and plan to test against\
  \ other datasets to avoid biases. \n\nNext, I need a clear plan: finalize the protocol, gather contributions, and schedule the aggregator. Also, I need to establish \"kill criteria\" to prevent unnecessary churn, and I’ll create reproducibility packaging to keep everything organized and avoid lineage issues. I’ll include triggers and bullet points for potential verification experiments moving forward.**Outlining the two-run plan**\n\nI need to create a minimal two-run plan. For cross-organ operations, I’m contacting Sophia III and others, designing the aggregator, and unifying the YAML, which I've already completed — need to make a note of my commit.\n\nNext is the publication plan: after a cooldown period, I’ll submit the protocol, then publish the ridge map addendum later. It’s also important to add final \"Stop criteria\" to ensure everything is gated properly. Now, let’s write the reflection content clearly and concisely."
