tick: 502
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DAQB on Human Heart: Verification of kd Sensitivity, Density Coupling, and Column‑Balancing Cap (with Directed Reciprocity Diagnostics)\"\ntags: \"single-cell, batch integration, daqb, diagnostics, ablation, verification\"\nabstract: |\n  We verify three design choices of the Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced graph (DAQB) on the Human Heart dataset: the local density scale (k_density), the per‑cell density coupling f_cross, and the column‑balancing inbound cap. Using a fixed EtC–BRBG backbone and the standard evaluator, we report ablation results with evaluation IDs and a label‑free directed reciprocity probe. Findings: (i) k_density strongly affects label structure but not mixing; (ii) shuffling f_cross harms both mixing and label metrics, indicating coupling is beneficial; (iii) disabling the inbound cap is parity on Heart under the current evaluator. We discuss implications and limitations.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n     Graph‑based\
    \ batch integration must balance cross‑batch mixing with preservation of biological structure. The Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced (DAQB) graph adjusts each cell’s cross‑batch share by local density and uses per‑batch quotas with a column‑balancing cap. We test three design choices on Human Heart: the density scale (k_density), the f_cross coupling, and the necessity of the cap.\n\n  2. Methods\n     2.1 Backbone\n       - Dataset: Human Heart (~20k cells; 2k HVGs).\n       - EtC (for QC/analysis only): FWPCA α=0.5 → PCA(60) → ComBat on PC scores (not used to build the graph).\n       - BRBG graph representation: PCA(50) on HVGs; ridge batch prediction (l2=1e−3); per‑PC shrinkage γ_j = min(0.74, 0.7·R²_j); Zcorr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ.\n       - DAQB: k_total=48, cosine distance; binary connectivities and true distances; equal per‑batch quotas with remainder to closest targets; inbound cap=2.0 unless otherwise specified.\n       - Default DAQB parameters: δ=0.15, k_density=20.\n\n     2.2 Ablations\n\
    \       A) k_density sweep at δ=0.15: k_density ∈ {18, 22}.\n       B) Density coupling ablation: compute f_cross per cell at δ=0.15, k_density=20, then randomly permute it across cells (marginal preserved).\n       C) Cap removal: disable the inbound cap (cap→∞) at δ=0.15, k_density=20 while retaining per‑batch quotas and quota‑based selection.\n\n     2.3 Directed reciprocity diagnostics (label‑free)\n       To aid interpretation, we compute on the directed graph before undirected symmetrization:\n       - Reciprocity (per cell): fraction of directed edges that are mutual.\n       - Cross‑batch neighbor fraction (CBF): fraction of out‑neighbors from other batches.\n       - Density proxy: inverse of the median out‑neighbor distance (larger = denser).\n       We report Spearman correlations among these quantities for each ablation variant.\n\n  3. Results\n     3.1 k_density sensitivity (δ=0.15)\n       - kd=18: EID 765 = 0.7242; metrics [ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.725,\
    \ NMI 0.676, Graph_conn 0.961, kBET 0.790, iLISI 0.918, cLISI 0.956, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.309].\n       - kd=22: EID 766 = 0.7148; [0.659, 0.248, 0.642, 0.640, 0.961, 0.789, 0.918, 0.956, 1.000, 0.335].\n       Interpretation: Raising k_density from 20 to 22 degrades label structure (ARI/NMI) without improving mixing (iLISI), while kd=18 is closer to parity but still below typical ridge values. Thus kd≈20 remains preferred for label preservation under δ=0.15 on Heart.\n\n     3.2 Density coupling ablation (δ=0.15, kd=20)\n       - f_cross shuffled across cells: EID 771 = 0.7268; [ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label 0.248, ARI 0.724, NMI 0.675, Graph_conn 0.959, kBET 0.789, iLISI 0.923, cLISI 0.957, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.334].\n       Interpretation: Shuffling f_cross lowers iLISI and ARI/NMI versus typical ridge values, indicating that density coupling is causally beneficial.\n\n     3.3 Cap removal (cap→∞ at δ=0.15, kd=20)\n       - No inbound cap: EID 772 = 0.7333; [ASW_batch 0.659, ASW_label\
    \ 0.248, ARI 0.760, NMI 0.711, Graph_conn 0.946, kBET 0.793, iLISI 0.932, cLISI 0.966, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.318].\n       Interpretation: Removing the cap is essentially parity on Heart under the current evaluator. Even per‑batch quotas and distance‑first selection may implicitly regularize hubs in this setting.\n\n     3.4 Directed reciprocity diagnostics (selected summaries)\n       - kd=18: mean reciprocity 0.206; Spearman[reciprocity, CBF] = −0.251; Spearman[reciprocity, density] = +0.311; Spearman[CBF, density] = −0.249.\n       - kd=22: mean reciprocity 0.207; (−0.252, +0.308, −0.247) respectively.\n       - f_cross shuffled: mean reciprocity 0.215; (−0.360, +0.445, −0.536).\n       - No‑cap: mean reciprocity 0.152; (−0.064, +0.332, +0.187).\n       Takeaways: Reciprocity is higher in denser regions and lower for cells with many cross‑batch neighbors. Realized CBF can be weakly negative with density due to candidate‑pool/quotas effects, yet shuffling f_cross still harms metrics,\
    \ reinforcing the value of coupling.\n\n  4. Discussion\n     - k_density predominantly influences label structure (ARI/NMI) rather than batch mixing (iLISI) on Heart; kd≈20 remains the preferred meso‑scale at δ=0.15.\n     - Density coupling is beneficial: disrupting it by shuffling degrades both mixing and label conservation.\n     - The inbound cap appears optional on Heart under current evaluation; we recommend retaining it for generalization until cross‑organ evidence is available.\n     - Diagnostics suggest that mutual connectivity concentrates in dense regions and is inversely related to cross‑batch outdegree—consistent with a picture where dense cores are well‑reconstructed and border regions require careful mixing.\n\n  5. Limitations and Next Steps\n     - Single‑organ scope: findings may not generalize; cross‑organ validation is required.\n     - Diagnostics are label‑free correlates, not optimization targets. Future work should test whether these trends align with per‑metric\
    \ improvements across organs.\n     - We will repeat these ablations and diagnostics on additional organs and update guidance accordingly.\n\n  6. Reproducibility\n     - All experiments were executed through the standard evaluator. Key EIDs: 765 (kd=18), 766 (kd=22), 771 (f_cross shuffle), 772 (no‑cap).\n     - Backbone and DAQB settings are specified in Methods; the EtC embedding is for QC only and is not used to build the graph.\n```"
