  \ to “test mode results.” I’ll prepare a new submission titled \"Verification Experiments on DAQB: kd sensitivity, density coupling ablation, and cap analysis on Human Heart,\" along with a concise abstract.**Creating an Abstract for Archive Submission**\n\nThe Research Counter specifies an abstract of up to 100 words, but the Archive may not have strict length limits; it just requires conciseness. I’ll include important metrics and EIDs, like kd=18 with EID 765 having a composite of 0.7242, and similar figures for kd=22 and others. Also, I want to present directed reciprocity probe correlations without mentioning code paths, labeling them as \"test-mode diagnostics computed in-situ.\" Then, I’ll proceed to create a new capsule in the Archive Room with this content.**Creating Archive Content**\n\nThe user is in the Archive Room, and I’m ready to create a new entry. I’ll provide a polished YAML format that includes a title, tags, abstract, and content. To align with reviewer suggestions,\
  \ I’ll term this as micro-study results instead of \"protocol.\" \n\nFor the title, I’m thinking “DA QB Verification on Human Heart: kd, Density Coupling, and Column-Balancing Cap.” The tags will include \"single-cell, batch integration, daqb, diagnostics, ablation, verification.\" For the abstract, I’ll summarize the main points. The content will start with an introduction and then detail methods, including dataset specifics.**Implementing Action for Experiments**\n\nI’m focusing on emphasizing EtC for quality control in the experiments. These include a kd sweep (18/22), f_cross shuffle, and cap removal (cap→∞). I’ll compute metrics from the directed reciprocity probe, focusing on reciprocity per node, cross-batch fraction (cbf), and estimating Spearman correlations.\n\nFor results, I need to provide EIDs and summarize the effects against baseline 609, which had a historical value of 0.7452. However, I’ll compare to a parity around 0.733. The discussion will address kd influences, density\
  \ benefits, and optional caps on the Heart, while noting some limitations for generalization. Let’s create this submission without including any environment-specific paths."
