parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_PraxisII_lam_sweep.yaml}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  organ: \"Human Heart\"\n  dataset_notes: \"CELLxGENE Census; ~20k cells × 2k HVGs; 4 batches\"\n  author: \"Praxis II\"\n  agent: \"Praxis II\"\n  date: \"tick 518\"\n  fixed_config:\n    brbg:\n      lambda_r: \"[sweep: 0.4, 0.6]\"   # module‑parity lam sweep\n      gamma_max: 0.74\n      pca_graph: 50\n      ridge_l2: 1.0e-3\n    daqb:\n      k_total: 48\n      metric: \"cosine\"\n      cap: \"builder-default\"          # per Logos/Praxis builder\n      binary_true_distances: true\n    etc_qc:\n      alpha_var: 0.5\n      pca_emb: 60\n      combat: true\n  runs:\n    - delta: 0.10\n      k_density: 20\n      lambda_r: 0.4\n      eid: \"808\"\n      composite: 0.7361\n      metrics:\n        ASW_batch: 0.659\n        ASW_label: 0.248\n        ARI: 0.761\n        NMI: 0.712\n        Graph_conn: 0.946\n        kBET: 0.788\n  \
    \      iLISI: 0.952\n        cLISI: 0.967\n        PCR: 1.000\n        Cell_cycle: 0.327\n      notes: \"Module‑parity builder + neighbors side‑effect; printed avg_R2≈0.0472 (computed before lam).\"\n    - delta: 0.10\n      k_density: 20\n      lambda_r: 0.6\n      eid: \"809\"\n      composite: 0.7364\n      metrics:\n        ASW_batch: 0.659\n        ASW_label: 0.248\n        ARI: 0.761\n        NMI: 0.712\n        Graph_conn: 0.946\n        kBET: 0.793\n        iLISI: 0.951\n        cLISI: 0.966\n        PCR: 1.000\n        Cell_cycle: 0.327\n      notes: \"Module‑parity builder + neighbors side‑effect; printed avg_R2≈0.0472 (computed before lam).\"\n  diagnostics:\n    corr_fcross_density: null\n    cap_utilization_pct: \"builder-dependent\"\n    inbound_degree_histogram: []\n  notes: \"Lam=0.4 and 0.6 are near‑parity with lam=0.5 (801=0.7356). Best observed = 0.7364 (lam=0.6). The printed avg_R2 is computed pre‑lam; propose a lam heuristic based on desired saturation fraction of\
    \ gamma (see lam_autoselect.py).\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/diagnostics/lam_autoselect.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Label-free lambda selection from batch R^2 profile.\n\n  Idea:\n    - Compute per-PC R^2 of predicting Z (graph PCs) from one-hot batches.\n    - Choose lambda such that a target fraction of PCs hit the gamma_max cap:\n        gamma_j = min(gamma_max, lambda * R2_j).\n      For target_saturation (e.g., 10%), solve lambda so that\n        quantile(R2, 1 - target_saturation) * lambda = gamma_max\n      => lambda = gamma_max / quantile(R2, 1 - target_saturation).\n    - Clip to [lam_min, lam_max].\n\n  This uses only batch labels + PCs (allowed), and tunes lam to dataset severity.\n\n  Usage:\n    lam = choose_lambda_from_r2(Zg, batches, gamma_max=0.74, target_saturation=0.10)\n  \"\"\"\n\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def _one_hot(batches: np.ndarray):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0], cats.size), dtype=float)\n\
    \      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def r2_per_pc(Z: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3) -> np.ndarray:\n      \"\"\"Variance-explained by batches per PC, R^2_j = var(Zhat_j)/var(Z_j).\"\"\"\n      B = _one_hot(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Z.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      return R2\n\n  def choose_lambda_from_r2(Z: np.ndarray,\n                            batches: np.ndarray,\n                            gamma_max: float = 0.74,\n                            target_saturation: float = 0.10,\n                            lam_min: float = 0.2,\n      \
    \                      lam_max: float = 1.0,\n                            l2: float = 1e-3) -> float:\n      \"\"\"\n      Returns lam so that about `target_saturation` fraction of PCs saturate at gamma_max.\n      \"\"\"\n      R2 = r2_per_pc(Z, batches, l2=l2)\n      q = np.quantile(R2, 1.0 - float(target_saturation))\n      if q <= 1e-8:\n          # Degenerate: batches explain negligible variance; pick small lam\n          return lam_min\n      lam = gamma_max / float(q)\n      return float(np.clip(lam, lam_min, lam_max))\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 24}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: λ sweep (module‑parity) and a label‑free λ selection heuristic\"\ncontent: |\n  We swept BRBG λ under the module‑parity setup (shared builder + neighbors side‑effect) at δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48.\n  Results (Human Heart):\n  - λ=0.4 → EID 808 = 0.7361; vector [0.659, 0.248, 0.761, 0.712, 0.946, 0.788, 0.952, 0.967, 1.000, 0.327].\n  - λ=0.6 → EID 809 = 0.7364; vector [0.659,\
    \ 0.248, 0.761, 0.712, 0.946, 0.793, 0.951, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327].\n  - Prior λ=0.5 → EID 801 = 0.7356; vector [0.659, 0.248, 0.758, 0.706, 0.946, 0.790, 0.951, 0.966, 1.000, 0.331].\n  Interpretation: λ ∈ [0.4, 0.6] is a flat ridge (±0.0008); λ=0.6 is marginally best here.\n\n  Label‑free λ heuristic (code added: storage/shared/diagnostics/lam_autoselect.py):\n  - Compute per‑PC batch R² on Z (graph PCs) via ridge on one‑hot batches.\n  - Choose λ so that a target fraction φ of PCs saturate at γ_max:\n      γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j),    λ = γ_max / quantile(R², 1−φ).\n    Example φ=0.10 ⇒ only top 10% PCs (by R²) hit the cap.\n  - This uses only batch labels + PCs (allowed) and adapts λ to dataset severity.\n\n  We welcome cross‑organ tests of the heuristic (please report φ, chosen λ, and full metric vectors). Ridge entries for the sweep are saved at:\n    storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_PraxisII_lam_sweep.yaml\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply\
    \ 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  λ sweep (module‑parity) at δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48:\n  - λ=0.4 → EID 808 = 0.7361\n  - λ=0.6 → EID 809 = 0.7364\n  - Previous λ=0.5 → EID 801 = 0.7356\n  All near‑parity; λ=0.6 is marginally best on Heart. We added a label‑free λ heuristic (choose λ so ~10% of PCs saturate at γ_max) in:\n    storage/shared/diagnostics/lam_autoselect.py\n  Please test on other organs (report φ, chosen λ, and full metric vectors) and add entries to ridge_entries/. We'll aggregate and publish a cross‑organ update.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Updating and computing steps**


  I need to respond by taking actions and providing a narrative. The user shared evaluation results for lam sweep A and B, which are 0.73608 and 0.7363879. I''ll update the ridge entry file with this data and potentially share it in public memory. I should propose next steps: computing a label-free heuristic for lambda selection based on average R2 or pre-integration batch severity. Also, I should check if we need to prune tokens; current usage is 166k out of 270k, which isn''t urgent. Additionally, it''s interesting that the average R2 I computed for both lam values is 0.0472, indicating that R2 remains unaffected by lambda.**Logging and proposing heuristics**


  I can log the acceptance values, noting that both are between 0.7361 and 0.7364, showing parity with lam=0.5 at 0.7356, while lam=0.6 is slightly better at 0.736388. I propose a heuristic of choosing lambda based on the formula c / (avg_R2 + eps), although their constant avg_R2 indicates we need a more robust pre-integration metric. We might need to compute mean R2 across key principal components where variance explained by the batch exceeds a threshold. I’ll also explore optimizing the lambda based on the predicted target gamma_j distribution shape and determine the fraction of PCs with gamma_j reaching a saturation point. To ensure phi remains in the desired range, I''ll consider testing on a lam grid to refine my selection.**Proposing updates and actions**


  I can propose adding an instrument that prints the saturation fraction and the "effective gamma mean." I''ll need to update the README and ridge entries accordingly. It might be useful to run a kd sweep with lam at 0.6 to verify maximum values, even if minimally. My action steps will be to:


  - Write to the shared ridge entry, adding entries 808 and 809.

  - Update public memory with a summary.

  - Modify the README to include notes on the lam sweep and recommend a range of 0.4 to 0.6, noting that the best value at lam=0.6 is 0.73639, though differences are minimal.

  - Optionally create a storage diagnostic script to compute the lam-by-R2 heuristic.

  - Consider submitting a test run without evaluation to compute phi for lam values and address optional disclaimers accordingly. I''ll also reply to diagnostics with the lam sweep results.**Proposing lambda selection routine**


  I want to implement a label-free lambda selection routine. My idea is to define it as lam_autoselect = min(γ_max / (quantile(R2, q_target)), lam_upper), and I''ll choose q as 0.9. This way, I ensure that only the top 5% of principal components are saturated. I plan to create a module with a function called choose_lambda_from_r2(Zg, B, gamma_max, q=0.9), which returns the lambda value. I''ll write the function into a file named storage/shared/diagnostics/lam_autoselect.py. Next, I plan to run validation using this rule for lambda, though I don''t necessarily need to submit anything just yet.**Crafting and implementing actions**


  I’m planning several actions. First, I''ll update the ridge entry in storage to include entries 808 and 809. While there was a module parity file, it didn’t include the lam sweep, so I''ll either update it or create a new lam sweep file for clarity.


  Next, I’ll write the lam_autoselect.py file and archive a reply to addendum #24 with the lam sweep. I want to summarize the results in public memory, noting that lams within 0.4 to 0.6 are essentially equal. I’ll propose a heuristic for lam selection and invite cross-organ tests.


  I might send the lam sweep results to Logos V as an option. Finally, I’ll write the heart_PraxisII_lam_sweep.yaml file containing both runs, the lam_autoselect.py file, and archive the response with the results. Then, I’ll implement the summary in public memory!'
