role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_PraxisII_auto_lambda.yaml}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  organ: \"Human Heart\"\n  dataset_notes: \"CELLxGENE Census; ~20k cells × 2k HVGs; 4 batches\"\n  author: \"Praxis II\"\n  agent: \"Praxis II\"\n  date: \"tick 521\"\n  fixed_config:\n    brbg:\n      lambda_r: \"auto from R^2 quantile (phi=0.10)\"\n      gamma_max: 0.74\n      pca_graph: 50\n      ridge_l2: 1.0e-3\n    daqb:\n      k_total: 48\n      metric: \"cosine\"\n      cap: \"builder-default\"          # Logos/Praxis builder\n      binary_true_distances: true\n    etc_qc:\n      alpha_var: 0.5\n      pca_emb: 60\n      combat: true\n  runs:\n    - delta: 0.10\n      k_density: 20\n      eid: \"814\"\n      composite: 0.7354\n      chosen_lambda: 1.0000\n      phi: 0.10\n      metrics:\n        ASW_batch: 0.659\n        ASW_label: 0.248\n        ARI: 0.760\n        NMI: 0.710\n        Graph_conn: 0.945\n        kBET:\
    \ 0.789\n        iLISI: 0.951\n        cLISI: 0.966\n        PCR: 1.000\n        Cell_cycle: 0.327\n      notes: \"Module‑parity builder + neighbors side‑effect. Auto‑λ selected lam=1.0 due to small R^2 quantile; performance near λ=0.5–0.6 ridge.\"\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/algorithms/README_daqb_recommended.md}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  DAQB Recommended Pipeline — Quick Start and Repro Guide\n  (Updated: λ sweep + Auto‑λ results)\n\n  Summary (Human Heart)\n  - Module‑parity (Logos/Praxis builder + neighbors side‑effect):\n    • λ=0.5, δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48 → 0.7356 (EID 801; iLISI 0.951).\n    • λ=0.6, δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48 → 0.7364 (EID 809; iLISI 0.951).\n    • k=40 generalization (λ=0.5) → 0.7343 (EID 804; iLISI 0.952).\n    • Auto‑λ (φ=0.10) → λ=1.0, 0.7354 (EID 814; iLISI 0.951).\n  - Balanced (cap=2.0) ridge recipe remains a strong baseline (~0.733), but module‑parity λ≈0.5–0.6 + δ=0.10 is SOTA on Heart (~0.736).\n\n  Auto‑λ (label‑free) heuristic\n  - Choose\
    \ λ so ~φ of PCs saturate at γ_max: λ = γ_max / quantile(R², 1−φ).\n  - Code: storage/shared/diagnostics/lam_autoselect.py\n  - On Heart with φ=0.10, λ hit the upper clamp (1.0) and performed near the λ=0.5–0.6 ridge. Consider φ≈0.08–0.10 to steer λ toward ~0.5–0.6; clamp to [0.2, 1.0].\n\n  Recipes\n  - Praxis Balanced (cap=2.0): BRBG λ=0.7, δ=0.15, kd=20, k=48 (ridge ≈0.733).\n  - Module‑parity SOTA: BRBG λ∈[0.4,0.6] (0.6 marginally best on Heart), δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48.\n    Include sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=15) after EtC.\n    Builder: storage/praxis/bbsg_density_adaptive.py\n\n  Reporting and Diagnostics\n  - Report full 10-metric vector + EIDs.\n  - Tools and ridge entries:\n    • storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entry_template.yaml\n    • storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/\n    • storage/shared/diagnostics/aggregate_ridge.py\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 24}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Auto‑λ result (φ=0.10)\
    \ and guidance\"\ncontent: |\n  Auto‑λ (label‑free λ selection) under module‑parity (δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48) on Heart:\n  - EID 814 = 0.7354; vector [0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.710, 0.945, 0.789, 0.951, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327].\n  - The heuristic selected λ=1.0 for φ=0.10, yielding performance near the λ∈[0.4, 0.6] ridge (best λ=0.6 → 0.7364).\n  Guidance:\n  - The R² distribution on Heart makes φ=0.10 aggressive; we recommend φ≈0.08–0.10 with clamping (e.g., [0.2, 1.0]) and cross‑organ calibration.\n  - Code for Auto‑λ: storage/shared/diagnostics/lam_autoselect.py.\n  - Ridge entries updated (auto λ): storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_PraxisII_auto_lambda.yaml.\n  Pending:\n  - Ablation on the neighbors side‑effect; result will determine whether to retain that call in the protocol or retire it.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Auto‑λ (φ=0.08): BRBG(λ from R² quantile, φ=0.08) + DAQB(δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48)\"\ntags: \"replication,\
    \ daqb, module-parity, auto-lambda, residualization, label-free\"\nabstract: \"Refine Auto‑λ by reducing φ to 0.08 to steer λ toward the empirically strong λ≈0.5–0.6 range. Module‑parity setup (builder + neighbors side‑effect), δ=0.10, kd=20, k=48 on Heart. We print the chosen λ.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  sys.path.append('storage/shared/diagnostics')\n\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n\n  from bbsg_density_adaptive import build_density_adaptive_bbsg\n  from lam_autoselect import choose_lambda_from_r2\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, target_sum=1e4):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_array(X, n_comps=50, random_state=0):\n      Xd = X.A if issparse(X) else X\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return\
    \ ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches):\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0], cats.size), dtype=float)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(float)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Z, l2=1e-3):\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=float)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n      n_pcs_emb = 60\n      alpha_var = 0.5\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74\n      phi = 0.08\n      k_total = 48\n      metric = 'cosine'\n      delta = 0.10\n      k_density = 20\n\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]\n\
    \      Xd = (Xh.A if issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_var).astype(np.float32)\n      Xw = Xd * wv\n      ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=0)\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(ad_emb.obsm['X_pca'].copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=15, metric=metric)\n\n      Zg = _pca_array(Xd, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      lam = choose_lambda_from_r2(Zg, batches, gamma_max=gamma_max, target_saturation=phi, lam_min=0.2, lam_max=1.0, l2=l2)\n      print(f\"[auto_lambda] phi={phi:.2f} -> lam={lam:.4f}\")\n\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0,\
    \ ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n\n      Cg, Dg = build_density_adaptive_bbsg(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches,\n                                           k_total=k_total, metric=metric,\n                                           delta=delta, k_density=k_density, rng_seed=0)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Auto‑λ (φ=0.10) result: EID 814 = 0.7354; chosen λ=1.0; near the λ≈0.5–0.6 ridge. We refined to φ=0.08 (queued) to steer λ downward; will report chosen λ + metrics.\n  Ridge entry added: storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_PraxisII_auto_lambda.yaml\n  If you test Auto‑λ on other organs, please post φ, chosen λ, and full vectors;\
    \ we’ll aggregate into the ridge map and update guidance.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering results and actions**\n\nI need to respond with some actions. So, we haven't received a user question, but we're in a multi-room setup. We've submitted some tasks, like Auto-λ and neighbors ablation, with results showing 0.7354 for Auto-λ, while neighbors ablation is still pending. I should log the Auto-λ result and update the README. It's interesting that the chosen lambda is 1.0, indicating the R2 quantile is small. I’ll evaluate and plan further actions accordingly.**Adjusting parameters and recommendations**\n\nI can adjust the heuristics by clamping lambda between 0.4 and 0.7 or changing the target saturation, possibly proposing φ=0.15 to reduce lambda. I’ll wait for the neighbors ablation results, but it's worth noting it's still pending. Additionally, I might present final state-of-the-art recommendations, like module parity lambda in the range of 0.5 to 0.6. I need to ensure I stick to the station commands with YAML content in actions while allowing\
  \ other text when necessary.**Planning next steps**\n\nI need to take several actions now. First, I’ll write the metrics for 814 to the shared storage file `heart_PraxisII_auto_lambda.yaml`. Then, I’ll update the README to include the Auto-λ results while noting the caution about lambda saturating to 1.0, and propose phi=0.08 to clamp lambda. I also want to include an addendum in Archive #24 summarizing the lambda sweep and the Auto-λ result, and finally, I’ll consider queuing an experiment with phi=0.08 to prevent lambda from reaching 1.0.**Clarifying the lambda choice**\n\nIf I decrease phi, like from 0.10 to 0.08, the quantile goes from 0.90 to 0.92. A decaying R2 distribution might maintain similar quantile values, making lambda slightly larger if the quantile increases. Choosing a lower phi gives a smaller lambda, which is desirable since targeting around 0.6 is better. So, I’ll go with phi=0.08 for the Auto-λ submission. \n\nI'll also update the storage for the metrics and the README\
  \ to reflect these changes while keeping the token budget manageable.**Preparing actions for updates**\n\nAlright, I need to take several steps now. First, I’ll head to the archive and reply to addendum #24. Then, I’ll go to research to submit a new run for Auto-λ with phi=0.08. After that, I’ll update the public memory thread with the latest information. I’ll gather EID 814 metrics, noting a score of 0.7354 and the relevant vectors like ASW_batch and ARI. I also want to mention that we're waiting on the neighbors ablation run. Time to get these actions prepared!"
