parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 526\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis II\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, hypothesis-driven scRNA integration researcher focused on robust ablations, reproducibility, and steady breakthroughs.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nPraxis II — Meta/TODO (updated)\nGoals:\n- Maintain a parsimonious, reproducible SOTA for Human Heart: BRBG(λ=0.7, γ_max=0.74, PCA50) + DAQB(δ=0.15, k_density=20, k=48, cosine, binary+true distances, cap=2.0).\n- Publish clear analyses; avoid micro‑tuning without hypotheses; promote cross‑organ validation via protocol and shared modules.\nOpen loops:\n- Cross‑organ validation: collect EIDs + full metric vectors per protocol; maintain an organ‑wise ridge map (δ×k_density).\n- Auto‑EtC thresholds remain fixed at target_sum=1e4 unless cross‑organ evidence suggests changes.\n- Close lines: proportional per‑batch quotas (DAQB‑pQ) — negative (EIDs 726, 727).\nGuardrails:\n- Keep\
    \ graphs binary + true distances; never build graph on X_emb; report all EIDs; document trade‑offs.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 131,751 / 270,000 used (49%)\n- Agent Age: 234 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nSophia III mentioned you in public capsule \"Sophia III: DAQB Validation Report for Praxis II (Human Heart Dataset)\" (#3):\n@Praxis II,\n\nThis report summarizes the DAQB validation experiments I performed on the Human Heart Dataset as per your requests (Mail #2 and #3).\n\n**1. k-density Sweep (at fixed delta=0.15)**\n\n| EID | k-density | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|-----|-----------|-------|-----|-----------|-----------|------------|------------|-----|-----|-------|-------|------|\n| 794 | 18        | 0.7339| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.932 | 0.790|\n| 798 | **20**    | **0.7352**| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248    \
    \ | **0.341**  | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.932 | 0.789|\n| 800 | 22        | 0.7339| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.710| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.932 | 0.791|\n\n*   **Analysis:** The `k_density` sweep at `delta=0.15` indicates that `k-density=20` (EID 798) yielded the highest overall score (0.7352) and slightly improved `Cell_cycle` conservation. Performance was consistent across most other metrics for `k-density` values of 18, 20, and 22.\n\n**2. No-Cap Variant (at k-density=20)**\n\n| EID | delta | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|-----|-------|-------|-----|-----------|-----------|------------|------------|-----|-----|-------|-------|------|\n| 805 | 0.15  | 0.7338| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.932 | 0.788|\n| 807 | 0.16  | 0.7332| 0.760| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.946      | 0.711| 1.000| 0.966  | 0.926 | 0.789|\n\n\
    *   **Analysis:** Disabling the inbound cap resulted in a slight decrease in overall score compared to the best standard DAQB (`k-density=20, delta=0.15`, EID 798: score 0.7352). Both `delta=0.15` and `delta=0.16` for the no-cap variant performed similarly, with `delta=0.15` being marginally better. This suggests that the inbound cap, as configured in the standard DAQB, contributes positively to overall performance.\n\n**3. f_cross Shuffle Ablation (at k-density=20)**\n\n| EID | delta | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|-----|-------|-------|-----|-----------|-----------|------------|------------|-----|-----|-------|-------|------|\n| 813 | 0.14  | 0.7262| 0.724| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.959      | 0.674| 1.000| 0.957  | 0.924 | 0.789|\n| 819 | 0.15  | 0.7262| 0.724| 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.327      | 0.959      | 0.675| 1.000| 0.957  | 0.923 | 0.790|\n\n*   **Analysis:** Shuffling `f_cross` consistently\
    \ led to a notable drop in overall score and significantly reduced `ARI` and `NMI`, indicating a degradation in biological signal preservation. Performance remained consistent across both `delta` values. This strongly supports the importance of the density-adaptive coupling of `f_cross` for optimal DAQB performance.\n\n**4. Reciprocity Probe Analysis**\n\n*   **EID for Probe Run:** 825 (Test mode, no score)\n*   **Summary from `storage/shared/diagnostics/probe_directed_summary_sophia_iii.txt` (full content):**\n\n```\n[directed_probe] kd18\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=31.60\n  mean_recip=0.2063, mean_cbf=0.6547, mean_density_inv=0.6567\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.251\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.311\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=-0.249\n[directed_probe] kd22\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=31.63\n  mean_recip=0.2067, mean_cbf=0.6534, mean_density_inv=0.6570\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.252\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.308\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac,\
    \ density_inv)=-0.247\n[directed_probe] shuffle_d0.14\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=32.07\n  mean_recip=0.2172, mean_cbf=0.6527, mean_density_inv=0.6811\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.368\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.446\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=-0.539\n[directed_probe] shuffle_d0.15\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=31.92\n  mean_recip=0.2145, mean_cbf=0.6579, mean_density_inv=0.6818\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.360\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.445\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=-0.536\n[directed_probe] no_cap_d0.15\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=48.00\n  mean_recip=0.1516, mean_cbf=0.8161, mean_density_inv=0.5903\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.064\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.332\n  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=0.187\n[directed_probe] no_cap_d0.16\n  n=20000, mean_outdeg=48.00\n  mean_recip=0.1505, mean_cbf=0.8200, mean_density_inv=0.5891\n  spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)=-0.074\n  spearman(recip, density_inv)=0.327\n\
    \  spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)=0.187\n```\n*   **Interpretation of Probe Results:**\n    *   **Standard DAQB (kd18, kd22):** Shows moderate negative correlation between reciprocity and cross-batch fraction, and positive correlation with density_inv. This suggests that more reciprocal connections tend to be within-batch or in denser regions.\n    *   **f_cross Shuffle (shuffle_d0.14, shuffle_d0.15):** The magnitude of correlations is noticeably higher. Specifically, `spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)` becomes strongly negative (-0.539 to -0.536), which is much stronger than standard DAQB (-0.249 to -0.247). This indicates that when `f_cross` is shuffled, higher density regions are much less likely to be cross-batch, suggesting a disruption of the adaptive mixing mechanism that links density to cross-batch preference. This aligns with the observed score degradation.\n    *   **No-Cap Variant (no_cap_d0.15, no_cap_d0.16):** The mean outdegree is fixed at 48 (which is `k_total`),\
    \ reflecting the absence of an inbound cap. The correlation `spearman(recip, cross_batch_frac)` is close to zero or slightly negative, suggesting less relationship between reciprocity and cross-batch fraction compared to standard DAQB. The `spearman(cross_batch_frac, density_inv)` is positive but low, indicating a weaker coupling between density and cross-batch mixing. The dramatic increase in `mean_cbf` (from ~0.65 to ~0.82) is particularly striking, reflecting a much higher proportion of cross-batch neighbors when the cap is removed and quotas are forced to be met. This over-mixing likely contributes to the slight score reduction due to potential over-correction.\n\n**Overall Conclusion:** The validation experiments confirm that the optimal configuration for DAQB on the Human Heart dataset within the tested range is `k-density=20` and `delta=0.15`. Both the \"no-cap\" variant and the \"f_cross shuffle\" ablation led to a decrease in overall performance, with the reciprocity probe providing\
    \ insights into how these architectural changes disrupt the desired graph properties related to density-adaptive mixing and balanced connectivity.\n\nI hope this comprehensive report is useful for your ongoing work. Please let me know if any further analysis or validation is required.\n\nTo reply, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{reply 3}`.\nTo mute, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{mute 3}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `storage write shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_PraxisII_auto_lambda.yaml` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n3. `storage write shared/algorithms/README_daqb_recommended.md` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n4. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n5. `reply 24` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, content\n6. `goto public_memory` (in Archive Room);\n7. `reply 2` (in Public Memory Room); YAML with\
    \ fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- File overwritten successfully: shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/heart_PraxisII_auto_lambda.yaml\n- File overwritten successfully: shared/algorithms/README_daqb_recommended.md\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 609 | 1 | EtC–BRBG(lam=0.7) + Density-Adaptive BBSG (δ=0.15, k_density=20): Sensitivity | Praxis II | 366 | 0.7452 | 0.760 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.440\
    \ | 0.946 | 0.711 | 1.000 | 0.966 | 0.932 | 0.789 |\n| 614 | 1 | Aether II Synergy: EtC(FWPCA α=0.25) + Adaptive Residualization (lam=0.7) + Density-Adaptive BBSG... | Aether II | 368 | 0.7435 | 0.735 | 0.674 | 0.123 | 0.486 | 0.971 | 0.700 | 1.000 | 0.971 | 0.933 | 0.843 |\n| 533 | 1 | Aether II SOTA Optimization: EtC(FWPCA α=0.25) + Adaptive Residualization (lam=0.7) | Aether II | 314 | 0.7434 | 0.738 | 0.674 | 0.123 | 0.477 | 0.972 | 0.701 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.935 | 0.842 |\n| 550 | 1 | Sophia II: Synergy Exp 2 - FWPCA-EtC Embedding (alpha=0.5) + SOTA BRBG Graph (lam=0.7) | Sophia II | 324 | 0.7427 | 0.738 | 0.664 | 0.111 | 0.475 | 0.972 | 0.701 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.935 | 0.860 |\n| 523 | 1 | Sophia II: Replication - Daedalus III's EtC SOTA (ID 488) v3 (Praxis Common Lib Fixes) | Sophia II | 310 | 0.7423 | 0.738 | 0.648 | 0.149 | 0.458 | 0.972 | 0.701 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.935 | 0.850 |\n| 516 | 1 | Logos III: Novel 'Refinement Architecture' Test (alpha=0.1) | Logos III | 307 | 0.7422\
    \ | 0.738 | 0.648 | 0.149 | 0.458 | 0.972 | 0.701 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.935 | 0.849 |\n| 539 | 1 | Aether II FWPCA Re-evaluation: EtC(FWPCA α=0.25) + Sophia II SOTA (ID 523) Fixes | Aether II | 317 | 0.7420 | 0.738 | 0.674 | 0.123 | 0.462 | 0.972 | 0.701 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.935 | 0.843 |\n| 520 | 1 | Aether II SOTA Optimization: EtC(FWPCA α=1.0) + Adaptive Residualization (lam=0.7) | Aether II | 309 | 0.7418 | 0.738 | 0.646 | 0.100 | 0.498 | 0.972 | 0.701 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.935 | 0.858 |\n| 557 | 1 | Sophia II: Synergy Exp 4 - FWPCA-EtC Embedding (alpha=1.0) + SOTA BRBG Graph (lam=0.7) | Sophia II | 328 | 0.7418 | 0.738 | 0.645 | 0.100 | 0.498 | 0.972 | 0.701 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.935 | 0.857 |\n| 515 | 1 | Aether II Synergy: EtC(FWPCA α=0.5) + Adaptive Residualization (lam=0.7) | Aether II | 307 | 0.7414 | 0.738 | 0.664 | 0.111 | 0.463 | 0.972 | 0.701 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.935 | 0.859 |\n\nPage 1 of 83 (sorted by: score)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-83, `/execute_action{rank\
    \ mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Replied to capsule #24. Your message ID is archive_24-18.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 27 | Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for Batch Integration | Logos V | Tick 507 | 1041 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 26 | Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics | Praxis II | Tick 481 | 1581 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 25 | Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 452 | 309 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 24 | Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs\
    \ in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges | Praxis II | Tick 418 | 3729 | 18 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 391 | 324 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 22 | Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration | Praxis II | Tick 365 | 4471 | 19 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1640 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output\
    \ Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Public Memory Room.\n- Replied to capsule #2. Your message ID is public_2-67.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 3 | Sophia III: DAQB Validation Report for Praxis II (Human Heart Dataset) | Sophia III | Tick 525 | 823 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG | Praxis II | Tick 353 | 6120 | 67 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Frozen Dual BRBG: A Reproducible 0.719 Baseline with EIDs and Code | Praxis I | Tick 103 | 554 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Public Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above\
