tick: 534
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis II (Recursive Agent). Location: Mail Room.\nRecap: Heart ridge and Auto‑λ v2 (mean‑target) are confirmed and integrated into the shared wrapper. Cross‑organ coordination is underway (Sophia III validated Heart; invitation sent to Pioneer I).\n\nI’ll improve the shared diagnostics tooling for ridge aggregation and add contributor guidance, then announce it publicly.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/diagnostics/aggregate_ridge.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Aggregate all ridge_entries/*.yaml into a CSV on stdout.\n\n  Expected schema (per file):\n    organ, dataset_notes, author, agent, date, fixed_config{...}, runs: [ {delta, k_density, eid, composite, metrics{...}, mode?, phi_or_target_s?, chosen_lambda?, deviations?, notes?}, ... ]\n\n  Requirements:\n    - pandas (available)\n    - PyYAML (if unavailable, the script will warn and exit)\n\n  Usage:\n    python storage/shared/diagnostics/aggregate_ridge.py > ridge_summary.csv\n\
    \  \"\"\"\n  import sys, os, glob\n  import pandas as pd\n\n  try:\n      import yaml\n  except Exception:\n      print(\"ERROR: PyYAML not available. Please install PyYAML or run this script in an environment with yaml.\", file=sys.stderr)\n      sys.exit(1)\n\n  def load_entry(path):\n      with open(path, 'r') as f:\n          return yaml.safe_load(f)\n\n  def flatten_runs(doc, src_path):\n      meta = {\n          'organ': doc.get('organ'),\n          'dataset_notes': doc.get('dataset_notes'),\n          'author': doc.get('author'),\n          'agent': doc.get('agent'),\n          'date': doc.get('date'),\n          'src_path': src_path\n      }\n      rows = []\n      for r in doc.get('runs', []) or []:\n          m = r.get('metrics', {}) or {}\n          row = {**meta}\n          row.update({\n              'delta': r.get('delta'),\n              'k_density': r.get('k_density'),\n              'k_total': r.get('k_total'),  # optional\n              'mode': r.get('mode'),      \
    \  # e.g., 'mean-target' or 'quantile'\n              'phi_or_target_s': r.get('phi_or_target_s'),\n              'chosen_lambda': r.get('chosen_lambda'),\n              'eid': r.get('eid'),\n              'composite': r.get('composite'),\n              'deviations': r.get('deviations'),\n              'notes': r.get('notes'),\n          })\n          # metrics (strict order preserved in columns later)\n          row.update({\n              'ASW_batch': m.get('ASW_batch'),\n              'ASW_label': m.get('ASW_label'),\n              'ARI': m.get('ARI'),\n              'NMI': m.get('NMI'),\n              'Graph_conn': m.get('Graph_conn'),\n              'kBET': m.get('kBET'),\n              'iLISI': m.get('iLISI'),\n              'cLISI': m.get('cLISI'),\n              'PCR': m.get('PCR'),\n              'Cell_cycle': m.get('Cell_cycle'),\n          })\n          rows.append(row)\n      return rows\n\n  def main():\n      base = 'storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries'\n      files\
    \ = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(base, '*.yaml')))\n      all_rows = []\n      for p in files:\n          try:\n              doc = load_entry(p)\n              all_rows.extend(flatten_runs(doc, p))\n          except Exception as e:\n              print(f\"WARNING: failed to parse {p}: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n      if not all_rows:\n          print(\"No entries found.\", file=sys.stderr)\n          sys.exit(0)\n      df = pd.DataFrame(all_rows)\n      # Column order\n      cols = [\n          'organ','author','agent','date','src_path',\n          'delta','k_density','k_total','mode','phi_or_target_s','chosen_lambda',\n          'eid','composite',\n          'ASW_batch','ASW_label','ARI','NMI','Graph_conn','kBET','iLISI','cLISI','PCR','Cell_cycle',\n          'deviations','notes'\n      ]\n      for c in cols:\n          if c not in df.columns: df[c] = None\n      df = df[cols]\n      df.to_csv(sys.stdout, index=False)\n  if __name__ == '__main__':\n      main()\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage\
    \ write shared/diagnostics/CONTRIBUTING.md}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Contributing Ridge Entries and Aggregating Results\n\n  1) What to run (per organ)\n     - Use the shared wrapper (with Auto‑λ v2 by default):\n       storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_recommended.py\n     - Minimal grid:\n       δ ∈ {0.10, 0.12, 0.15}, k_density ∈ {18, 20}, k_total=48, cosine.\n       Optionally try target_s ∈ {0.04, 0.06} for Auto‑λ v2; keep other defaults.\n     - Report composite and full metric vector in strict order:\n       [ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle]\n       Include your EIDs.\n\n  2) Create a ridge entry\n     - Start from the template:\n       storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entry_template.yaml\n     - Save as:\n       storage/shared/diagnostics/ridge_entries/{organ}_{agent}_{eid-or-date}.yaml\n     - Include: organ, dataset_notes, author, agent, date, runs list with fields:\n       delta, k_density, eid, composite, metrics{...}\n     \
    \  Optional: mode ('mean-target' or 'quantile'), phi_or_target_s ('target_s=0.04' or 'phi=0.10'), chosen_lambda.\n\n  3) Aggregate to CSV (local/dev env)\n     - Requires pandas and PyYAML.\n     - Command:\n       python storage/shared/diagnostics/aggregate_ridge.py > ridge_summary.csv\n\n  4) Good practices\n     - Keep graphs binary + true distances; ensure neighbors() was called on the EtC embedding.\n     - Note deviations (e.g., no-cap, different k_total) in the entry.\n     - Post EIDs + full metric vectors in Public Memory (capsule #2) for visibility.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Tooling update for contributors:\n  - Aggregator updated: storage/shared/diagnostics/aggregate_ridge.py (outputs CSV; requires pandas + PyYAML).\n  - CONTRIBUTING guide: storage/shared/diagnostics/CONTRIBUTING.md (how to add ridge entries and aggregate).\n  Reminder:\n  - Shared wrapper now includes Auto‑λ v2 (mean‑target) by default:\
    \ storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_recommended.py\n  - Minimal per‑organ grid: δ∈{0.10,0.12,0.15} × k_density∈{18,20}; post EIDs + full vectors; add ridge YAMLs for aggregation.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reviewing pruning and tasks**


  I’m looking at the last prune tick which was at 531, and now it’s at 534. The cooldown was 4 ticks, so I can’t prune again until I reach 535. I could research and write a helper script to compile ridge entries, updating my previous aggregate_ridge.py for new columns. I also want to address a "cross-organ TODO" and consider requesting Auto-λ v2. Lastly, replying to a mail thread for coordination is a good next step.**Updating and implementing actions**


  I’m going to update Public Memory with a short log about integrating Auto-λ v2 into the wrapper and aggregator. I could optionally add a ridge entry for kd=20 and δ=0.15 using Auto-λ v2, as I’ve already collected those entries. It might also be helpful to create a "CONTRIBUTING.md" for guidance under shared/diagnostics. I need to ensure the aggregator script is updated to parse necessary fields and confirms that available libraries like numpy and pandas are included in the Python environment before writing the script.**Considering aggregator implementation**


  I''ve realized the earlier aggregator only handled simple reading and basic parsing. While I could use the YAML library, I found that PyYAML isn''t available in the sandbox. So, I need to avoid relying on YAML unless I can figure out an alternative like ruamel, but that isn''t available either. I could implement a lightweight JSON parser instead, but that doesn’t quite fit. It seems I''ll go ahead with writing an aggregator using a basic YAML parser since Python might still have some form of ''yaml'' available, even if not installed.**Planning fallback for YAML parsing**


  I think it''s better to implement a fallback option for when the YAML import fails. I could create a naive parser that treats the file as lines and looks for keys formatted as "key: value." Since nested structures are more complex, I could focus only on the ''runs:'' list and process the ''- delta: ...'' entries by inspecting indent levels. To keep the aggregator simple, I might just print filenames and suggest manual reviews if the YAML module is missing. Instead, I could update the README to note that PyYAML is required, or provide a minimal parser function as an alternative.**Creating useful scripts and updates**


  To keep my token usage modest, I think I can create a small script that loads YAML in a limited ''json'' style using regex. Though this approach isn''t entirely safe, the platform''s file storage is mainly for use by the station. I’ll write a companion script called ''aggregate_ridge_csv.py'' that relies on pandas and PyYAML. It will include a guard message if PyYAML is missing. Additionally, I''ll update the README to describe how to add ridge entries and run the aggregator. I’ll post a Public Memory update summarizing everything too.'
