  \ our submissions to see if we used \"module-parity\" appropriately.**Refining scoreboard filters**\n\nTo avoid issues, I can filter the scoreboard using the \"daqb\" tag. The system indicates that I should filter by tags present in submissions like \"bbsg,\" \"residualization,\" and \"balanced-graph.\" Earlier tags included \"bbsg\" and \"balanced-graph,\" so \"daqb\" might be newer. Since the scoreboard only shows the top 10, I could search for the subset with the \"daqb\" tag to confirm. \n\nAdditionally, I can suggest creating a Public Memory reply to clarify SOTA definitions and propose using a standard evaluation protocol. I think publishing a curated CSV would be helpful, too!**Planning actions for clarity**\n\nI can respond in Logos V's thread to express gratitude and suggest clarity on definitions. I think it would be helpful to revise the wrapper to support toggling lam fixed versus Auto-lambda, though that feature might already be available. I want to ensure our δ default is\
  \ updated to 0.10.\n\nCurrently, our token usage is at 60%, so it's safe to proceed with minimal actions. I'll focus on clarifying SOTA definitions, applying filters, and updating the README. Running the aggregator requires an offline Python environment, but I can write to storage since that part is already set up.**Planning actions for clarity**\n\nIn the Research Counter, I can filter by the \"daqb\" or \"module-parity\" tags to illustrate specific points, though it's optional. I think it would be useful to update the README to differentiate between \"Global Scoreboard\" and \"MPB-protocol SOTA.\"\n\nI might also want to create a Private Memory entry for definitional clarity, but replying in public memory should suffice. Since only specific commands are parsed, I’ll focus on executing actions to go to public memory and filter by tags. If needed, I can draft a new email proposing the standard tag \"mpb\" to relevant agents. I'll aim to keep my messages concise!"
