  \ with a lambda sweep, trying values of 0.6 and 0.7 to start. Each agent can run up to two experiments concurrently, and since we currently have none running, we can go ahead with the submissions.**Planning submissions and updates**\n\nI'm preparing to submit two new evaluations: one with fixed λ at 0.6 and another using Auto-λ v2 with δ values of 0.12 and possibly 0.15, but I’ll limit it to two.\n\nFor my actions, I should head to research since we're currently in the Archive Room. I’ll write the ridge entry YAML for Heart MPB Graph FWPCA, including results from 845 and 846, although I need to gather the full vector for the 846 score. Updating heart_ridge_summary.csv and replying to public threads about our replication progress are next. Finally, I'll submit the new evaluations based on the outlined parameters.**Planning code integration and file updates**\n\nI need to include some code in my tasks. I can reuse the code from earlier but may also create a helper module in storage to reduce\
  \ duplication. \n\nFirst, I’ll head to research. Then, I’ll write the ridge entries for heart_PraxisII_graphfwpca.yaml, capturing the 845 and 846 metrics. For the heart_ridge_summary.csv, I should append new lines but rewriting the current content will lose previous entries. Instead, I might create a new file called heart_ridge_summary_update2.csv or rely on the YAML new file and aggregator for updates. So, I’ll hold off on updating the CSV for now.**Confirming results and submitting evaluations**\n\nI need to go through a series of actions. First, I'll head to public_memory to reply to message 5 with confirmation about our replication and the updated MPB protocol SOTA of 0.7445, while noting the global top score remains at 0.7452. \n\nNext, I’ll reply to message 24 in the archive, and then I’ll go to research to submit the fixed λ at 0.6, ensuring to use the same code as before. I’ll also submit Auto-λ v2 with δ at 0.12. Lastly, I’ll organize the metric entries for both 846 and 845 to\
  \ include in the YAML."
