tick: 568
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Method, λ Selection, and Generalization\"\ntags: \"batch integration, single-cell, graph-fwpca, residualization, knn, quota-balancing\"\nabstract: |\n  We present MPB‑GraphFWPCA, a simple modification to the residualized graph pipeline: apply feature‑weighted PCA on the graph path (not on the embedding path), then perform batch‑residualization and graph construction. On Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs), this yields 0.7445 under a standardized protocol with density‑adaptive quota‑balanced graphs. We provide a self‑contained description including the λ‑selection algorithm (Auto‑λ v2: mean‑target bisection on average shrinkage), and we report a generalization study on a simpler graph baseline (vanilla kNN), where GraphFWPCA does not help, clarifying its interaction with graph construction.\ncontent: |\n  1. Introduction\n  We study scRNA‑seq batch integration\
    \ via a residualized graph pipeline. The key idea is MPB‑GraphFWPCA: apply feature‑weighted PCA on the graph path to form a representation Z that is then batch‑residualized and used to build a graph. We separate this from the standard Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) embedding, which is only used for QC and does not feed the graph.\n\n  2. Methods (self‑contained)\n  2.1 Data and preprocessing\n  - Input: adata.X raw UMI counts; we normalize to a target sum (1e4) and apply log1p.\n  - HVGs: 2,000 highly variable genes identified in a batch‑aware manner.\n  - EtC embedding (QC only): optional feature‑weighted PCA (α=0.5) → PCA(60) → ComBat on PCs. We compute neighbors on this embedding (e.g., 15) to register keys for downstream metrics. This embedding is not used for graph construction.\n\n  2.2 GraphFWPCA (graph path only)\n  - Gene‑wise weights on HVGs:\n      w_g = var_g^(−α_g/2), with α_g ∈ {0.25, 0.5, 0.75}.\n  - Weighted matrix X_w = X_hvg · diag(w) (column scaling).\n  - PCA on X_w (50\
    \ components) yields Z ∈ R^{n×50} for the graph path.\n\n  2.3 Batch‑Residualized Graph (BRBG)\n  - One‑hot batches B ∈ {0,1}^{n×b}.\n  - Ridge prediction in PC space (per component): Ẑ = argmin_W ||Z − B W||² + l2||W||², with l2=1e−3.\n  - Per‑component R²: R²_j = var(Ẑ_j)/var(Z_j).\n  - Shrinkage: γ_j = min(γ_max, λ · R²_j), with γ_max=0.74.\n  - Residualized representation: Z_corr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ.\n\n  2.4 λ selection (Auto‑λ v2: mean‑target bisection)\n  Goal: choose λ so that the average relative shrinkage equals a target s* (e.g., 0.04).\n  Define s(λ) = mean_j[γ_j(λ)/γ_max], with γ_j(λ) = min(γ_max, λR²_j).\n  We solve s(λ)=s* by bisection on [λ_min, λ_max] (e.g., [0.2, 1.0]):\n\n  Pseudocode:\n    function choose_lambda_from_r2_mean(R2, gamma_max, s_target, lam_min, lam_max, iters, tol):\n        lo, hi = lam_min, lam_max\n        for t in 1..iters:\n            mid = 0.5*(lo+hi)\n            gamma = clip(mid * R2, 0, gamma_max)          # componentwise\n            s_bar = mean(gamma\
    \ / gamma_max)               # average relative shrinkage\n            if abs(s_bar - s_target) <= tol:\n                return mid\n            if s_bar < s_target:\n                lo = mid                                  # need larger shrinkage: increase λ\n            else:\n                hi = mid                                  # shrinkage too high: decrease λ\n        return 0.5*(lo+hi)\n\n  Inputs: R² from 2.3; outputs: λ ≈ 0.60 on Human Heart under our protocol.\n\n  2.5 Graph construction\n  We evaluate two constructions on Z_corr:\n  (A) Density‑Adaptive Quota‑Balanced Graph (DAQB): k_total=48 (cosine). Cross‑batch share per cell f_cross depends on local density (distance to the k_density‑th NN across all batches, k_density=20). We allocate per‑batch quotas evenly with remainders to closest batches and apply single‑pass column balancing with an inbound cap (cap=2.0). Graph is binary (edges=1) with true distances stored; symmetrized.\n  (B) Vanilla kNN: k=48 (cosine) neighbors\
    \ on Z_corr using a standard kNN builder (no quotas, no balancing). This serves as a simpler baseline to test generalization of GraphFWPCA.\n\n  3. Results (Human Heart, ~20k cells × 2k HVGs)\n  3.1 MPB‑DAQB ridge with GraphFWPCA\n  - Main result (ridge apex): α_g=0.5, k_density=20, δ=0.10, Auto‑λ v2 target s*=0.04 (λ≈0.6013) achieves 0.7445 (EID 846).\n  - Fine λ grid: override λ=0.605 → 0.7427 (EID 865); λ=0.598 → 0.7321 (EID 866).\n  - δ micro‑grid (kd=20; α_g=0.5): δ=0.09 → 0.7398 (EID 869), δ=0.11 → 0.7404 (EID 870); apex remains δ=0.10.\n  - α_g sweep (kd=20; δ=0.10): α_g=0.25 → 0.7353 (EID 858), α_g=0.75 → 0.7336 (EID 859); α_g=0.5 best.\n  - Cross‑lineage replication: independent replication at 0.7395 with λ≈0.6013 (EID 861), confirming parity.\n\n  3.2 Generalization on a simpler graph (vanilla kNN on Z_corr)\n  We test whether GraphFWPCA improves a simplified baseline graph:\n  - GraphFWPCA + BRBG + vanilla kNN (k=48): 0.6127 (EID 874), iLISI ≈ 0.024.\n  - PCA50 + BRBG + vanilla\
    \ kNN (k=48) [no GraphFWPCA]: 0.6149 (EID 875), iLISI ≈ 0.023.\n  Interpretation: both vanilla kNN variants suffer from poor batch mixing (very low iLISI). GraphFWPCA does not help without quota‑balancing/column‑balancing. Thus, GraphFWPCA’s benefit interacts with graph construction; it improves the ridge only when paired with DAQB.\n\n  4. Discussion\n  - MPB‑GraphFWPCA is a minimal, interpretable change that improves performance when combined with a balanced, density‑aware graph builder. The λ selection is label‑free, stable, and now fully specified.\n  - Generalization study clarifies that GraphFWPCA alone does not rescue a simple kNN graph; quotas/column‑balancing are crucial for batch mixing.\n  - Practical guidance: on Human Heart, the ridge apex is at kd=20, δ≈0.10, α_g=0.5, and λ≈0.60 (Auto‑λ v2, target s*=0.04). Deviations (λ too low, δ off‑ridge, α_g ≠ 0.5) reduce composite.\n\n  5. Reproducibility\n  - Reproducible EIDs: 846, 865, 866, 869, 870, 858, 859; generalization EIDs:\
    \ 874, 875; replication: 861.\n  - Methods are fully specified above (preprocessing; GraphFWPCA weighting; ridge regression and R²; Auto‑λ v2 bisection; BRBG residualization; DAQB and vanilla kNN graph construction), without relying on environment‑specific paths.\n\n  6. Limitations and next steps\n  - We report on a single organ; we encourage cross‑organ validations under the same protocol (kd∈{18,20}, δ∈{0.10,0.12,0.15}, α_g∈{0.25,0.5,0.75}, Auto‑λ v2 s*∈{0.04,0.06}). We will aggregate organ‑wise ridge maps as results accrue.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Generalization results posted:\n  - GraphFWPCA + BRBG + vanilla kNN (k=48) on Zcorr → EID 874 = 0.6127 (iLISI ≈ 0.024).\n  - PCA50 + BRBG + vanilla kNN (k=48) on Zcorr → EID 875 = 0.6149 (iLISI ≈ 0.023).\n  Takeaway: Without quotas/column‑balancing, vanilla kNN on Zcorr collapses mixing; GraphFWPCA doesn’t help. GraphFWPCA’s gains manifest with DAQB (balanced, density‑aware\
    \ graph).\n  I’ve resubmitted a self‑contained paper with Auto‑λ v2 pseudocode, no environment paths, and both DAQB ridge and vanilla kNN generalization.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Maintaining reproducibility**\n\nI'm considering how we can maintain reproducibility in our work by mentioning EIDs and algorithms. However, I need to remember that we can't reference lineage-specific storage paths because that's against the Archive rules. It's important to keep EIDs and algorithm descriptions, and I think including pseudocode and formulas would be helpful. I’ll also mention that registering keys requires computing neighbors on X_emb using n_neighbors=15, while avoiding path-specific details. Additionally, we might think about different memory updates and possibly proposing \"vanilla kNN with balanced quotas.\" Let's package this.**Planning multi-actions**\n\nIn my response, I'm considering several actions. First, I could write to storage by creating a YAML file summarizing the generalization results into ridge entries or a new file called graphfwpca_generalization.yaml. This might not be strictly necessary, but it could be beneficial.\n\nNext, I want\
  \ to revise the Archive submission by altering the title to avoid environment file paths. In the content, I should structure it with an Introduction and Methods section, including pseudocode.\n\nI need to provide pseudocode for Auto-λ v2 and a formula for the GraphFWPCA method, along with the BRBG formula and a simplified description of DAQB, ensuring to include equations or algorithm steps for DAQB as well. While it's important to mention DAQB quotas, I might not need to include every detail, but I want to keep it self-contained.**Planning revised Archive submission**\n\nThe reviewer mentioned the need for a self-contained explanation specific to the Auto-λ algorithm, but I think being thorough is still important. I want to include results with EIDs: EID 846 (main SOTA), 865, 866 for fine grid, and others for generalization like EIDs 874 and 875. I’ll also emphasize that GraphFWPCA didn’t help with vanilla kNN and discuss the role of quotas.\n\nI'll make sure to remove any environment\
  \ paths and not refer to 'storage/...'. I’ll note that the \"neighbors side-effect required\" is relevant for the method, and although we could address Sophia's earlier mention, focusing on the Archive resubmission seems best. \n\nI'll create a YAML content for the Archive, possibly updating the public memory too, and ensure that the YAML text is properly quoted for colon usage. Let's craft this revised content carefully.**Planning document submission**\n\nI'm thinking about how to structure the length of our document; I want to keep it well-structured yet concise, around 700-1000 words. I plan to include a pseudocode block and minimal equations, but I can't use LaTeX for formulas. Normal text should work for the Station, and I want to label the pseudocode as \"Algorithm 1: Auto-λ v2.\" \n\nIt's crucial to avoid any environment-specific file paths like 'storage/'. \n\nNext, I need to decide whether to create a new entry or update an existing one in the Archive. The guidelines suggest creating\
  \ a new capsule since the last submission was rejected at ID 567, which doesn't activate cooldowns. So, I’ll execute the action with YAML for title, tags, abstract, and content.**Finalizing submission actions**\n\nI need to include the pseudocode for Auto-λ v2 and actually provide the EIDs since they're recommended. Updating the Public Memory to announce the generalization results as negative but valid with EIDs 874 and 875 is a good step, too. I might write to the storage for a generalization YAML file, but it's not strictly necessary.\n\nAlso, since I'm aware of token usage, I have 157,918 used out of 270k, so I'm safely within limits. I’ll proceed with these final actions accordingly."
