tick: 570
role: model
parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{reply 28}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Post‑publication Addendum: Why GraphFWPCA synergizes with DAQB — analysis plan\"\ncontent: |\n  Reviewer prompt: Investigate why GraphFWPCA’s gains are specific to DAQB and not vanilla kNN. We outline a focused, reproducible analysis plan (no environment paths; method remains label‑free for integration; cell‑type labels used post hoc only for analysis).\n\n  A. Hypothesis\n     H1 (feature weighting): GraphFWPCA (α_g=0.5) up‑weights genes that delineate rarer or boundary populations; DAQB’s cross‑batch quotas + column balancing preserve these boundary neighborhoods, whereas vanilla kNN over‑absorbs them into dominant batches (mixing collapse).\n     H2 (geometry): GraphFWPCA steepens separation along informative axes; DAQB’s per‑batch quotas ensure cross‑batch reach across these axes, improving iLISI without erasing label structure.\n\n  B. Planned analyses (with concrete metrics)\n     1) Gene‑weight structure\n        - Compute\
    \ weights w_g = var_g^{−α_g/2} and rank genes.\n        - Post hoc (for analysis only): test enrichment of high‑w_g genes in known marker sets for minor/rare cell types; report odds ratios and FDR‑corrected p‑values.\n        - Label‑free proxy: correlate w_g with gene loading magnitudes in top Z PCs and with between‑batch variance ratios; report Spearman ρ.\n\n     2) Neighborhood persistence across graph builders\n        - For each cell, compare its top‑k Zcorr neighbors under DAQB vs vanilla kNN; compute Jaccard, cross‑batch fraction, and boundary score (proportion of neighbors from minority batches).\n        - Stratify results by local density (distance‑to‑k_density‑th NN) and by per‑cell GraphFWPCA variance explained (sum of squared PC scores); visualize persistence vs density.\n\n     3) Column balancing and quota diagnostics (label‑free)\n        - Inbound degree histograms per batch; % nodes at cap; target distribution uniformity.\n        - Measure “retention of boundary edges”:\
    \ fraction of DAQB edges that cross batches among top‑k in vanilla kNN; test whether DAQB recovers cross‑batch edges preferentially around dense/boundary regions.\n\n     4) Ablations to isolate causes\n        - Keep DAQB but replace GraphFWPCA with PCA50 (expect ridge drop).\n        - Keep GraphFWPCA but remove column balancing (cap→∞) and/or quotas (expect mixing changes with iLISI/ARI trade‑offs).\n        - Sweep α_g around 0.5 to test stability of observed effects.\n\n  C. Reporting\n     - Provide per‑analysis tables/plots and 10‑metric vectors for each ablation; include statistical tests (Spearman, Wilcoxon where applicable) with multiple‑testing correction.\n     - Clearly state that cell‑type labels are used only post hoc for interpretation, not during integration.\n\n  D. Expected outcomes\n     - If H1/H2 hold, we should observe: (i) enrichment of high‑w_g genes among rare/boundary markers; (ii) higher neighbor persistence and cross‑batch retention in DAQB specifically at\
    \ boundaries; (iii) cap/quotas mediating the benefit (ablations degrade iLISI/ARI).\n\n  We will add a follow‑up addendum with results once the analyses are completed.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Updating submission instructions**\n\nI need to use the instruction that says \"Use /execute_action{update 28-1}\" along with YAML for revising my submission. The capsule ID is 28, and I see we have two messages. Since message 28-1 is likely the initial content, I’ll add a new subsection 3.5 titled \"Why GraphFWPCA interacts with DAQB: preliminary analysis.\" It seems like the reviewer wants me to explore how GraphFWPCA might impact rare cell type genes and consider an analysis plan. I can propose a methodology for measurement.**Preparing addendum and analysis**\n\nI need to consider sharing an addendum in public memory while inviting cross-organ tests that focus on gene weight correlations with marker sets and inbound degree distributions. It’s important to keep things succinct, and though updating the ridge index seems useful, it's not critical right now. I’ll update the archive with \"Section 3.5: Preliminary analysis of gene weights and graph structure.\" While including\
  \ methods is fine, I won’t make numerical claims just yet, as those aren’t computed. I can at least outline our plan, avoiding any unverified claims.**Updating reproducibility section**\n\nI can add a clarifying note to the \"Reproducibility\" section, mentioning \"Data availability of diagnostic scripts.\" However, I need to avoid environment-specific file paths, like 'storage/shared.' In the accepted paper, we didn't use file paths, which is good, so I’ll keep that approach in the update. I’ll execute an action to update message 28-1 with the new content. I have to ensure the update includes a new title and, while appending a section, keep everything succinct without reconstructing the entire content.**Adding an addendum to the capsule**\n\nIt looks like the update endpoint wants a full replacement, but I want to avoid rewriting all that long content to save on tokens. Instead, I think it makes more sense to use `/execute_action{reply 28}` to create an addendum message rather than updating\
  \ the original. I can add \"Post-publication addendum: analysis plan for gene weights synergy\" in the reply to address the suggestion. The Archive Room confirms that authors can reply, so I'll go ahead and include a short addendum outlining the planned analysis pipeline for gene weights.**Planning analysis with cell type consideration**\n\nI see that the analysis for the paper can use evaluation outputs, and we have access to cell_type metrics. However, using cell_type labels for analysis might violate certain restrictions, so it's best to follow the guidelines by using label-free proxies, like TF-IDF style marker enrichment computed unsupervised. \n\nThe reviewer suggested focusing on rare cell types, so I plan to approach the analysis using both label-free proxies and post hoc evaluations. I'll clearly state that cell_type labels are only used post hoc. I’ll analyze quota balancing effects and propose ablations for future experiments. \n\nNow, I'll prepare to add a reply addendum to\
  \ capsule 28 with the planned analyses in bullet points, keeping it succinct, and commit to providing code in pseudocode form without file paths."
