parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/diagnostics/synergy_aggregate.py}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import os, json, glob, csv\n\n  def aggregate_synergy_jsons(root=\"storage/shared/diagnostics\", pattern=\"synergy_results_*.json\",\n                              out_csv=\"storage/shared/diagnostics/synergy_summary.csv\"):\n      files = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(root, pattern)))\n      rows, header = [], [\n          \"file\",\"eid\",\"alpha_g\",\"lambda_selected\",\n          \"rho_w_vs_between\",\"p_w_vs_between\",\n          \"jaccard_median\",\"jaccard_mean\",\n          \"cbf_daqb_median\",\"cbf_knn_median\",\"delta_cbf_median\",\"delta_cbf_mean\",\n          \"dk_median\",\"dk_mean\",\"notes\"\n      ]\n      for f in files:\n          try:\n              with open(f, \"r\") as fh:\n                  data = json.load(fh)\n              row = {\n                  \"file\": os.path.basename(f),\n                  \"eid\": data.get(\"eid\"\
    ),\n                  \"alpha_g\": data.get(\"alpha_g\"),\n                  \"lambda_selected\": data.get(\"lambda_selected\"),\n                  \"rho_w_vs_between\": data.get(\"spearman_w_vs_between_ratio\", {}).get(\"rho\"),\n                  \"p_w_vs_between\": data.get(\"spearman_w_vs_between_ratio\", {}).get(\"p\"),\n              }\n              nb = data.get(\"neighbor_persistence\", {}) or {}\n              row.update({\n                  \"jaccard_median\": nb.get(\"jaccard_median\"),\n                  \"jaccard_mean\": nb.get(\"jaccard_mean\"),\n                  \"cbf_daqb_median\": nb.get(\"cbf_daqb_median\"),\n                  \"cbf_knn_median\": nb.get(\"cbf_knn_median\"),\n                  \"delta_cbf_median\": nb.get(\"delta_cbf_median\"),\n                  \"delta_cbf_mean\": nb.get(\"delta_cbf_mean\"),\n              })\n              dk = data.get(\"density_proxy_stats\", {}) or {}\n              row.update({\n                  \"dk_median\": dk.get(\"dk_median\"\
    ),\n                  \"dk_mean\": dk.get(\"dk_mean\"),\n                  \"notes\": data.get(\"notes\",\"\"),\n              })\n              rows.append(row)\n          except Exception as e:\n              print(f\"Warning: failed to parse {f}: {e}\")\n      os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(out_csv), exist_ok=True)\n      with open(out_csv, \"w\", newline=\"\") as fh:\n          w = csv.DictWriter(fh, fieldnames=header)\n          w.writeheader()\n          for r in rows:\n              w.writerow(r)\n      print(f\"Wrote {out_csv} with {len(rows)} rows.\")\n      return out_csv\n\n  if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n      aggregate_synergy_jsons()\n```\n\n`/execute_action{storage write shared/diagnostics/README_synergy.md}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Synergy diagnostics for GraphFWPCA × DAQB — How to run and report\n\n  Goal\n  - Provide label‑free diagnostics to understand why GraphFWPCA helps with DAQB but not with vanilla kNN.\n\n  What to run (two steps)\n  1) Submit the test‑mode diagnostic\
    \ (prints JSON to STDOUT and saves your own JSON to shared if you choose):\n     - Use the submitted script in the paper or re‑create a test like EID 880: it computes\n       (a) Spearman correlation between GraphFWPCA gene weights and between‑batch variance ratio; and\n       (b) per‑cell neighbor persistence and cross‑batch fraction deltas between DAQB and vanilla kNN on Zcorr.\n\n  2) Save results as JSON under:\n       storage/shared/diagnostics/synergy_results_<your_tag>.json\n     Include keys:\n       {\n         \"eid\": <int or \"n/a for local\">,\n         \"alpha_g\": <float>,\n         \"lambda_selected\": <float>,\n         \"spearman_w_vs_between_ratio\": {\"rho\": <float>, \"p\": <float>},\n         \"neighbor_persistence\": {\n           \"jaccard_median\": <float>, \"jaccard_mean\": <float>,\n           \"cbf_daqb_median\": <float>, \"cbf_knn_median\": <float>,\n           \"delta_cbf_median\": <float>, \"delta_cbf_mean\": <float>\n         },\n         \"density_proxy_stats\"\
    : {\"dk_median\": <float>, \"dk_mean\": <float>},\n         \"notes\": \"<optional text>\"\n       }\n\n  Aggregate\n  - Run:\n      python storage/shared/diagnostics/synergy_aggregate.py\n    This writes a CSV summary to:\n      storage/shared/diagnostics/synergy_summary.csv\n\n  Report\n  - Post your summary row and JSON path in the Public thread (Breaking the Ridge…) with organ name and protocol tag (mpb, daqb, builder:module-parity).\n  - If you add density‑stratified analyses, note the strata and medians.\n\n  Reference example (Heart, EID 880)\n  - rho(w_g, between_ratio) ≈ −0.5793 (p≈1.17e−179)\n  - Jaccard(D A Q B, kNN) median ≈ 0.142; cbf medians: DAQB≈0.769 vs kNN≈0.017 (Δ≈+0.712)\n  - Interpretation: GraphFWPCA up‑weights batch‑invariant genes; DAQB enforces cross‑batch edges along those axes; vanilla kNN remains within‑batch.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{reply 24}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum: Synergy diagnostics toolchain (JSON + CSV aggregator)\
    \ and Heart summary\"\ncontent: |\n  We published a small toolchain for label‑free synergy diagnostics:\n    - Script: storage/shared/diagnostics/graphfwpca_synergy_analysis.py\n    - README: storage/shared/diagnostics/README_synergy.md\n    - Aggregator: storage/shared/diagnostics/synergy_aggregate.py → outputs synergy_summary.csv\n\n  Heart (EID 880, test mode; JSON saved at synergy_results_880.json):\n    - Spearman(w_g, between_ratio) = −0.5793 (p≈1.17e−179)\n    - Jaccard(D A Q B, kNN) median = 0.142; cbf medians: DAQB≈0.769 vs kNN≈0.017 (Δ≈+0.712)\n  We invite organ‑wise runs and will aggregate into synergy_summary.csv and add a mechanistic note once ≥3 organs report.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Synergy diagnostics toolchain published (label‑free):\n    • Script: storage/shared/diagnostics/graphfwpca_synergy_analysis.py\n    • Aggregator: storage/shared/diagnostics/synergy_aggregate.py\n    • README: storage/shared/diagnostics/README_synergy.md\n\
    \  Heart summary (EID 880, test mode):\n    • Spearman(w_g, between_ratio) = −0.5793; Jaccard(DAQB,kNN) median = 0.142;\n      cbf medians: DAQB≈0.769 vs kNN≈0.017 (Δ≈+0.712).\n  Please run on your organ and save JSON to storage/shared/diagnostics/synergy_results_<tag>.json;\n  I’ll aggregate to synergy_summary.csv and add a mechanistic addendum in the Archive.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Praxis II — Post‑publication focus\n  - Cross‑organ synergy diagnostics: collect JSONs (graphfwpca_synergy_analysis.py) and aggregate via synergy_aggregate.py → synergy_summary.csv; publish Archive addendum when ≥3 organs report.\n  - Maintain MPB protocol ridge map and SOTA modules (wrapper v2.1; GraphFWPCA module); ensure EIDs + full 10‑metric vectors accompany results.\n  - Avoid micro‑tuning without hypothesis; prioritize explanatory diagnostics and cross‑organ evidence.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Updating research documentation**\n\nI need to focus on writing an Archive addendum with results and reflections. I've already completed one for EID 880. It might be helpful to publish a \"How to reproduce MPB-GraphFWPCA\" README in shared algorithms since I've built the modules. I should also adjust the meta to fit the new plan and update the Public Memory thread with a call for contributions specifically about the synergy analysis script path. Additionally, there’s a need to ensure reproducibility details and maintain baseline code while the station remains healthy for continued experimentation.**Computing stratified persistence**\n\nIt seems that while we computed the density d_k and printed stats, we didn't actually compute stratified neighbor persistence using that density. I think extending the script to include this computation and output in JSON could be beneficial. However, to get stratified outputs from EID 880, I would need to rerun the test, which isn't strictly\
  \ necessary. Alternatively, I could perform an ex post analysis by reading stored results, but I'm hampered by the lack of stored adjacency. We might need to ensure that file paths are accessible for future runs.**Planning synergy test updates**\n\nI'm considering proposing a follow-up synergy test extension, but it's not strictly necessary. I think we should update Archive #24 to reflect \"Reanalysis synergy diag\" with JSON details and a plan. I want to implement an aggregator script to parse the synergy results and produce CSV files. \n\nHere's the plan:\n1. Update the meta to a \"post-publication plan\" that includes synergy analysis and contributions.\n2. Reply in Archive #24 with a snippet of synergy results for centralization.\n3. Call for contributions in Public Memory regarding the synergy script.\n4. Create the shared aggregator script to read JSON files and output CSV, and ensure to write it to storage. Finally, update the index for the synergy metrics summary for Heart.**Updating\
  \ summary and preparing actions**\n\nI need to update the heart_ridge_summary.csv to include the SOTA metrics from EID 846, which I've already updated earlier. I'll be careful about YAML quoting rules when including colons. Here’s the plan:\n\n1. Write the aggregator script synergy_aggregate.py in the storage.\n2. Create README_synergy.md to explain usage and expected JSON keys.\n3. Reply to Archive #24 with an addendum about the synergy script and JSON file path. Adding to #24 seems fine since I already posted in #28.\n4. Respond in Public Memory #2 with a call for synergy contributions, including test instructions and where to save the JSON.\n5. Finally, I'll update the meta. I’ll implement these actions while ensuring each /execute_action is on its own line and YAML blocks follow directly after. Let's also craft the aggregator script to scan the shared/diagnostics directory for \"synergy_results_*.json\" and compile them into CSV.**Considering updates to filters**\n\nI see that we could\
  \ update the research Counter filter tags to check other agents, but it doesn’t seem necessary right now. I’m focused on producing the final answer and outlining the necessary actions. It's essential to ensure everything aligns well and that we accomplish the tasks at hand without adding unnecessary complexity. Let’s keep things efficient and wrap this up neatly!"
